WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.335 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Hey everybody,
welcome to the podcast.

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:06.673 align:start size:88% position:13%
My guest today is the
phenomenal Alexi Pappas.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:09.776
I love this woman. She
is truly extraordinary.

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:11.745
She is an Olympics athlete.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:15.782
She competed in Rio in the
10,000 meters for Greece,

00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:19.819
but she's also a poet,
a writer, a film maker.

00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:24.691
She co-wrote, co-directed and
starred in two feature films.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:26.359
And she's also an author.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.194
Her new book is called "Bravey".

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:29.829
It's a beautiful, lovely read.

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:31.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
I highly suggest
everybody check it out.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:35.235 align:start size:84% position:16%
And this conversation
is amazing.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:36.669
So hit that subscribe button

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.505
and please enjoy
this conversation

00:00:38.505 --> 00:00:40.707
with my new favorite person.

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:43.276 line:0%
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:53.319 align:start position:0% line:0%
Well, I'm so thrilled to finally
meet you and talk to you.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.290 line:0%
And to kind of introduce this,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:58.425 line:0%
it's a little embarrassing,

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:02.395 line:0%
but like I knew who you were

00:01:02.395 --> 00:01:05.031
and I had a sense of the
things that you had done

00:01:05.031 --> 00:01:08.935
and are involved in, but
I didn't like know you.

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:13.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then when the New York
times Op-Doc came out,

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:16.376
I was really moved by that
and Lindsey's a mutual friend

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:18.244
and that kind of prompted me

00:01:18.244 --> 00:01:21.114 align:start size:84% position:16%
to do a deeper dive
into your life.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:22.615
And that happened to coincide

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
with your publicist reaching
out about doing the show,

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:27.654
which I was like immediately,
yes, please come on the show.

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:33.726
So I've spent like the
last week really enjoying

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:36.563
like all the different
things that you've done.

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:38.898
I watched both movies.
I'm well into your book.

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
The book is so good.
It's fantastic.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.304
Like you did such
a remarkable job.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:47.507
I love the movies and you're
like my new favorite person

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:49.209 align:start size:81% position:19%
so I'm all nervous
to talk to you,

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:50.944
and this is just super exciting.

00:01:50.944 --> 00:01:52.445
- Well, it's exciting for me

00:01:52.445 --> 00:01:56.649
and it's exciting for me
in more ways than one.

00:01:56.649 --> 00:02:01.387
I admire how committed
you are to what you do,

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:04.624 align:start size:88% position:13%
and how interesting
and interested you are.

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:08.995
But also my best friend
since two years old,

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:12.866 align:start position:0%
you are like the greatest thing
to her and you've kept her,

00:02:12.866 --> 00:02:14.434
she's actually a COVID nurse

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:18.438 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you have kept her
company on like mountain runs

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:22.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
and long drives to the
hospital in Denver.

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:26.246 align:start size:88% position:13%
And when she learned
that this was happening,

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:29.616
suddenly, she was like this
is the most exciting thing

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:32.118 align:start size:88% position:13%
you have ever done
Alexi, and I've done...

00:02:32.118 --> 00:02:33.353
I'm very proud of my life-

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:34.654
- Now you feel so much pressure.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.156 align:start size:72% position:28%
I'm touched.
- No! No!

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:38.224
It meant so much to
me in the same way

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:41.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
that she used to love,
like Rihanna, for example,

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:43.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I didn't love
Rihanna music growing up,

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:45.865
but Amanda, my best
friend in the world

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:47.600
loved Rihanna music so much.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.336
And you know when someone
you love is like joyful,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
you begin to like the
things that make them happy.

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.842 align:start size:94% position:6%
I felt like my excitement
was like compounded

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.878 align:start size:88% position:13%
with the joy and
excitement that she had.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:02.248
And she really is the
coolest person I know.

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
And since you're the
coolest person she knows,

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
then that makes
this very exciting.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.420
- Well, what's up Amanda?

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:08.922
Thank you for that.
I appreciate it.

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:11.524 align:start size:88% position:13%
Thank you for listening.
- That's amazing.

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:13.092
- That's super cool, thank you.

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:16.429
There's so many things I
want to talk to you about

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:19.699
but the first thing I
have to do this first,

00:03:19.699 --> 00:03:24.704
like I have to understand how
you could possibly compete

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:28.975 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the Olympics in the same
year that you co-wrote,

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:31.311 align:start size:88% position:13%
co-directed and starred
in a feature film.

00:03:31.311 --> 00:03:33.079
And I feel like it's so insane.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:37.850
And what's interesting about you

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.921
and myself and our
different backgrounds

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:46.359
is that I think there
are very few people,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.395
an average person can
kind of understand

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:50.763
what it takes to be an Olympian

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.999
or what it takes to
make a feature film,

00:03:52.999 --> 00:03:55.401
but I'm deeply connected
to both of those things.

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:58.271 align:start position:0%
I wasn't an Olympian, but I
trained with a lot of Olympians.

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:01.174
As an entertainment lawyer,
I've been deeply involved

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:03.876
in the production of many
independent feature films.

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:08.581
And I have a very tactile
sense of what's required

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:12.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
to execute at the highest
level to realize those dreams.

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:16.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
Each in their own right
are feats of impossibility,

00:04:16.389 --> 00:04:20.159
but to accomplish both in the
same year is so astounding.

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:23.196
Like I just can't even
wrap my head around it.

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:26.466 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's such an magnificent
accomplishment.

00:04:26.466 --> 00:04:27.800
- Thank you, well-

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.436 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And to do it well
like the movie,

00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:33.139 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I love "Tracktown",
I love "Olympic Dreams"

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:35.575
and for "Tracktown" to
be your first feature

00:04:35.575 --> 00:04:40.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you and Jeremy did
together, I was really amazed.

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:41.881
- I really appreciate that.

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:46.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think sometimes
our willingness

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:48.988 align:start size:84% position:16%
to stretch ourselves
and try something

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:50.423
that we've never done before

00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:54.360
is made more powerful by
the fact that we don't know

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.364
what it will really take,
but once we're committed

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:02.168 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think people like us will
we'll do what it takes.

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:05.805
And I was noticing,
I know this isn't,

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know if I
can say this on here,

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:08.941
but this is a new studio, right?

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:12.812 align:start position:0%
This is a new setup and you
have a shower in your bathroom.

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:14.781
- I know that we just finished

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.716
construction on that yesterday.

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:19.385
- And is it so that you
might train and shower

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:20.987
and then do some
of your work here?

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:22.221
- 100%.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:26.059
- So that the idea that
you like have created,

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:31.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
you've created a life around
being able to transition

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:35.368
from one thing that you're
doing in a day to the other.

00:05:35.368 --> 00:05:38.404
And I think that's practical

00:05:38.404 --> 00:05:42.575
but there's also
something mental

00:05:42.575 --> 00:05:46.746
about shifting gears and
being able to hyper-focus

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:49.315
with any one of your
tasks in any given day

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.385
and then being able
to consciously shift
to the next one.

00:05:52.385 --> 00:05:55.688
And I think some of
the the misconception

00:05:55.688 --> 00:05:57.623
that people might have
when they look the things

00:05:57.623 --> 00:06:00.426 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I've done on
paper in any given year

00:06:00.426 --> 00:06:02.895
is that I'm doing them
all at the same time.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:07.500 align:start position:0%
And the truth is that I'm
trying to do one of those things

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.569
really well in any given moment

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:13.573 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then excellently
transition to the next task.

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:15.341
And I think that in that year,

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:19.278
it kept me healthy as an
athlete to end practice

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:22.682
at a certain time and to not
have practice last all day,

00:06:22.682 --> 00:06:26.486
because as you and I know,
you can run for several hours

00:06:26.486 --> 00:06:29.021
but you can't run all day.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:31.257
But some people let that happen

00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:33.259 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether it's mentally
or physically.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:36.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so in some ways
ending practice

00:06:36.429 --> 00:06:40.032
and just worrying about how
are we gonna fund this movie

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:45.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
was a blessing to my
Olympic trajectory as well.

00:06:46.439 --> 00:06:50.109 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Because you couldn't just
sit and stew and obsess

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:53.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
about things that you
don't have control over.

00:06:53.112 --> 00:06:56.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like it allowed you to
shift gears and stay fresh

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:59.786
with both things because
you had other pursuits

00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:02.622 align:start size:84% position:16%
outside of just
that singular focus?

00:07:02.622 --> 00:07:05.191
- Yeah, and it even
simplified running

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:08.761
because if I thought
about how challenging

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:10.930
it was going to be
to do this workout,

00:07:12.231 --> 00:07:14.367 align:start position:0%
I could also have the
perspective of how simple it is

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.202
that I just need to
keep putting one foot

00:07:16.202 --> 00:07:20.506
in front of the other when
it felt like so complicated

00:07:20.506 --> 00:07:22.208 align:start size:84% position:16%
to try to put together
a feature film.

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:25.645
So I think the feature
film, people dynamics,

00:07:25.645 --> 00:07:29.282
you know what it takes
to put a movie together

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:32.585
is so different than what it
takes to just keep running

00:07:32.585 --> 00:07:34.086
when you're in a mile repeat.

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:39.258
And likewise, the pain
of doing that mile repeat

00:07:39.258 --> 00:07:43.563
or doing multiple was very
painful compared to the ease

00:07:43.563 --> 00:07:45.832
of sitting on a couch
and making phone calls

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:48.234 align:start size:84% position:16%
or writing a script,
which is hard,

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:51.537
but it's not like my body
hurts while I'm doing it.

00:07:51.537 --> 00:07:55.308
So I think they were Tweedledee

00:07:55.308 --> 00:07:58.010
and Tweedledum to each
other, just playful.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:00.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
- (indistinct) writer
so overwhelming.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.415 align:start size:97% position:3%
There must have been
moments where you just felt

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:05.284
like I'm climbing a mountain

00:08:05.284 --> 00:08:07.987
that is double the
size of Everest.

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:11.724
- Yeah, it felt hard.

00:08:11.724 --> 00:08:13.426
I have a great partner too.

00:08:13.426 --> 00:08:15.928
You know, like I do
have Jeremy with me

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:20.399 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was never pulling
all nighters while my goal,

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:23.035
my number one goal was
to go to the Olympics.

00:08:23.035 --> 00:08:25.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think part of it, and
perhaps you can relate,

00:08:25.571 --> 00:08:29.642 align:start size:97% position:3%
is knowing what is the
priority in any given moment

00:08:29.642 --> 00:08:31.878 align:start size:88% position:13%
and always knowing
what my North star was,

00:08:31.878 --> 00:08:34.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then everything
needs to fall under that.

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:38.584
So like I am gonna sleep

00:08:38.584 --> 00:08:42.121
at least eight hours a night
and that's non-negotiable,

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:44.757
even if we need to
stay up late editing,

00:08:44.757 --> 00:08:46.559 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's a time when
I need to stop.

00:08:47.827 --> 00:08:52.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
So maybe those
boundaries are more clear

00:08:52.398 --> 00:08:56.569 align:start position:0%
because I knew what the priority
one was and priority two,

00:08:56.569 --> 00:08:57.737
but it was stressful.

00:08:59.505 --> 00:09:03.276
But it was stressful
in like a thrilling way

00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:07.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I was playing
a game, running,

00:09:07.246 --> 00:09:08.714
and I was making a movie.

00:09:08.714 --> 00:09:11.117
- And also no one's ever done
anything like that before,

00:09:11.117 --> 00:09:13.052
which is kind of exhilarating

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:15.388
and intimidating
I would suspect.

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:18.724 align:start position:0%
And I'm sure you get the
balanced question all the time.

00:09:18.724 --> 00:09:20.293
Like, how do you
balance your life?

00:09:20.293 --> 00:09:24.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I hate that I don't look
at life in that context.

00:09:24.196 --> 00:09:25.965
It just seems confusing to me.

00:09:25.965 --> 00:09:29.135
Like, it seems my sense is
that it's more with you.

00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:31.170
It's more about synergy.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.272
Like, are these things in
synergy with each other?

00:09:33.272 --> 00:09:35.107
Do they feed off of each other?

00:09:35.107 --> 00:09:38.177 align:start position:0%
Do each one of these things
make me better at the other one?

00:09:38.177 --> 00:09:40.046 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Do you feel like
you're thriving, right?

00:09:40.046 --> 00:09:42.315
It's like more of a feeling
and how do you answer it

00:09:42.315 --> 00:09:44.483 align:start size:81% position:19%
when people ask
you that question?

00:09:44.483 --> 00:09:47.019
- I mean, I used to feel,

00:09:47.019 --> 00:09:48.588
I used to feel guilt and shame

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:51.691
because my life is
not really balanced

00:09:51.691 --> 00:09:53.225
in that traditional sense.

00:09:53.225 --> 00:09:57.530
And I would strive to make
it fit into those buckets.

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.566
And at some point I just
let go of that whole thing

00:10:00.566 --> 00:10:03.069 align:start size:84% position:16%
and, you know,
look, in the macro,

00:10:03.069 --> 00:10:05.638
and I'm sure this is
the case with yourself,

00:10:05.638 --> 00:10:07.940
in the macro, your life
is very much imbalanced

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:10.576 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you're shifting gears
between intense focus

00:10:10.576 --> 00:10:13.479
on different things that
give your life meaning

00:10:13.479 --> 00:10:16.782
and make you excited when
you wake up in the morning.

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:20.820 align:start size:94% position:6%
but to the average person,
on a day-to-day basis,

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:23.356
it would appear that you're
very much out of balance.

00:10:23.356 --> 00:10:25.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, okay, now I
feel like I understand

00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:26.993
what you're getting at

00:10:26.993 --> 00:10:28.961 align:start size:88% position:13%
because there were
people during that time

00:10:28.961 --> 00:10:32.465
who were like I think you and
Jeremy should take a break

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:34.000
and go camping this weekend.

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:35.668 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like, I think you
guys need a night off

00:10:35.668 --> 00:10:40.339
or like I think you need, I
think you should, I think...

00:10:40.339 --> 00:10:43.175
And that was like...

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:46.912
You know, I was at an
age where it was like,

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:48.347
I felt a little weird
about it, I was like,

00:10:48.347 --> 00:10:49.715
I know I'm living in Oregon

00:10:49.715 --> 00:10:51.117 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there's so
much great camping,

00:10:51.117 --> 00:10:53.352
but all I want to do is
work on these projects.

00:10:53.352 --> 00:10:56.822
Like, I was so happy

00:10:56.822 --> 00:11:00.526
being so overflowing
with what I was doing.

00:11:00.526 --> 00:11:03.863 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I feel like what
you're saying is similarly,

00:11:05.464 --> 00:11:09.502 align:start size:88% position:13%
you live to work but
your work is your choice

00:11:09.502 --> 00:11:11.037
and your work is your passion.

00:11:11.037 --> 00:11:15.808
And so it's your pleasure to
have this bubbling cauldron

00:11:17.276 --> 00:11:20.479
that someone might look at
and not think is healthy.

00:11:20.479 --> 00:11:24.083
- Yeah, I mean, there's that,
but it's also the intensity

00:11:24.083 --> 00:11:26.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you bring to whatever
it is you're doing.

00:11:26.185 --> 00:11:27.887 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like if it's an
ultra endurance race

00:11:27.887 --> 00:11:29.121
or you training for the Olympics

00:11:29.121 --> 00:11:31.090 align:start size:84% position:16%
or trying to execute
on a feature film

00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:34.026
or me doing what I do here,

00:11:34.026 --> 00:11:37.963 align:start size:84% position:16%
I get joy out of
being totally immersed

00:11:37.963 --> 00:11:39.865
in whatever it is
that I'm doing.

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:44.103
And I'm not effective unless
I can say no to other things

00:11:44.103 --> 00:11:47.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
and pop things out so
that I can give my best.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.276 align:start size:88% position:13%
But that means that
other things in my life

00:11:50.276 --> 00:11:51.510
that are important to me

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.946 align:start size:88% position:13%
aren't getting adequate
amount of attention

00:11:53.946 --> 00:11:55.247
on that particular day.

00:11:55.247 --> 00:11:58.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
So the gear shifting
is important,

00:11:58.184 --> 00:12:00.619
such that everything in
my life that I care about

00:12:00.619 --> 00:12:02.254
is attended to properly.

00:12:02.254 --> 00:12:04.724 align:start size:84% position:16%
It just doesn't happen
on an hour-to-hour

00:12:04.724 --> 00:12:06.992
or day-to-day basis necessarily.

00:12:06.992 --> 00:12:11.997
- Yeah. Yep.

00:12:11.997 --> 00:12:14.700
- Yeah, right, I
know you feel it,

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
your volume on that, right?
- Yeah.

00:12:16.669 --> 00:12:19.505
- I feel like when I
watched "Tracktown",

00:12:19.505 --> 00:12:23.275
your erstwhile boyfriend in
the movie was sort of a proxy

00:12:23.275 --> 00:12:26.278 align:start size:97% position:3%
for the people who are saying,
take a break or go camping.

00:12:26.278 --> 00:12:30.483
And I found that movie, like
it's so heartfelt and authentic

00:12:32.118 --> 00:12:34.019 align:start size:88% position:13%
and beautifully realized
in so many ways,

00:12:34.019 --> 00:12:35.921 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it was also very
stressful for me

00:12:35.921 --> 00:12:37.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
to watch that movie-
- Really?

00:12:37.456 --> 00:12:42.461
- Because my sense is that
you were trying to craft

00:12:43.896 --> 00:12:46.499
an alternate universe in
which you weren't somebody

00:12:46.499 --> 00:12:48.868
who has many different interests

00:12:48.868 --> 00:12:52.471 align:start position:0%
and allocates your energy
across a variety of disciplines,

00:12:52.471 --> 00:12:55.141
but to stand in the
shoes of that person

00:12:55.141 --> 00:12:58.978
who is all in a 100%
on just that one thing,

00:12:58.978 --> 00:13:01.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're tiptoeing
outside of that, you know,

00:13:01.647 --> 00:13:05.417
on the precipice of trying
to qualify for the Olympics.

00:13:05.417 --> 00:13:07.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I was like,
just wait two days,

00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:12.358 align:start position:0%
like can't you just go back
into your altitude tent and rest

00:13:12.358 --> 00:13:14.894
until like Thursday or
whatever day it was,

00:13:14.894 --> 00:13:16.462
like, your race is tomorrow.

00:13:16.462 --> 00:13:19.265
- Yes, well, that's funny
because so many people

00:13:19.265 --> 00:13:22.268
who don't know me think that
"Tracktown" is a documentary.

00:13:22.268 --> 00:13:25.704 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I was like, well, first
of all, that would mean

00:13:25.704 --> 00:13:27.106
a number of different things

00:13:28.240 --> 00:13:29.809 align:start size:81% position:19%
if it was actually
a documentary,

00:13:29.809 --> 00:13:33.946
like that we caught me losing
my virginity on camera somehow

00:13:33.946 --> 00:13:35.414 align:start size:84% position:16%
and things like that.
(both laughing)

00:13:35.414 --> 00:13:36.615 align:start size:72% position:28%
Which is not-
- No,

00:13:36.615 --> 00:13:38.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
people didn't actually
think that, right?

00:13:38.384 --> 00:13:39.618 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No, we've had that.
- Really?

00:13:39.618 --> 00:13:44.623 align:start size:88% position:13%
- We've also had people
who did not believe

00:13:45.724 --> 00:13:48.527 align:start size:84% position:16%
that Plum as a
character was real.

00:13:48.527 --> 00:13:50.729 align:start size:97% position:3%
And these are people who are
outside the running world

00:13:50.729 --> 00:13:53.566 align:start size:88% position:13%
like really prestigious,
like film advisers.

00:13:54.934 --> 00:13:56.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were in a
Sundance lab with it

00:13:56.669 --> 00:13:59.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
and some people
just didn't believe

00:13:59.405 --> 00:14:01.140
that someone like
that could exist.

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:03.609
Someone who had never
kissed a boy at age 21

00:14:03.609 --> 00:14:06.378
and that's very real.

00:14:06.378 --> 00:14:07.980
- It's incredibly real.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.884
- To you, but to the
world, she isn't known.

00:14:13.485 --> 00:14:15.054
And that was part of the reason

00:14:15.054 --> 00:14:17.289
why I wanted to
put her on camera

00:14:18.557 --> 00:14:22.528
and to say that this
person does exist.

00:14:22.528 --> 00:14:25.264
She's a patchwork quilt
of people that I know

00:14:25.264 --> 00:14:28.701 align:start size:94% position:6%
and experiences I've
had, but she could exist.

00:14:28.701 --> 00:14:31.103
And those are the
types of characters

00:14:31.103 --> 00:14:33.873
that I'm interested in
putting in my movies,

00:14:33.873 --> 00:14:36.842
is like the characters
that I know are real,

00:14:36.842 --> 00:14:38.811
the world doesn't know them yet,

00:14:38.811 --> 00:14:41.080
and these scenarios could exist.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:44.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I know that girl, I've
been around a lot of people

00:14:44.683 --> 00:14:46.418
very much like that.

00:14:46.418 --> 00:14:50.623 align:start size:88% position:13%
Have you read David
Epstein's book "Range"?

00:14:50.623 --> 00:14:52.024
- Yes.

00:14:52.024 --> 00:14:54.927 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, so I've been thinking
a lot about that book

00:14:54.927 --> 00:14:56.662
in the context of your story

00:14:56.662 --> 00:15:00.699
because for people who don't
know his thesis is essentially

00:15:00.699 --> 00:15:05.704
that people who are
super high performers

00:15:06.872 --> 00:15:09.775 align:start size:84% position:16%
by and large defy
conventional wisdom.

00:15:09.775 --> 00:15:12.811
They're not the tiger
woods or the plums

00:15:12.811 --> 00:15:17.349
who go all in at a very early
age on one singular pursuit,

00:15:17.349 --> 00:15:19.285 align:start size:84% position:16%
but rather there are
people who dabble

00:15:19.285 --> 00:15:22.121
in lots of different things
before they finally end up

00:15:22.121 --> 00:15:25.124
like kind of deciding
what their lane is.

00:15:25.124 --> 00:15:29.094
And you're somebody who
is a beautiful case study

00:15:29.094 --> 00:15:33.599
of that thesis and Plum
is of the 10,000 hour,

00:15:33.599 --> 00:15:35.801 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, Malcolm
Gladwell kind of rule.

00:15:35.801 --> 00:15:39.204
And I say conventional wisdom
because there is this idea,

00:15:39.204 --> 00:15:42.074
like if you wanna excel
at that highest level

00:15:42.074 --> 00:15:46.512
you have to be that
plum-type individual.

00:15:46.512 --> 00:15:50.015 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in truth more often
than not, it's not the case.

00:15:50.015 --> 00:15:54.053
And the people that are like
the plums end up burning out.

00:15:54.053 --> 00:15:56.622
Like you see this individual

00:15:56.622 --> 00:16:00.326 align:start size:97% position:3%
who's not fully emotionally
realized, right?

00:16:00.326 --> 00:16:03.896
And her strength, which is her
focus, is also her weakness.

00:16:03.896 --> 00:16:05.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's her blind spot
that's holding her back

00:16:05.898 --> 00:16:08.100
from performing at
the highest level,

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.537
because she still has so
much to learn and grow

00:16:11.537 --> 00:16:15.107
because she's so siloed
in her life experience.

00:16:15.107 --> 00:16:18.877
- Yeah, it, it reminds
me of this advice we got

00:16:18.877 --> 00:16:21.814
from our college coach
when I was at Dartmouth

00:16:21.814 --> 00:16:23.949
and we were going to a
national championships.

00:16:23.949 --> 00:16:25.517
It was like the first
one I'd ever been to.

00:16:25.517 --> 00:16:28.721
I was like a junior. It
was for a relay team.

00:16:28.721 --> 00:16:30.789
And he turns to us,
this is Mark Coogan,

00:16:30.789 --> 00:16:32.758
he was also an Olympian.

00:16:32.758 --> 00:16:36.128
And he was like, this isn't
an Ivy League Reference,

00:16:36.128 --> 00:16:39.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
but he was like, just
remember that you're smart

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:43.202
and you can think and
that's a strength too.

00:16:43.202 --> 00:16:44.670
And I think he felt bad

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:47.373
because some of us were writing
our final papers on the bus,

00:16:47.373 --> 00:16:49.008
like we were balancing a lot.

00:16:50.409 --> 00:16:53.445 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think what he really
meant was that we had

00:16:53.445 --> 00:16:56.982
some perspective to
bring into that race,

00:16:56.982 --> 00:17:00.319
whatever that meant, and
to use that as a strength.

00:17:00.319 --> 00:17:02.588
And I have felt that in races

00:17:02.588 --> 00:17:07.426
where just being able
to think critically

00:17:07.426 --> 00:17:12.331
or zoom out a little or have
some perspective does help.

00:17:12.331 --> 00:17:14.500
And I think that's
what you're saying

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:17.936
about like a character like
Plum, where that hyper-focus,

00:17:19.071 --> 00:17:24.076 align:start size:88% position:13%
that hyper-focus is
really noble and useful.

00:17:25.411 --> 00:17:29.081 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the range, the bringing
in people dynamics,

00:17:30.482 --> 00:17:32.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
understanding the world
in a different way

00:17:32.818 --> 00:17:34.453
can be a strength too.

00:17:36.021 --> 00:17:40.459 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, it's a
balancing act, right?

00:17:40.459 --> 00:17:44.563
Because you do have to
be all in in some way,

00:17:44.563 --> 00:17:48.700 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you have to provide that
room to be a human being.

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:50.302
- Especially in a
sport like running

00:17:50.302 --> 00:17:51.804
which has to grow with you.

00:17:51.804 --> 00:17:54.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like probably all
sports are like that,

00:17:54.406 --> 00:17:57.142
but running as much as any

00:17:57.142 --> 00:18:00.079
has got to evolve
as you're evolving.

00:18:00.079 --> 00:18:03.816 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think it's the people
who try to keep it what it was

00:18:03.816 --> 00:18:07.553 align:start size:97% position:3%
or what they think it
should be that become bitter

00:18:08.954 --> 00:18:11.957
which is just not
the word we want.

00:18:11.957 --> 00:18:14.960 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right, but by cutting
this unique path

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:16.462
that you've carved for yourself,

00:18:16.462 --> 00:18:19.031
I would imagine along the way,
there's been a lot of people

00:18:19.031 --> 00:18:24.036
who are sort of naysayers
or trying to dissuade you

00:18:25.204 --> 00:18:26.572
or saying you're
taking on too much

00:18:26.572 --> 00:18:28.874 align:start size:84% position:16%
or why don't you
just do it this way?

00:18:28.874 --> 00:18:32.845
- For sure, I mean,
from the very minute

00:18:32.845 --> 00:18:34.813
that I graduated college,

00:18:38.383 --> 00:18:42.521 align:start position:0%
there were certain opportunities
that would have either,

00:18:43.956 --> 00:18:46.725
there were opportunities that
would have actually blended

00:18:46.725 --> 00:18:50.662
my storytelling and my
running goals into one

00:18:50.662 --> 00:18:52.698
much earlier than I was ready.

00:18:52.698 --> 00:18:54.900
So we're getting a
little esoteric here,

00:18:56.301 --> 00:18:59.238 align:start size:97% position:3%
but basically, when I first
graduated from college,

00:18:59.238 --> 00:19:03.342
I wanted to be a filmmaker,
an actress, and an Olympian.

00:19:03.342 --> 00:19:08.347 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I knew that until I
had created those platforms

00:19:09.448 --> 00:19:11.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
and had those
stand on their own,

00:19:11.850 --> 00:19:14.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
they wouldn't be
able to come together

00:19:14.319 --> 00:19:17.523 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the way that I feel
that they have now.

00:19:19.124 --> 00:19:21.627
And so I made certain choices

00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:23.996
where I think I was
being encouraged

00:19:23.996 --> 00:19:27.065
to be the filmmaker
runner right away.

00:19:27.065 --> 00:19:30.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I needed to make choices
for my running career

00:19:30.669 --> 00:19:33.071
that would set me up
to be a great athlete

00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:35.007
independent of the film career.

00:19:35.007 --> 00:19:38.010
And I needed to make
choices as a creative

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:39.545
that would make a great movie

00:19:39.545 --> 00:19:42.181
regardless of if I was
a great athlete or not.

00:19:42.181 --> 00:19:44.917
- Right, I mean, this
is something that you

00:19:44.917 --> 00:19:47.786
and Jeremy talked about with
Dave Chang on his podcast,

00:19:47.786 --> 00:19:49.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
which I really enjoyed
that conversation,

00:19:49.788 --> 00:19:54.793
but the idea being that
your filmmaking success

00:19:55.961 --> 00:19:58.430
cannot be contingent
upon name recognition

00:19:58.430 --> 00:20:00.499
around you as an athlete

00:20:00.499 --> 00:20:03.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
and your performances have
to stand on their own.

00:20:03.669 --> 00:20:06.905
And you've done that,
like you are, you know,

00:20:06.905 --> 00:20:08.207
you are the athlete that you are

00:20:08.207 --> 00:20:10.142 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it has nothing to
do with your filmmaking.

00:20:10.142 --> 00:20:12.311
And you don't have
to know who you are

00:20:12.311 --> 00:20:15.280 align:start size:84% position:16%
to watch these movies
and enjoy them.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:17.883
It has nothing to do
with your, you know,

00:20:17.883 --> 00:20:20.519
how fast you can
run 10,000 meters

00:20:20.519 --> 00:20:22.554
or a marathon or anything else.

00:20:22.554 --> 00:20:27.526
- Thank you, and maybe
that speaks to range.

00:20:27.526 --> 00:20:29.328 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think those people
who are seeking

00:20:30.495 --> 00:20:33.699
the range of experiences,

00:20:35.901 --> 00:20:39.037
I would hope that all those
people are still seeking

00:20:39.037 --> 00:20:41.773
to seek the best of
those worlds, right?

00:20:41.773 --> 00:20:46.178
Like I don't know, like
I don't love cupcakes.

00:20:46.178 --> 00:20:48.080
I like them, but
if I'm gonna go,

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:50.349
I wanna find like
the best cupcakes.

00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:53.485 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, I wanna like go
to the peaks of these worlds

00:20:53.485 --> 00:20:56.455
because there's something
to be learned from people

00:20:56.455 --> 00:21:00.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
and communities that are
chasing the best of any world.

00:21:00.792 --> 00:21:04.696
And that's why I listened
to David Chang's podcasts

00:21:04.696 --> 00:21:08.867 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I like how invested
he is in his craft.

00:21:08.867 --> 00:21:10.569
And I know I can learn from him

00:21:10.569 --> 00:21:12.404
even though I'm not
in the food industry.

00:21:12.404 --> 00:21:14.773 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, it's the mindset,
it's the approach.

00:21:14.773 --> 00:21:17.042
It's the respect for the craft,

00:21:17.042 --> 00:21:19.011 align:start size:84% position:16%
the attention to
detail, the quality,

00:21:19.011 --> 00:21:20.846 align:start size:94% position:6%
all of those things
whether you're an athlete,

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:25.017
a filmmaker, or a writer or
a chef, they're all related,

00:21:25.017 --> 00:21:28.487
they're all relevant
to pursuing something

00:21:28.487 --> 00:21:30.255
and trying to be
excellent at it.

00:21:30.255 --> 00:21:31.490
- And I think that for me,

00:21:31.490 --> 00:21:35.694
that comes from like a
really melancholic place

00:21:35.694 --> 00:21:36.895
if I really think about it

00:21:36.895 --> 00:21:38.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I've never thought
about it this way,

00:21:38.664 --> 00:21:41.133
but I think that
I always wanted...

00:21:41.133 --> 00:21:44.770
Like I did not have a
mom, I lost her young.

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:46.672
And I think I always wanted that

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:48.674 align:start size:81% position:19%
or was curious
what that could be

00:21:48.674 --> 00:21:52.544 align:start position:0%
with full awareness that I
could never have it really truly

00:21:52.544 --> 00:21:54.112
how I would want it,

00:21:54.112 --> 00:21:57.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I did know that I
could have everything else.

00:21:57.416 --> 00:22:01.053
And I talk about this in the
book as the mentor buffet,

00:22:01.053 --> 00:22:05.324
it's what I call it, but
I think what I meant was,

00:22:05.324 --> 00:22:10.329
well, I'm just gonna go chase
the best of every other world

00:22:10.329 --> 00:22:13.398
and try to patchwork it,
quilt it into something

00:22:13.398 --> 00:22:16.501
that can fill that need

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:19.838 align:start size:88% position:13%
for that keystone mentor
as best that I can.

00:22:19.838 --> 00:22:21.373
So, I think it comes from that.

00:22:21.373 --> 00:22:24.209
Like, I truly think it was
like a survival thing of,

00:22:25.210 --> 00:22:27.212
I can't have the very best

00:22:27.212 --> 00:22:30.449
of the very singular
thing I would like,

00:22:30.449 --> 00:22:35.454
but I'm gonna chase and
shamelessly draw from

00:22:36.855 --> 00:22:40.492 align:start position:0%
and dip myself into every other
world if that interests me.

00:22:40.492 --> 00:22:42.527
- Yeah, yeah, yeah,
I mean, in the book,

00:22:42.527 --> 00:22:45.130
you did a beautiful job
of kind of underscoring

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:49.167
or illustrating this
void in your life.

00:22:49.167 --> 00:22:51.436
And I wanna go back and
hear more about this

00:22:51.436 --> 00:22:52.637
of not having a mom

00:22:54.473 --> 00:22:56.541
and like your hypervigilance,

00:22:56.541 --> 00:22:58.443
whenever you were in a situation

00:22:58.443 --> 00:23:00.178 align:start size:81% position:19%
in which daughters
were with moms,

00:23:00.178 --> 00:23:02.647
whether it was girl scouts
or the feeding of the ducks

00:23:02.647 --> 00:23:06.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
and all of these experiences
where you looked at it

00:23:06.885 --> 00:23:09.654
almost as something exotic

00:23:09.654 --> 00:23:13.859 align:start position:0%
that you couldn't quite
emotionally connect with, right?

00:23:13.859 --> 00:23:16.728
And that tracks like all
the way through your life

00:23:16.728 --> 00:23:18.964
even to the point where
you're at the Olympics

00:23:18.964 --> 00:23:21.299
and you go to get
like a make-over

00:23:21.299 --> 00:23:23.502 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that experience
of having that person,

00:23:25.237 --> 00:23:27.606
have you comb your
hair and all of that,

00:23:27.606 --> 00:23:31.009
like it was so foreign to you
because you didn't grow up

00:23:31.009 --> 00:23:33.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a mom who taught
you these things.

00:23:33.645 --> 00:23:37.682
- Yes, and I think it
takes a certain muscle

00:23:37.682 --> 00:23:42.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
to feel like the
world is a reservoir

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:44.923
that gives rather than takes,

00:23:44.923 --> 00:23:49.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that we all have a
choice to make about that.

00:23:49.528 --> 00:23:51.930 align:start size:84% position:16%
Whether we see the
world that way or not,

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.099 align:start size:97% position:3%
but if we can see it as
something where we're allowed

00:23:54.099 --> 00:23:58.537
to get things and soak it in,

00:23:58.537 --> 00:24:00.672
then we can get it
anywhere we look.

00:24:02.107 --> 00:24:04.743 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that wears off for
a lot of people eventually,

00:24:04.743 --> 00:24:07.512 align:start size:84% position:16%
like you feel like I'm
formed as a person.

00:24:07.512 --> 00:24:10.849 align:start size:84% position:16%
I now will create
content and put it out

00:24:10.849 --> 00:24:12.584
rather than take it in anymore,

00:24:12.584 --> 00:24:16.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's fine, we don't need
to take in from everywhere.

00:24:16.154 --> 00:24:21.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
But the world is remarkable
and there is a lot

00:24:21.893 --> 00:24:23.161
that we can get from it

00:24:23.161 --> 00:24:27.933
even in adulthood if
we see it that way.

00:24:28.667 --> 00:24:29.968
- Yeah, that's so true.

00:24:29.968 --> 00:24:32.137
Well, let's take it back.

00:24:32.137 --> 00:24:34.072
You're growing up in Berkeley.

00:24:35.240 --> 00:24:37.075 align:start size:84% position:16%
Let's go all the way
to the beginning.

00:24:37.075 --> 00:24:38.310
- [Alexi] Okay.

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:43.114
- So, walk me through
you as a young girl,

00:24:43.715 --> 00:24:44.483
four years old.

00:24:45.817 --> 00:24:47.719
- Well, I was born in Berkeley

00:24:47.719 --> 00:24:51.022 align:start size:84% position:16%
and grew up on an
Island called Alameda.

00:24:51.022 --> 00:24:54.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was more like a
peninsula Bay Farm Island.

00:24:54.359 --> 00:24:56.428
- I've never heard Alameda
referred to as an Island,

00:24:56.428 --> 00:24:57.929
but yes, I guess it is.

00:24:57.929 --> 00:24:59.831
- I think it's like
the Island city,

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:02.400
but then Bay Farm
was at peninsula

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:05.670 align:start position:0%
which is essentially the Oakland
Airport it's right there.

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:09.808
And that was a safe Island
to be, like you could walk

00:25:09.808 --> 00:25:12.577
with your friends to the
ice cream shop as we did,

00:25:12.577 --> 00:25:14.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
and your parents
would generally think

00:25:14.646 --> 00:25:16.248
you were going to be okay.

00:25:17.916 --> 00:25:21.987
But yeah, so when I was young,

00:25:21.987 --> 00:25:24.322
the first like four or
five years of my life,

00:25:24.322 --> 00:25:28.860 align:start size:94% position:6%
it coincided with my
mom's like really intense,

00:25:30.595 --> 00:25:31.963
you know, downward spiral.

00:25:31.963 --> 00:25:34.799
She was manic bipolar

00:25:34.799 --> 00:25:39.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think my birth
really catalyzed

00:25:40.672 --> 00:25:43.808
a really tough turn for her.

00:25:43.808 --> 00:25:46.444 align:start position:0%
She was addicted to
painkillers. She had scoliosis.

00:25:46.444 --> 00:25:48.213
So this was in the 90s,

00:25:48.213 --> 00:25:50.782
so there was a lot
of over prescription,

00:25:50.782 --> 00:25:52.717
I think, of painkillers.

00:25:54.352 --> 00:25:56.555
And so she was addicted to
them and she was very smart

00:25:56.555 --> 00:26:00.158
and able to like obtain
them in any way possible,

00:26:00.158 --> 00:26:03.461
which now my dad and I were
able to actually have a laugh

00:26:03.461 --> 00:26:05.397
about when I read him the book,

00:26:05.397 --> 00:26:09.034 align:start position:0%
I read it to him out loud,
which was a really, really fun,

00:26:09.034 --> 00:26:12.037 align:start position:0%
it was a really meaningful
weekend, but he opened up a lot

00:26:12.037 --> 00:26:17.042 align:start size:88% position:13%
and she was very
scrappy about her drugs

00:26:17.042 --> 00:26:18.643
and she got them.

00:26:19.811 --> 00:26:23.949 align:start size:88% position:13%
And she was depressed
and she was getting help

00:26:23.949 --> 00:26:27.886 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I now understand would
not have helped anybody

00:26:27.886 --> 00:26:29.387
in her position.

00:26:29.387 --> 00:26:31.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that I only understood
in the last year or two

00:26:31.389 --> 00:26:35.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
discovering paperwork
from her care in Berkeley.

00:26:35.327 --> 00:26:36.695 align:start size:81% position:19%
But she was in and
out of our life

00:26:36.695 --> 00:26:40.298 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause she was in
institutions or drug rehab.

00:26:40.298 --> 00:26:45.303
And so I never had
that relationship

00:26:47.105 --> 00:26:49.941 align:start size:97% position:3%
with her where I don't
remember really touching her.

00:26:49.941 --> 00:26:52.377
And that has always been
a strange thing for me,

00:26:53.845 --> 00:26:56.948
an obsession maybe of like
just what it would be like

00:26:56.948 --> 00:26:59.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
to be embraced like that-
- Right.

00:26:59.784 --> 00:27:04.122
Then the one recollection that
you have of physical contact

00:27:04.122 --> 00:27:08.360 align:start size:88% position:13%
with her was her giving
you her cigarette

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:10.328 align:start size:84% position:16%
when you're like this
tiny little girl.

00:27:10.328 --> 00:27:12.330
- Oh, I'll never forget that.

00:27:12.330 --> 00:27:14.065
I mean, and it's so...

00:27:14.065 --> 00:27:16.635
When I wrote that
too, I was afraid

00:27:16.635 --> 00:27:19.704
that people might read it and
think she was a bad person

00:27:19.704 --> 00:27:21.206
or like a bad mom.

00:27:21.206 --> 00:27:23.908
And I think that's why a book
is a really important medium

00:27:23.908 --> 00:27:26.645 align:start size:94% position:6%
as compared to like a
one off social media post

00:27:26.645 --> 00:27:30.081 align:start size:94% position:6%
or other forms of sharing,
which is fragmented.

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:33.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I wanted to
write that in a way

00:27:33.685 --> 00:27:36.488 align:start size:88% position:13%
where perhaps you didn't
understand her fully,

00:27:36.488 --> 00:27:38.790
'cause at that age
I didn't either.

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:40.959
And that's why the book...

00:27:40.959 --> 00:27:43.261
You know, I tried to
emotionally progressive

00:27:43.261 --> 00:27:46.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
rather than just linearly.
- Right.

00:27:46.498 --> 00:27:50.902 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So she was smoking a
cigarette and she did often,

00:27:50.902 --> 00:27:52.604
and it was just me and her.

00:27:54.973 --> 00:27:57.142
I rarely remember her
giving me attention

00:27:57.142 --> 00:28:02.080
but she did in this moment and
she offered me her cigarette

00:28:03.381 --> 00:28:06.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I smoked it and I was-
(both laughing)

00:28:06.618 --> 00:28:07.819
- It's so amazing.

00:28:07.819 --> 00:28:09.554
- Well, it so, you know,

00:28:09.554 --> 00:28:11.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
the world is-
- It's heartbreaking.

00:28:11.289 --> 00:28:13.525 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, yeah, but
what's heartbreaking is,

00:28:13.525 --> 00:28:16.261
or what's cool about it and
what's been tough about it

00:28:16.261 --> 00:28:18.329
is that I realized much later

00:28:18.329 --> 00:28:20.865
that all I've looked
for ever since then

00:28:20.865 --> 00:28:24.135 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that feeling of
somebody I admire so much

00:28:24.135 --> 00:28:26.237
sharing something with me.

00:28:26.237 --> 00:28:29.941
And it like means so much
'cause it makes me feel

00:28:31.142 --> 00:28:35.080
like that again, you know?

00:28:35.080 --> 00:28:38.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, her condition
was very acute.

00:28:38.850 --> 00:28:41.152
She's in and out of
these institutions

00:28:41.152 --> 00:28:42.887
and when she would come home

00:28:42.887 --> 00:28:45.857
seemingly in a state
of relative fitness

00:28:45.857 --> 00:28:48.293
so that she could
be with her family,

00:28:48.293 --> 00:28:49.794
everybody's on pins and needles,

00:28:49.794 --> 00:28:52.831
all the attention
is on her 24/7.

00:28:52.831 --> 00:28:56.735
And it's interesting
how that blueprint

00:28:56.735 --> 00:28:58.670
gets imprinted on you.

00:28:58.670 --> 00:29:03.141
And then, you know, as a kind
of survivor of this trauma,

00:29:03.141 --> 00:29:05.777
you then become this
person who is like,

00:29:05.777 --> 00:29:07.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
I want the attention
on me, you know,

00:29:07.645 --> 00:29:09.080
how can I get the attention?

00:29:09.080 --> 00:29:11.549
How can I be seen
in a certain way?

00:29:11.549 --> 00:29:15.653 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so many of the pursuits
that you've gone after

00:29:15.653 --> 00:29:19.557 align:start size:84% position:16%
are kind of like,
well, what do you do?

00:29:19.557 --> 00:29:21.593 align:start size:84% position:16%
You make movies or you
become an Olympian,

00:29:21.593 --> 00:29:23.628
that way it's undeniable, right?

00:29:23.628 --> 00:29:25.730
Everybody's gonna have
to pay attention to you.

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:30.735 align:start position:0%
- Yes, it was a completely
effective and unhealthy pursuit

00:29:31.636 --> 00:29:33.271 align:start size:72% position:28%
of chasing-
- And awesome.

00:29:33.271 --> 00:29:35.573
Like it's messy and
complicated, right?

00:29:35.573 --> 00:29:38.543
- But I was chasing, I
think for so many years,

00:29:41.446 --> 00:29:42.914
external solutions to
an internal problem,

00:29:46.284 --> 00:29:50.221 align:start size:88% position:13%
like it was so
effective though, right?

00:29:50.221 --> 00:29:52.490 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was like, I can put
myself through as much pain

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:54.859
as humanly possible
in the good way.

00:29:54.859 --> 00:29:56.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like 'cause I
consider running pain

00:29:56.961 --> 00:30:01.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
or the vulnerability of acting
with someone as good pain.

00:30:01.733 --> 00:30:04.969
I was like, I will go
to the world's edge

00:30:04.969 --> 00:30:09.707 align:start size:88% position:13%
in the game of good
pain and just watch me.

00:30:09.707 --> 00:30:12.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it has been
tremendously powerful,

00:30:12.811 --> 00:30:16.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
like I can go to the
end of good pain.

00:30:16.714 --> 00:30:19.918
- And what does that,
you know, diving deeper

00:30:19.918 --> 00:30:23.521
into the relationship with
pain, like how does that act

00:30:23.521 --> 00:30:28.326
as a therapeutic for
some of these issues?

00:30:28.326 --> 00:30:31.763 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, it's not the
feeling of pain is therapy,

00:30:31.763 --> 00:30:32.864
it's the feeling,

00:30:34.766 --> 00:30:37.001
it's the feeling of
mattering that I get

00:30:37.001 --> 00:30:39.470 align:start size:84% position:16%
as a result of
enduring that pain.

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:42.006
And that's not been an entirely,

00:30:42.006 --> 00:30:45.577
I think, healthy thing to chase.

00:30:45.577 --> 00:30:48.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
To chase, I want to matter
because I didn't feel

00:30:48.613 --> 00:30:51.482 align:start size:84% position:16%
like I mattered enough
for my mom to stay.

00:30:51.482 --> 00:30:53.685
I now understand
that not to be true

00:30:53.685 --> 00:30:58.056
but for so much of my life,
I think until the Olympics,

00:30:58.056 --> 00:31:01.826 align:start size:88% position:13%
I thought that my
mission in life would be

00:31:01.826 --> 00:31:03.595
to not be like her,

00:31:03.595 --> 00:31:05.697
to be as far from
her as possible

00:31:05.697 --> 00:31:09.300
which I saw as being
successful and happy.

00:31:09.300 --> 00:31:12.370
And the ways that I
might've chased that

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:17.375 align:start size:81% position:19%
were chasing these
external goals.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:22.380 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, but through
running, did you, you know,

00:31:22.380 --> 00:31:24.482 align:start size:88% position:13%
there's something
cathartic about running

00:31:24.482 --> 00:31:27.619 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that relationship
with pain that does work

00:31:27.619 --> 00:31:31.456
to kind of self emotional
wounds in a certain way,

00:31:31.456 --> 00:31:33.424
like in a kind of
meditative way,

00:31:33.424 --> 00:31:37.228
like it's almost like
performing an exorcism,

00:31:37.228 --> 00:31:41.032
you know, of unconscious
things that are going on

00:31:42.233 --> 00:31:45.436
in your awareness
that are traumatic.

00:31:45.436 --> 00:31:47.505
- Yeah, that's how
you feel about it?

00:31:47.505 --> 00:31:48.539
- Yeah, I do.

00:31:49.674 --> 00:31:53.244
Yeah, I do. Not for you though.

00:31:53.244 --> 00:31:54.712
- No, no, I think I do too.

00:31:54.712 --> 00:31:58.283
I think that I feel that

00:31:58.283 --> 00:32:02.186
as long as I'm in the
territory of good pain,

00:32:02.186 --> 00:32:06.991 align:start size:84% position:16%
then that's a good
application of myself.

00:32:06.991 --> 00:32:11.996 align:start position:0%
And I think that she could have
been chasing good pain too.

00:32:13.164 --> 00:32:17.702
She just didn't have
the right mentorship.

00:32:18.870 --> 00:32:20.705 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like she just didn't
have the guidance

00:32:20.705 --> 00:32:23.207
to go there instead
of there, right?

00:32:24.309 --> 00:32:27.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
But running does
feel like that to me.

00:32:27.845 --> 00:32:29.447
It feels like a really...

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:34.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
You're on the outermost
limits of yourself mentally

00:32:34.852 --> 00:32:36.354
and physically with running.

00:32:36.354 --> 00:32:40.792
And so it is, you are dancing
on that edge and it's good.

00:32:43.328 --> 00:32:45.763
- So much of your
life was premised

00:32:45.763 --> 00:32:48.733 align:start size:84% position:16%
on differentiating
yourself from your mom

00:32:48.733 --> 00:32:52.971
like out of a fear that you
might follow in her footsteps

00:32:52.971 --> 00:32:54.505
in a certain way.

00:32:54.505 --> 00:32:58.910
And I would imagine that you
felt like you had escaped that

00:33:00.111 --> 00:33:02.747
in some way, but as
the OP-Doc shows,

00:33:04.682 --> 00:33:07.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
and as you talk about in
your book, like, nonetheless,

00:33:07.452 --> 00:33:10.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
you have this experience
with depression

00:33:10.121 --> 00:33:13.591
in the aftermath of
the Olympics, you
know, most acutely,

00:33:15.026 --> 00:33:19.530 align:start position:0%
it must've been terrifying
when that was visited upon you.

00:33:19.530 --> 00:33:21.866
You must've thought
like, oh my goodness,

00:33:21.866 --> 00:33:24.035
am I going to end
up like my mother?

00:33:24.035 --> 00:33:25.336
- Yeah, it was.

00:33:26.404 --> 00:33:28.406
I mean, when I started having...

00:33:30.041 --> 00:33:32.377
First of all I didn't
have the vocabulary

00:33:32.377 --> 00:33:35.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I tried to
share in the OP-Doc.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:37.148
I didn't have that vocabulary

00:33:38.416 --> 00:33:40.918 align:start size:97% position:3%
when I started experiencing
these symptoms.

00:33:40.918 --> 00:33:44.188 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I was in total
denial that I was sick

00:33:44.188 --> 00:33:46.791
because I didn't understand

00:33:46.791 --> 00:33:49.727 align:start size:97% position:3%
that your brain can get
injured just like your knee.

00:33:49.727 --> 00:33:51.562
I just didn't understand that.

00:33:51.562 --> 00:33:56.567
And so I was of the mindset as
I had always been in my life

00:33:56.567 --> 00:33:59.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I needed to
keep pressing forward

00:33:59.303 --> 00:34:03.241 align:start position:0%
and fix all on my own, whatever
it was that I was feeling

00:34:03.241 --> 00:34:06.044
to the point where
I wasn't sleeping

00:34:06.044 --> 00:34:08.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I tried to force
myself to sleep.

00:34:08.312 --> 00:34:11.749
And, you know, it's called
falling asleep for a reason.

00:34:11.749 --> 00:34:14.786
You have to it happen.

00:34:14.786 --> 00:34:16.621
- Being a good type A athlete,

00:34:16.621 --> 00:34:19.590
you're trying to know whirl
everything into existence.

00:34:19.590 --> 00:34:22.794
- And then I started to have
these even darker thoughts,

00:34:22.794 --> 00:34:26.798
and that's when I felt
like I understood my mom

00:34:26.798 --> 00:34:29.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a way that I never
wanted to understand her.

00:34:29.634 --> 00:34:33.905 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, you have these
thoughts that you wanna die

00:34:33.905 --> 00:34:37.075 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I don't think you
really want to want to die

00:34:37.075 --> 00:34:40.945
but the thoughts say otherwise,
and that's when you're sick.

00:34:42.513 --> 00:34:46.317 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it was terrifying because
I've always been afraid

00:34:46.317 --> 00:34:51.322 align:start size:97% position:3%
my whole life for the moment
that might happen to me.

00:34:52.790 --> 00:34:56.794
And where that fear came from
was I have a photo book of her

00:34:57.662 --> 00:34:59.564
when she was like teenager.

00:34:59.564 --> 00:35:02.166
And she looked really happy.
And I was like, there's no way.

00:35:02.166 --> 00:35:05.470
I just don't believe that
this 16-year old knows

00:35:05.470 --> 00:35:06.971
what's gonna happen to her.

00:35:06.971 --> 00:35:09.407
And that was so scary 'cause
I was like, is it gonna...

00:35:09.407 --> 00:35:12.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
Am I a ticking time bomb?
Does it just happen to people?

00:35:13.678 --> 00:35:16.581 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you know, her brother
took his own life.

00:35:16.581 --> 00:35:18.216
Like it runs deep in her family.

00:35:18.216 --> 00:35:20.818 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I just knew that
there was a possibility,

00:35:20.818 --> 00:35:24.088
and then when it did,
but before I understood

00:35:24.088 --> 00:35:26.090
that there was a
way to get better,

00:35:26.090 --> 00:35:28.459
I thought that that
was just my fate.

00:35:28.459 --> 00:35:31.329
That like, it now happened to me

00:35:31.329 --> 00:35:34.332 align:start size:94% position:6%
and because the narrative
I was told about her

00:35:34.332 --> 00:35:36.367
was she just had to go,

00:35:36.367 --> 00:35:39.737 align:start size:88% position:13%
like she just was so
sick that she had to go.

00:35:39.737 --> 00:35:42.340
And I was like, well
I guess I'm so sick

00:35:42.340 --> 00:35:43.674
that maybe I have to go

00:35:43.674 --> 00:35:45.877
'cause I don't know
what else there is.

00:35:47.211 --> 00:35:50.014 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's so embarrassing
honestly to share

00:35:50.014 --> 00:35:52.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I don't feel
that way anymore,

00:35:52.617 --> 00:35:54.118
but I didn't understand.

00:35:54.118 --> 00:35:58.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think it's sad that even
someone who was susceptible

00:35:58.055 --> 00:35:59.323
to these things, you know,

00:35:59.323 --> 00:36:02.393
my family history
was public, right?

00:36:02.393 --> 00:36:06.164
There was no prehab, if you
will, if you wanna call like,

00:36:06.164 --> 00:36:07.865 align:start size:84% position:16%
if we wanna use
this body comparison

00:36:07.865 --> 00:36:11.135
of the brain is a body part.

00:36:11.135 --> 00:36:14.772 align:start size:97% position:3%
I had no prehab, I had no
preparation to deal with this.

00:36:14.772 --> 00:36:17.508
And it wasn't until my dad,

00:36:17.508 --> 00:36:20.645 align:start size:97% position:3%
because of his experience
with my mom made me get help

00:36:20.645 --> 00:36:23.281
that I met a doctor, Dr. Pam,

00:36:23.281 --> 00:36:27.084 align:start size:84% position:16%
who told me very
simply that I was sick

00:36:27.084 --> 00:36:28.953 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that my brain
had a scratch on it.

00:36:28.953 --> 00:36:32.056 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that it could get injured,
like any other body part

00:36:32.056 --> 00:36:34.759
but it could also heal
like any other body part

00:36:34.759 --> 00:36:37.495
and suddenly everything like,

00:36:37.495 --> 00:36:39.797
it literally turned
around in a day.

00:36:39.797 --> 00:36:42.800
I wasn't happy, but I believed

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:44.902
that I could be on
a path to healing

00:36:46.404 --> 00:36:47.905
and that I could commit to it

00:36:47.905 --> 00:36:49.874
just like I would
in Olympic dream.

00:36:51.342 --> 00:36:53.477
- Did it also provide you
with a little more empathy

00:36:53.477 --> 00:36:56.914
for your mom?
- Oh, 100%.

00:36:58.349 --> 00:37:00.551 align:start size:97% position:3%
My relationship with her has
been such a rollercoaster

00:37:00.551 --> 00:37:03.955
where I didn't know how
she died for a long time.

00:37:03.955 --> 00:37:05.256
So I felt sorry for her

00:37:05.256 --> 00:37:08.159
'cause I used to think
she died from smoking.

00:37:08.159 --> 00:37:09.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I was really angry-
- You didn't find out

00:37:09.694 --> 00:37:11.796
until like seventh grade or
something like that, right?

00:37:11.796 --> 00:37:14.365 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And it was Amanda,
our dear friend, Amanda,

00:37:14.365 --> 00:37:16.234
who loves you, (laughs),

00:37:17.301 --> 00:37:18.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
Who-
- She should be here today.

00:37:18.903 --> 00:37:20.905
- She should be here today.

00:37:20.905 --> 00:37:22.373
She's saving lives.
- Next time Amanda.

00:37:22.373 --> 00:37:23.808
- She's saving lives.

00:37:23.808 --> 00:37:27.478 align:start size:97% position:3%
So we were in middle school
and there's this day,

00:37:27.478 --> 00:37:29.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
where everyone was
supposed to make tombstones

00:37:29.947 --> 00:37:32.883
for people that they,
cardboard tombstones,

00:37:32.883 --> 00:37:35.853
people they knew to
have died of smoking.

00:37:35.853 --> 00:37:38.923
And I wrote my mom's
name, felt very brave,

00:37:38.923 --> 00:37:40.791
you know those moments
in your life where like,

00:37:40.791 --> 00:37:43.261
today's the day where I'm brave.

00:37:43.261 --> 00:37:45.730
So it was brave and I
wrote her name down,

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:48.499 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it was in retrospect
really embarrassing

00:37:48.499 --> 00:37:51.335
because that's not how she
died and everybody else knew,

00:37:51.335 --> 00:37:53.170
but I didn't know.

00:37:53.170 --> 00:37:54.505
And it's in the yearbook.

00:37:54.505 --> 00:37:55.806
It's in my seventh
grade year book,

00:37:55.806 --> 00:37:58.042
this picture, it was
on no smoking day.

00:37:58.042 --> 00:37:59.944
Her name is there.
- Oh, my goodness.

00:38:01.379 --> 00:38:03.981 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And everyone else wrote
Walt Disney for some reason.

00:38:03.981 --> 00:38:06.250
And then Amanda pulled
me aside and she told me

00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:09.253
that her mom told her she
should tell me the truth.

00:38:09.253 --> 00:38:12.757
And it was really a
gift that she told me

00:38:12.757 --> 00:38:15.092
'cause it's good to know.

00:38:15.092 --> 00:38:17.495 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, but you
must've been off too

00:38:17.495 --> 00:38:20.064 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you were the last
person to find out.

00:38:20.064 --> 00:38:22.967
- You know, I've never been
able to like truly be mad

00:38:22.967 --> 00:38:25.803 align:start size:84% position:16%
at my dad for anything
relating to my mom.

00:38:25.803 --> 00:38:30.107
Like I was angry
in the preteen way,

00:38:30.107 --> 00:38:32.576
but I wasn't angry
in the real way.

00:38:32.576 --> 00:38:34.478
And I was never gonna tell him.

00:38:34.478 --> 00:38:36.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like I remember I was like,
I'm not gonna tell him

00:38:36.213 --> 00:38:41.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I know because
clearly this is, I just...

00:38:42.286 --> 00:38:43.554
And there's a chapter
in the book called

00:38:43.554 --> 00:38:45.189
"Dad's Said That's
About Just This,"

00:38:45.189 --> 00:38:47.191
where you're just more angry

00:38:47.191 --> 00:38:50.594 align:start size:88% position:13%
at the circumstance
that he's in in general,

00:38:50.594 --> 00:38:51.796
it feels unfair.

00:38:51.796 --> 00:38:54.799
So I wasn't angry at
him for not telling me,

00:38:54.799 --> 00:38:58.135
just like I'm not angry at
him for any of the strange

00:38:58.135 --> 00:39:00.371 align:start size:84% position:16%
or unusual parenting
that he's done.

00:39:00.371 --> 00:39:03.441
- Well, your dad was in
an impossible situation.

00:39:03.441 --> 00:39:06.210 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he rose to the
occasion like a champ.

00:39:06.210 --> 00:39:08.512
I mean, there's things that
he did to like raise you

00:39:08.512 --> 00:39:11.182
and your brother
are quite amazing.

00:39:11.182 --> 00:39:12.583 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I wanna get into
that in a minute,

00:39:12.583 --> 00:39:17.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
but on this subject
of depression

00:39:17.388 --> 00:39:19.357
and elite athletics.

00:39:19.357 --> 00:39:22.660 align:start position:0%
We're in a moment right now
where we're having conversations

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:24.362
that we should have
had a long time ago.

00:39:24.362 --> 00:39:27.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's very much in the kind
of mainstream parlance.

00:39:28.399 --> 00:39:31.969 align:start position:0%
I recently had Caroline Burckle
on the show, an Olympian,

00:39:31.969 --> 00:39:36.107
who has her version
of your experience.

00:39:36.107 --> 00:39:38.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
We've talked about the "Weight
of Gold" that documentary,

00:39:38.976 --> 00:39:41.145
and kind of what Michael
Phelps is doing right now

00:39:41.145 --> 00:39:43.214
to talk about these issues.

00:39:43.214 --> 00:39:47.051
So there's an openness, I
think, to this discussion

00:39:47.051 --> 00:39:49.820
but I think what makes your
story a little bit different

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:54.825 align:start position:0%
and unique is that most of these
athletes are so singularly.

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:57.962
They're like plump, like
they're just on this track

00:39:57.962 --> 00:40:00.798
and then they either make
the Olympics or they don't,

00:40:00.798 --> 00:40:03.467 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then they're faced with
this existential crisis

00:40:03.467 --> 00:40:04.935 align:start size:81% position:19%
about what they're
supposed to do

00:40:04.935 --> 00:40:09.707
with the rest of their life and
they're only 24, 30 at most.

00:40:11.075 --> 00:40:13.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you're unique in
that you already not only

00:40:13.344 --> 00:40:17.348
had a complete grip on what
it is that you wanted to do

00:40:17.348 --> 00:40:21.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
outside of sport, you're
already fully immersed in it

00:40:21.085 --> 00:40:22.586
and successful in it.

00:40:22.586 --> 00:40:26.657
So that transition away
from athletic performance

00:40:26.657 --> 00:40:29.126
into creative pursuits,

00:40:29.126 --> 00:40:31.862
that path was already
being blazed by you.

00:40:31.862 --> 00:40:34.865
And yet that was not enough

00:40:34.865 --> 00:40:37.835
for you to escape
this experience,

00:40:37.835 --> 00:40:41.672 align:start position:0%
which whether genetic or
whatever it is still visited you

00:40:41.672 --> 00:40:44.074
and was still very difficult.

00:40:44.074 --> 00:40:46.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, perhaps that's
like the epiphany

00:40:46.444 --> 00:40:48.446 align:start size:84% position:16%
that some people have
when they realize

00:40:48.446 --> 00:40:52.583
that running hurts for
everyone, no matter what.

00:40:52.583 --> 00:40:56.020 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like when I talked to
people and they realized

00:40:56.020 --> 00:40:59.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
that running still hurts
me, they're amazed.

00:40:59.924 --> 00:41:03.093
They're like, wait, it hurts
you just like it hurts me?

00:41:03.093 --> 00:41:04.562
I'm like, it does hurt me.

00:41:04.562 --> 00:41:08.232 align:start size:97% position:3%
I just have a different
relationship now with pain.

00:41:08.232 --> 00:41:11.001
And I see it as more of a
sensation, not a threat.

00:41:11.001 --> 00:41:14.238
But I think the thought
that anyone is immune

00:41:14.238 --> 00:41:19.043
to this kind of scratch on
your brain or these illnesses

00:41:19.043 --> 00:41:21.812 align:start size:97% position:3%
is just like that
misconception where it's like,

00:41:21.812 --> 00:41:24.982
no actually anxiety
about the future

00:41:24.982 --> 00:41:29.987 align:start position:0%
or a fundamental
misunderstanding about the brain

00:41:31.422 --> 00:41:35.359 align:start size:97% position:3%
or whatever it is that leads
to these mental illnesses

00:41:36.727 --> 00:41:38.195
can happen to everyone.

00:41:38.195 --> 00:41:42.933 align:start position:0%
And to think, it's like how
growing up is hard for everyone.

00:41:42.933 --> 00:41:47.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like whether you're extremely
privileged or challenge,

00:41:47.471 --> 00:41:51.475 align:start size:84% position:16%
no matter what I think
in different ways,

00:41:51.475 --> 00:41:53.978
growing up is always
gonna be challenging.

00:41:53.978 --> 00:41:58.949 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so probably to
assign a godlike maturity

00:42:01.285 --> 00:42:03.988
to someone who's at
the top of their game

00:42:03.988 --> 00:42:06.590
is not the right thing, right?

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:09.293 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like we might be
really, really mature.

00:42:09.293 --> 00:42:12.396 align:start size:88% position:13%
Our bodies might be
totally peaked on paper.

00:42:12.396 --> 00:42:14.899 align:start size:88% position:13%
We might be doing great,
but there's still,

00:42:14.899 --> 00:42:17.801
we all have to go through
the emotional maturity

00:42:17.801 --> 00:42:22.806
in growing up and if
we haven't been given

00:42:24.208 --> 00:42:26.810
the vocabulary to see our
lives a different way,

00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:29.613
or to see those challenges
when they come differently,

00:42:29.613 --> 00:42:32.716
then we're gonna,
we might mess up.

00:42:32.716 --> 00:42:35.920
- Right, right, right, I
mean, it's that identity

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:37.821
that you shoulder as an Olympian

00:42:37.821 --> 00:42:41.692
but also the projection that
everybody places on you, right?

00:42:41.692 --> 00:42:45.162 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like you're supposed
to be immune

00:42:45.162 --> 00:42:46.630
from these sorts
of things, right?

00:42:46.630 --> 00:42:49.233
So then of course that
makes it more difficult

00:42:49.233 --> 00:42:51.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
for the person who's
suffering to actually reckon

00:42:51.669 --> 00:42:54.238 align:start size:97% position:3%
with what's happening because
there's guilt and shame

00:42:54.238 --> 00:42:56.907
and why is this happening to me?

00:42:56.907 --> 00:42:59.276
I have no reason to feel the
way that I'm feeling right now.

00:42:59.276 --> 00:43:02.379
- For sure, when you feel
like the world sees you

00:43:02.379 --> 00:43:06.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
a completely different
way than you see yourself,

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:10.754 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's nothing
more mind-blowingly
difficult than that.

00:43:11.789 --> 00:43:14.124
It's so weird. It's so strange.

00:43:14.124 --> 00:43:16.827 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And then to talk about
it feels indulgent.

00:43:16.827 --> 00:43:20.297
Like you're gonna burden
somebody with your like,

00:43:20.297 --> 00:43:22.466 align:start size:81% position:19%
you know, Madam
Olympian over here

00:43:22.466 --> 00:43:24.935
with your little
depression problem.

00:43:24.935 --> 00:43:26.470 align:start size:75% position:25%
- For sure.
(host laughing)

00:43:26.470 --> 00:43:30.774
But again, I think that's
abstracting it to like...

00:43:31.976 --> 00:43:33.711
We are in a place where
we can accept, I think,

00:43:33.711 --> 00:43:37.181 align:start size:97% position:3%
as a world that elite
athletes and high-achievers

00:43:37.181 --> 00:43:41.418 align:start position:0%
can have these mental injuries,
these mental illnesses.

00:43:41.418 --> 00:43:44.154 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I think the most
important thing now is like,

00:43:45.389 --> 00:43:47.224
what do we do about them?

00:43:47.224 --> 00:43:51.895 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that was something
that I find that sometimes,

00:43:51.895 --> 00:43:55.933
you know, we point fingers
at the pinnacle institutions

00:43:55.933 --> 00:43:58.802
that we're chasing but
actually I truly think

00:43:58.802 --> 00:44:00.604
that this kind of education

00:44:00.604 --> 00:44:02.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
or shift has to
happen much younger

00:44:02.973 --> 00:44:06.276
and on a more universal level,

00:44:06.276 --> 00:44:08.679
not just at those pinnacles.

00:44:08.679 --> 00:44:10.948
- So what would that look
like if you were in charge

00:44:10.948 --> 00:44:13.484
and could put those
things in place?

00:44:13.484 --> 00:44:17.154
- Well, let's look at body,

00:44:18.255 --> 00:44:19.790 align:start size:84% position:16%
the way we've
approached the body

00:44:19.790 --> 00:44:22.393 align:start size:94% position:6%
and like how that's
progressed over the last,

00:44:22.393 --> 00:44:23.627
let's say 10 years.

00:44:23.627 --> 00:44:26.664
Like 10 years ago,
I don't think my dad

00:44:26.664 --> 00:44:30.300 align:start size:97% position:3%
or my friend's little
sister would have seen a PT

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:34.038 align:start size:84% position:16%
for their body without
having an injury.

00:44:34.038 --> 00:44:37.007
Meaning like regularly
take care of their body.

00:44:37.007 --> 00:44:40.577
And so just looking at that
world, we've come a long way

00:44:40.577 --> 00:44:43.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
to accepting that our
body is something not only

00:44:43.447 --> 00:44:44.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
elite athletes
should take care of,

00:44:44.948 --> 00:44:46.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
but everybody
should take care of.

00:44:46.717 --> 00:44:49.453
And that we should take care
of it before it's a problem,

00:44:49.453 --> 00:44:53.424
ideally, if you are able to
have that kind of support

00:44:53.424 --> 00:44:54.491
and it's not...

00:44:56.093 --> 00:44:59.763
There's just like systems and
you can get that kind of help

00:44:59.763 --> 00:45:02.099
if you can and need it, right?

00:45:02.099 --> 00:45:03.734
And I think with mental health,

00:45:04.902 --> 00:45:09.440
comparing it to
healing an injury

00:45:09.440 --> 00:45:12.476
is so simple to me and
makes so much sense.

00:45:12.476 --> 00:45:16.580
So what it would look like
to me is accepting honestly

00:45:16.580 --> 00:45:19.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
that our brain is a body
part and it can get injured.

00:45:19.883 --> 00:45:24.054 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when it gets injured, just
like when we break our leg

00:45:24.054 --> 00:45:26.824
or feel something
strange in our leg,

00:45:26.824 --> 00:45:30.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
we have no shame about
sharing that something's off

00:45:30.527 --> 00:45:33.497
and we get help and we
know where to get help.

00:45:33.497 --> 00:45:36.767
And it's either built into
the system that we're in,

00:45:36.767 --> 00:45:38.669
like a team might have a physio,

00:45:38.669 --> 00:45:40.871
they might have a
psychologist too.

00:45:40.871 --> 00:45:44.141
Or someone can refer you
to their favorite physio

00:45:44.141 --> 00:45:45.375
or their favorite psycho.

00:45:45.375 --> 00:45:47.211
Like there's just
more accessibility,

00:45:47.211 --> 00:45:49.680
just like there is
in like the PT world.

00:45:51.115 --> 00:45:53.917
And then we get that help and
we are as kind to ourselves

00:45:53.917 --> 00:45:55.219
as we are hard on ourselves

00:45:55.219 --> 00:45:57.721 align:start size:88% position:13%
knowing that it's not
gonna resolve overnight.

00:45:57.721 --> 00:45:59.123
Like nobody is demanding

00:45:59.123 --> 00:46:01.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
that somebody's broken
leg heal tomorrow.

00:46:01.558 --> 00:46:03.193
And so why are we demanding

00:46:03.193 --> 00:46:06.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
that somebody's
depression heal tomorrow?

00:46:06.463 --> 00:46:09.833
- Because it's uncomfortable
for us to talk about, right?

00:46:09.833 --> 00:46:13.070 align:start position:0%
We wanna pretend that it doesn't
exist or there's shame or-

00:46:13.070 --> 00:46:14.471
- Right, because it's invisible.

00:46:14.471 --> 00:46:17.775
But if we think about
it more like an injury,

00:46:17.775 --> 00:46:21.645 align:start size:88% position:13%
like no one can see your
torn hamstring either

00:46:21.645 --> 00:46:23.380 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they know that it's
there if you say it

00:46:23.380 --> 00:46:25.482
and they believe it's there.

00:46:25.482 --> 00:46:28.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if we just see it as
more of a physical injury,

00:46:28.118 --> 00:46:31.455
which it is, then I think
it becomes a little less

00:46:32.556 --> 00:46:35.659 align:start size:84% position:16%
subjective and a
little more objective.

00:46:35.659 --> 00:46:38.428 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, well, I also think
in the athlete context

00:46:38.428 --> 00:46:41.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
that at a university
every athletic department

00:46:41.999 --> 00:46:47.004
has a whole like complex
around physical therapy.

00:46:47.871 --> 00:46:50.174
But the mind is only beginning

00:46:50.174 --> 00:46:53.010
to be addressed in that world.

00:46:53.010 --> 00:46:56.113 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's so important
we're just now beginning

00:46:56.113 --> 00:46:58.816
to really understand
how critical it is

00:46:58.816 --> 00:47:02.419 align:start size:88% position:13%
that our emotional
wellbeing is attended to

00:47:02.419 --> 00:47:05.656 align:start size:88% position:13%
and needs to be rehabbed
from time to time

00:47:05.656 --> 00:47:08.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the same way
that your joints do.

00:47:08.625 --> 00:47:10.627
- Totally. I think that's it.

00:47:16.834 --> 00:47:18.735
I can only speak
to my experience

00:47:18.735 --> 00:47:22.773 align:start size:94% position:6%
but that change of
perspective saved my life.

00:47:22.773 --> 00:47:27.077
Like it allowed me
to buy into a process

00:47:27.077 --> 00:47:29.413 align:start size:97% position:3%
and believe in it and
believe that it could work.

00:47:29.413 --> 00:47:34.418 align:start position:0%
And I remember being told
you're going to be sad every day

00:47:34.418 --> 00:47:36.086
for a long time.

00:47:36.086 --> 00:47:39.523 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it was not unlike
hearing when I broke my foot,

00:47:39.523 --> 00:47:42.526
your foot is gonna be broken
every day for a long time.

00:47:42.526 --> 00:47:44.628
It's gonna take
12 weeks to heal.

00:47:44.628 --> 00:47:46.296
But it will be healing.

00:47:46.296 --> 00:47:48.632
And I started to
think about myself

00:47:48.632 --> 00:47:53.637
as like a crockpot, like
a soup where, you know,

00:47:54.838 --> 00:47:56.607
so I was told that I
was going to be sad

00:47:56.607 --> 00:47:58.642 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that I should rather
focus on my actions

00:47:58.642 --> 00:48:00.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that those were the
only things I could control.

00:48:00.911 --> 00:48:03.480
And I thought about
myself as that soup

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:06.416
where I was gonna put in actions

00:48:06.416 --> 00:48:11.121
and I wasn't gonna know in
what ratio, like, you know,

00:48:11.121 --> 00:48:12.823
how much did medication help

00:48:12.823 --> 00:48:14.725
versus cognitive
behavioral therapy

00:48:14.725 --> 00:48:16.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
versus finally getting
the sleep I needed

00:48:16.660 --> 00:48:19.696
versus simply waiting
for a period of time

00:48:19.696 --> 00:48:20.964
versus going for walks.

00:48:20.964 --> 00:48:23.567
Like you never know
what kind of ratios,

00:48:23.567 --> 00:48:24.768
what the perfect ratio is,

00:48:24.768 --> 00:48:27.371
but you do know the
soup is cooking.

00:48:27.371 --> 00:48:28.972
You're putting in ingredients,

00:48:28.972 --> 00:48:30.540
you're stirring, you're waiting

00:48:32.442 --> 00:48:33.977
and it will become soup.

00:48:33.977 --> 00:48:36.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right, and in the
meantime, just being told

00:48:36.046 --> 00:48:38.081
like you're gonna feel this way

00:48:38.081 --> 00:48:40.350
allows you to be in acceptance

00:48:40.350 --> 00:48:42.552
rather than beating yourself up

00:48:42.552 --> 00:48:45.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you woke up again,
it didn't feel good.

00:48:45.889 --> 00:48:48.725 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You don't have the secondary
emotion of being offended

00:48:48.725 --> 00:48:50.394
by the sadness as much.

00:48:50.394 --> 00:48:53.263
You almost are like, you
wake up with the sadness.

00:48:53.263 --> 00:48:55.766
Like it's, you know,
I don't have a child

00:48:55.766 --> 00:48:58.435 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I think about like,
if a baby's crying

00:48:58.435 --> 00:49:01.104
and you're at the grocery
store, the baby's crying

00:49:01.104 --> 00:49:03.173
but you still gotta to
get the milk, you know?

00:49:03.173 --> 00:49:05.742
So I think it in
that way sometimes

00:49:05.742 --> 00:49:08.979
where you almost have a
sense of humor about it

00:49:08.979 --> 00:49:13.183
or at least some levity to
understand that it's there,

00:49:13.183 --> 00:49:14.651
it will be there for a while

00:49:14.651 --> 00:49:17.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're in the process
of making it go away.

00:49:17.788 --> 00:49:20.557
- It almost sounds like
stepping outside of it,

00:49:20.557 --> 00:49:22.426 align:start size:84% position:16%
like not self
identifying with it.

00:49:22.426 --> 00:49:25.028
It's this thing. It's not me.

00:49:25.028 --> 00:49:27.497
- Yes, it's this-

00:49:29.900 --> 00:49:32.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Entity and sadness.
- Yeah, it's your sadness.

00:49:35.272 --> 00:49:39.710
And I was told that
actions change first,

00:49:39.710 --> 00:49:43.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
then thoughts, then
feelings, and in that order.

00:49:43.213 --> 00:49:46.783 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that was another life
saving rule basically

00:49:46.783 --> 00:49:50.520 align:start size:94% position:6%
because what I've observed
from my mom was that

00:49:50.520 --> 00:49:53.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
her caretakers were trying
to force the feelings

00:49:53.190 --> 00:49:56.727 align:start size:97% position:3%
and she was trying to force
the feelings to change.

00:49:56.727 --> 00:49:58.328
And we can't.

00:49:58.328 --> 00:50:01.665
They follow our thoughts,
which follow our actions.

00:50:01.665 --> 00:50:04.167
- Right, I always say
mood follows action.

00:50:04.167 --> 00:50:05.769 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Perfect, right?
- Right.

00:50:05.769 --> 00:50:07.838
So you get to the
other side of this,

00:50:07.838 --> 00:50:10.407
what is your daily regimen now

00:50:10.407 --> 00:50:14.945
for kind of maintaining
your mental wellbeing.

00:50:14.945 --> 00:50:18.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, okay, that's
a great question.

00:50:18.115 --> 00:50:22.552
First of all, I found it
very difficult to find

00:50:22.552 --> 00:50:26.323 align:start size:97% position:3%
like psychiatric,
psychological care in general.

00:50:26.323 --> 00:50:28.191
It was hard when needed it.

00:50:28.191 --> 00:50:32.963
I had to ask for favors
and that sucked honestly

00:50:32.963 --> 00:50:34.464
that you can't get it as readily

00:50:34.464 --> 00:50:36.400
as you might be able to see a PT

00:50:36.400 --> 00:50:38.635 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I've still found
it challenging.

00:50:38.635 --> 00:50:42.005 align:start size:97% position:3%
I still found it labyrinthian
to find good care,

00:50:43.373 --> 00:50:46.743
but I've found like
continued support.

00:50:48.378 --> 00:50:50.013
I work with the
psychiatrist here

00:50:50.013 --> 00:50:52.482
to just make sure I check in.

00:50:52.482 --> 00:50:55.819
And also, I work with a team

00:50:59.589 --> 00:51:02.826 align:start position:0%
it's not just sports psychology,
it's like psychology.

00:51:02.826 --> 00:51:05.896
I work with a woman
named Natalie Pacetti

00:51:05.896 --> 00:51:10.901 align:start size:88% position:13%
and she's helped me
like continue to unbound

00:51:12.369 --> 00:51:16.907
some of the childhood personal
laws that might be driving me

00:51:16.907 --> 00:51:19.976
in an unhealthy way and
might be limiting me.

00:51:19.976 --> 00:51:23.613
So this is like an
ever unfolding thing

00:51:23.613 --> 00:51:27.017 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I'm working on it,
but on a day-to-day level,

00:51:27.017 --> 00:51:30.220
I think I'm also
monitoring myself,

00:51:30.220 --> 00:51:32.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so I take a little
bit more seriously.

00:51:32.889 --> 00:51:36.226
If there's a night where I'm
like restless and can't sleep,

00:51:36.226 --> 00:51:38.095
that's a moment to
pause and figure out

00:51:38.095 --> 00:51:40.797 align:start size:84% position:16%
like what is keeping
me up at night?

00:51:40.797 --> 00:51:44.334
Because prior I was
like plowing ahead

00:51:44.334 --> 00:51:48.705
and never not slowing down to
wonder why that was happening.

00:51:48.705 --> 00:51:52.676
And then there's this one
tool that my physio gave me

00:51:52.676 --> 00:51:54.978 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I think is the
coolest thing in the world,

00:51:56.580 --> 00:51:59.583
which is, can I share?

00:51:59.583 --> 00:52:00.517
- Yeah.

00:52:00.517 --> 00:52:01.785
- I feel like I'm talking a lot.

00:52:01.785 --> 00:52:03.453
- No, come on, this is
what we're here to do.

00:52:03.453 --> 00:52:05.255
- This is so cool.
- I wanna here this.

00:52:05.255 --> 00:52:07.724
- Okay, so I had this physio,

00:52:08.758 --> 00:52:10.494
he is from Japan.

00:52:10.494 --> 00:52:12.996
I was working with him,
his name's Cudi in LA.

00:52:15.499 --> 00:52:18.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was having like some
symptoms in my hip one time.

00:52:18.735 --> 00:52:22.339 align:start size:97% position:3%
And instead of like looking
at my hip right away,

00:52:22.339 --> 00:52:24.474
he asked me about my face.

00:52:24.474 --> 00:52:26.776
And he was like, "have
you noticed anything?"

00:52:28.211 --> 00:52:29.779
'Cause I was like, is my hip
broken? Like what's going on?

00:52:29.779 --> 00:52:32.616
And he was like, "okay back up,
last week when this started,

00:52:32.616 --> 00:52:34.484 align:start size:88% position:13%
did you notice
anything in your face?"

00:52:34.484 --> 00:52:39.222
And I actually had like
a small red discoloration

00:52:39.222 --> 00:52:40.957
under my eye that was abnormal,

00:52:40.957 --> 00:52:43.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
almost like a
little mini sunburn.

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:44.895
And he was like, okay, cool.

00:52:44.895 --> 00:52:48.431
So you have the most
nerves in your face,

00:52:48.431 --> 00:52:50.500
your stomach, and your hands.

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:53.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
And when your body is
starting to get overloaded,

00:52:53.370 --> 00:52:55.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
whether you're stressed
out or it's over-training,

00:52:55.639 --> 00:52:57.841
like the cells only no effort.

00:52:57.841 --> 00:53:01.044
So he was basically saying,
when you start overdoing it,

00:53:01.044 --> 00:53:04.414
your body wants you to succeed.

00:53:04.414 --> 00:53:07.918
So it will give you signs
that tell you to pause.

00:53:07.918 --> 00:53:10.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he told me that I needed
to pay better attention

00:53:10.320 --> 00:53:12.622
to my face because this
was not a broken hip.

00:53:12.622 --> 00:53:15.225 align:start size:88% position:13%
This was just a nervous
system overload,

00:53:15.225 --> 00:53:18.295
and my nerves, once
your face is ignored

00:53:18.295 --> 00:53:20.997 align:start size:97% position:3%
will start shutting down
parts of your body for a bit,

00:53:20.997 --> 00:53:24.634
and then eventually, you know,
it'll keep shutting you down

00:53:24.634 --> 00:53:26.703
because it wants
to preserve you.

00:53:26.703 --> 00:53:29.306
So from there forward,

00:53:29.306 --> 00:53:31.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
I started paying
close attention to...

00:53:33.243 --> 00:53:35.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
I have wonderful Greek skin
and I don't get a lot of acne.

00:53:35.512 --> 00:53:39.916 align:start position:0%
So when I get a pimple, it
might mean a day off, literally.

00:53:39.916 --> 00:53:42.719
And for other people, it
might be a canker sore

00:53:42.719 --> 00:53:46.890 align:start size:97% position:3%
or an eye twitch, or just
whatever is abnormal for you

00:53:46.890 --> 00:53:49.626 align:start size:88% position:13%
on your face, I suppose,
or your stomach,

00:53:49.626 --> 00:53:52.028
but the face is just
a really obvious one,

00:53:52.028 --> 00:53:54.331
it just means that you're
starting to get overloaded.

00:53:54.331 --> 00:53:58.001
And so what I'm doing now is
trying to pay better attention

00:53:58.001 --> 00:54:02.372 align:start size:97% position:3%
to the signs, the very
first signs of overdoing it

00:54:04.207 --> 00:54:07.043
so that I don't get to
the point of not sleeping

00:54:07.043 --> 00:54:08.011
for three months.

00:54:08.011 --> 00:54:09.913
- Right, that's fascinating.

00:54:09.913 --> 00:54:12.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Isn't it great?
- Yeah. That's very cool.

00:54:12.182 --> 00:54:13.383
I've never thought of that.

00:54:13.383 --> 00:54:16.319 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, it's sort of
kind of a acupuncturist,

00:54:16.319 --> 00:54:18.822
kind of ayurvedic way
of looking at things,

00:54:18.822 --> 00:54:21.958
like everything you know,
your body is this organism

00:54:21.958 --> 00:54:24.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's trying to always find
its balance point, right?

00:54:24.594 --> 00:54:26.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
And when something's
out of balance,

00:54:26.463 --> 00:54:29.299
there will be some kind of
symptom that might show up

00:54:29.299 --> 00:54:32.369 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a very unpredictable
and different way

00:54:32.369 --> 00:54:34.471
than you might suspect
that has nothing to do

00:54:34.471 --> 00:54:38.208
with whatever is actually
wrong right itself, right?

00:54:38.208 --> 00:54:42.746 align:start position:0%
It's a little glimmer, a glimpse
into paying more attention.

00:54:42.746 --> 00:54:46.616
- And that the body wants
you to succeed, right?

00:54:46.616 --> 00:54:49.219
It's not, I feel like
so often we're like,

00:54:49.219 --> 00:54:50.520
my body hates me today

00:54:50.520 --> 00:54:52.756
and it's like your
body never hates you.

00:54:52.756 --> 00:54:55.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
That can't be true, right?
(host laughing)

00:54:55.058 --> 00:54:56.826
- I hope not.
- I hope not.

00:54:56.826 --> 00:54:59.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so maybe we
shift our, you know,

00:54:59.562 --> 00:55:02.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
maybe we shift to feeling
like our body's rooting for us

00:55:02.165 --> 00:55:05.035
and it's telling us in all
the languages that it knows.

00:55:05.035 --> 00:55:06.536
- It can be mad at us.

00:55:06.536 --> 00:55:08.738
- Yeah, it could be
frustrated, right?

00:55:08.738 --> 00:55:11.274
But always trying to help.

00:55:11.274 --> 00:55:15.278 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right, that's a
very optimistic, cool
way to look at it.

00:55:15.278 --> 00:55:16.780
- Yeah, and I've shared that

00:55:16.780 --> 00:55:20.116
with like elite athlete
friends, and it's really,

00:55:20.116 --> 00:55:21.985 align:start size:81% position:19%
when they think
back to an injury,

00:55:23.386 --> 00:55:25.989
they can recall, like I had
a canker sore that week.

00:55:25.989 --> 00:55:30.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
And now we in my small
satellite teammate community,

00:55:32.028 --> 00:55:34.497 align:start size:88% position:13%
we pay better
attention to our faces.

00:55:34.497 --> 00:55:36.499
- That's a good practice.

00:55:36.499 --> 00:55:39.135 align:start size:81% position:19%
Well, let's go
back a little bit.

00:55:39.135 --> 00:55:43.340
You know, let's talk about
you growing up with your dad

00:55:43.340 --> 00:55:45.709
back to the kind of David
Epstein "Range" thing.

00:55:45.709 --> 00:55:49.412
Like you weren't a track
standout from the get go

00:55:49.412 --> 00:55:51.047 align:start size:94% position:6%
or you were playing all
different kinds of sports.

00:55:51.047 --> 00:55:54.284 align:start size:97% position:3%
You were like a soccer
standout originally, right?

00:55:54.284 --> 00:55:59.289 align:start size:97% position:3%
And dabbling in track but
then you were forced to quit

00:56:00.757 --> 00:56:02.826
the track team or you got
fired from the track team

00:56:02.826 --> 00:56:04.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you wouldn't
go all in on that

00:56:04.761 --> 00:56:07.263
and let go of the other sports?

00:56:07.263 --> 00:56:12.268 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Correct, there was
leadership at my high school

00:56:12.268 --> 00:56:17.273
that in running, we needed
to not do anything else.

00:56:18.675 --> 00:56:21.478 align:start size:97% position:3%
There were other sports
where it felt like athletes,

00:56:21.478 --> 00:56:23.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
particularly male
athletes were embraced

00:56:23.613 --> 00:56:25.815
for being multi-sport athletes,

00:56:25.815 --> 00:56:29.552 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I was definitely not
embraced in that way.

00:56:29.552 --> 00:56:34.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
And because I was not
willing to, at 15 years old,

00:56:35.725 --> 00:56:37.394 align:start size:81% position:19%
quit soccer and
student government

00:56:37.394 --> 00:56:38.895
and all these other things,

00:56:38.895 --> 00:56:42.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like de facto kicked
off the running team.

00:56:42.732 --> 00:56:44.134
And I was good.

00:56:44.134 --> 00:56:46.636 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was like one of the
top runners in the State

00:56:46.636 --> 00:56:49.539
but I didn't love
running at that age

00:56:49.539 --> 00:56:52.776
because I didn't love the team.

00:56:52.776 --> 00:56:54.411
There was no team environment

00:56:56.045 --> 00:56:58.214
which is my favorite
part about sports.

00:56:58.214 --> 00:57:00.083
It didn't feel positive.

00:57:00.083 --> 00:57:03.052
The coach, it wasn't a
positive environment.

00:57:03.052 --> 00:57:08.057
So I naturally did not feel
like I could specialize.

00:57:09.225 --> 00:57:10.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Did he tell you
that you had to quit

00:57:10.026 --> 00:57:11.261
student government too?

00:57:11.261 --> 00:57:13.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yes, so I was kicked
off the team twice.

00:57:13.430 --> 00:57:16.266
The first time was
because of soccer

00:57:16.266 --> 00:57:18.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the second-
- Did they overlap in seasons?

00:57:20.203 --> 00:57:24.274 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes, there were some
soccer practices overlapping

00:57:24.274 --> 00:57:27.877
with the running season.

00:57:27.877 --> 00:57:29.212 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was club soccer.
- Right.

00:57:29.212 --> 00:57:31.080
- So they do overlap.

00:57:31.080 --> 00:57:34.050
And then, the next
year, my senior year,

00:57:34.050 --> 00:57:38.054
I was like, okay, maybe I
won't do the soccer overlap

00:57:38.054 --> 00:57:39.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I was vice
president of my class

00:57:39.889 --> 00:57:42.091
and I wasn't allowed
to do that either.

00:57:42.091 --> 00:57:46.963
And that was hard as
well because what...

00:57:49.532 --> 00:57:52.202
I feel that today that would
have been like a New York times

00:57:52.202 --> 00:57:53.603 align:start size:88% position:13%
piece in and of itself-
- Right.

00:57:53.603 --> 00:57:55.772
I mean, it's high school track.

00:57:55.772 --> 00:57:57.440
I mean, come on.

00:57:57.440 --> 00:57:58.975
- I agree. I agree.

00:57:58.975 --> 00:58:01.845 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was committed, I
was there when I could be,

00:58:01.845 --> 00:58:05.114 align:start size:88% position:13%
but I was trying to
like be a whole person.

00:58:05.114 --> 00:58:09.152
And I don't think any 15
year old should be told

00:58:09.152 --> 00:58:12.388
to quit anything, you know.

00:58:12.388 --> 00:58:15.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I just remember feeling
like I was a bad kid

00:58:15.658 --> 00:58:17.760
and I didn't like that.

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:22.365
I didn't like how
shameful it felt

00:58:23.266 --> 00:58:26.035
to do what I was doing.

00:58:26.035 --> 00:58:27.337
- But at the same time,

00:58:27.337 --> 00:58:29.472
you've demonstrated
healthy boundaries.

00:58:29.472 --> 00:58:32.242 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like there's a self-awareness
and a sense of self

00:58:32.242 --> 00:58:34.644
that you demonstrated
as a young person.

00:58:34.644 --> 00:58:38.214
Because I suspect like if
I was in that situation,

00:58:38.214 --> 00:58:40.683 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I was showing promise
as a track athlete,

00:58:40.683 --> 00:58:42.986 align:start size:88% position:13%
I probably would have
just done what the coach

00:58:42.986 --> 00:58:46.089
wanted me to do and
quit everything else

00:58:46.089 --> 00:58:48.825
and gone all in on that.

00:58:48.825 --> 00:58:53.029
- Well, you might have, and
then you might have not had

00:58:54.464 --> 00:58:59.402
the joy to last you through
10 more years of training

00:59:00.570 --> 00:59:01.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
or maybe-
- Right, well I'm not saying

00:59:01.804 --> 00:59:03.273
it would have been
the right decision.

00:59:03.273 --> 00:59:04.741
I'm just thinking, like, I
don't know that my boundaries

00:59:04.741 --> 00:59:07.343 align:start size:94% position:6%
would have been much more
porous and influenced

00:59:07.343 --> 00:59:09.279
by people of authority.

00:59:09.279 --> 00:59:12.582
You're somebody who knew
yourself well enough to say

00:59:12.582 --> 00:59:14.083
these other things
are important to me,

00:59:14.083 --> 00:59:16.920 align:start size:88% position:13%
I'm not gonna do with
this guy wants me to do.

00:59:16.920 --> 00:59:21.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I've always like
been really unable do things

00:59:23.226 --> 00:59:25.862
that are against my gut.

00:59:25.862 --> 00:59:27.830
Like, it feels so bad.

00:59:28.998 --> 00:59:31.935
Feels bad, and my
gut is really loud.

00:59:35.705 --> 00:59:36.973
- So like a Greek thing?

00:59:36.973 --> 00:59:39.175
- Yeah, the Greek
gut. It's so loud.

00:59:39.175 --> 00:59:43.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have the loudest gut.
- My microbiome is very vocal.

00:59:43.313 --> 00:59:45.381
- I don't know, it
didn't feel right.

00:59:45.381 --> 00:59:49.419
Even though there wasn't like
a bigger national discussion

00:59:49.419 --> 00:59:51.788 align:start size:97% position:3%
about it at that time, there
wasn't even social media,

00:59:51.788 --> 00:59:53.723 align:start size:84% position:16%
so nobody would have
known about this,

00:59:53.723 --> 00:59:58.561
I think I knew that it wasn't
quite like a healthy thing.

01:00:00.663 --> 01:00:03.733
- So obviously you do
really well in school.

01:00:03.733 --> 01:00:05.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
You get into
Dartmouth, you know,

01:00:05.702 --> 01:00:09.205
going all the way to the
other coast for college

01:00:09.205 --> 01:00:11.608
and you end up running there,

01:00:11.608 --> 01:00:14.277
but what's amazing is that you,

01:00:14.277 --> 01:00:15.712 align:start size:81% position:19%
I have a hard time
believing this,

01:00:15.712 --> 01:00:18.648 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you were like the slowest
on the team originally?

01:00:18.648 --> 01:00:21.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I couldn't even, I mean,
I was like really fit

01:00:21.517 --> 01:00:24.687 align:start size:94% position:6%
for being a defender,
center defender in soccer.

01:00:24.687 --> 01:00:27.857 align:start size:84% position:16%
You are sprint
fast, you're middy,

01:00:27.857 --> 01:00:29.525
no one's gonna knock you over,

01:00:29.525 --> 01:00:30.927
but you can't run four miles.

01:00:30.927 --> 01:00:32.528 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It's a totally
different body type.

01:00:32.528 --> 01:00:33.496
- It's totally different.

01:00:33.496 --> 01:00:36.232
I was like, you know, dense.

01:00:36.232 --> 01:00:37.700
I was really, really dense.

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:40.503
And I think that that agility

01:00:40.503 --> 01:00:44.073 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that athleticism carried
me and kept me healthy

01:00:44.073 --> 01:00:48.845 align:start position:0%
not a single injury until after
the Olympics, pretty solid,

01:00:48.845 --> 01:00:51.247 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it did mean that
when I got to Dartmouth,

01:00:51.247 --> 01:00:55.018 align:start size:81% position:19%
I was really a
fish out of water,

01:00:55.018 --> 01:00:58.488
like across the country
first winter of my life

01:00:58.488 --> 01:01:02.358
and could not run more than
four miles without walking

01:01:02.358 --> 01:01:05.528 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it was humiliating
to be very honest.

01:01:05.528 --> 01:01:06.963
And I was failing classes

01:01:06.963 --> 01:01:10.700 align:start position:0%
'cause I was not super prepared
for the academics there.

01:01:10.700 --> 01:01:12.702 align:start size:84% position:16%
I definitely called
my dad and I was like,

01:01:12.702 --> 01:01:17.573
this was all a mistake.
- Wow, what did he say?

01:01:17.573 --> 01:01:21.177
- He said, "just
keep trying Lex."

01:01:21.177 --> 01:01:22.912
'Cause that's all he ever says.

01:01:22.912 --> 01:01:27.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I knew he wasn't gonna
be the type of parent,

01:01:27.350 --> 01:01:29.519
he's not a dragon parent,
but he wasn't gonna give me

01:01:29.519 --> 01:01:32.455
the option to quit anything.

01:01:32.455 --> 01:01:36.125 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I knew, and I
wrote this in the book

01:01:36.125 --> 01:01:39.429
that the good thing
and the bad thing

01:01:39.429 --> 01:01:41.764
about not giving up
is that it works,

01:01:41.764 --> 01:01:45.702
and I knew that it was
gonna be a long climb up.

01:01:47.036 --> 01:01:49.105
I just wanted to
score a team point

01:01:49.105 --> 01:01:50.573
but then I couldn't do it.

01:01:50.573 --> 01:01:53.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like no matter what
anyone else thought of me

01:01:53.276 --> 01:01:54.811
when I was really, really slow,

01:01:54.811 --> 01:01:58.081
I knew that with time and
patience, I could do it.

01:01:58.081 --> 01:02:00.483
It just was gonna be
really uncomfortable.

01:02:00.483 --> 01:02:03.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, you had said
that you had this feeling

01:02:03.886 --> 01:02:06.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there was a
champion inside of you.

01:02:06.189 --> 01:02:08.424 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like that's something
that you carried with you

01:02:08.424 --> 01:02:13.062
like this idea that there was
greatness packed inside of you

01:02:14.097 --> 01:02:15.732
even if it wasn't being manifest

01:02:15.732 --> 01:02:17.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
in that moment like
where does that

01:02:17.500 --> 01:02:19.235 align:start size:94% position:6%
come from?
- Where does it come from?

01:02:19.235 --> 01:02:22.071 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, I think that
comes from my mom too,

01:02:22.071 --> 01:02:23.606
because when you feel,

01:02:24.741 --> 01:02:27.143
and I don't know how
healthy this is to say,

01:02:27.143 --> 01:02:30.913
but when you feel so like...

01:02:34.450 --> 01:02:39.355
It was so wild what I saw
and experienced as a child,

01:02:39.355 --> 01:02:43.426
like some of the memories
of her were so unbelievable

01:02:45.228 --> 01:02:48.798
that I truly felt that if the
most unbelievably bad things

01:02:48.798 --> 01:02:51.033
were true then the most
unbelievably good things

01:02:51.033 --> 01:02:56.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
could be true, which I
think is a survival thing.

01:02:56.005 --> 01:02:59.275
And also that I was special.

01:02:59.275 --> 01:03:01.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was special because
this awful thing happened

01:03:01.577 --> 01:03:05.114 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then I would be
capable of the opposite.

01:03:05.114 --> 01:03:07.750 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like it was almost
like, if this is true,

01:03:07.750 --> 01:03:10.186
then the opposite
must be true too.

01:03:10.186 --> 01:03:13.189
So there's always something
inside of me that was like,

01:03:14.957 --> 01:03:19.595 align:start size:88% position:13%
that believed, suspended
disbelief or believed

01:03:19.595 --> 01:03:24.033
that I was, I don't know,

01:03:24.033 --> 01:03:26.936
like part mythical or
something, you know,

01:03:26.936 --> 01:03:28.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
something like really-
- Did that extended

01:03:28.604 --> 01:03:30.072
to being an Olympian?

01:03:30.072 --> 01:03:32.008 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like when you're the slowest
on the team at Dartmouth

01:03:32.008 --> 01:03:34.310 align:start size:94% position:6%
are you thinking, I'm
gonna go to the Olympics?

01:03:34.310 --> 01:03:38.281 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So, no, and that's another
really interesting thing

01:03:38.281 --> 01:03:41.551
that I think is important
for people to understand

01:03:41.551 --> 01:03:43.286
is that I never planned

01:03:43.286 --> 01:03:45.922
more than a year of
my life in advance.

01:03:45.922 --> 01:03:48.558
And that's so important to share

01:03:48.558 --> 01:03:51.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
because had I planned
five years in the future,

01:03:51.527 --> 01:03:53.830 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would have counted
myself short.

01:03:53.830 --> 01:03:56.532
I would have put a limit
on what I was capable of

01:03:56.532 --> 01:03:58.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause I definitely
didn't even think about

01:03:58.768 --> 01:04:00.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
being capable of
going to the Olympics

01:04:00.336 --> 01:04:02.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
when I was a
freshman in college,

01:04:02.271 --> 01:04:04.173
but I also didn't
think past a year.

01:04:04.173 --> 01:04:05.875
So I wasn't like,
it's not possible.

01:04:05.875 --> 01:04:07.577
I just didn't think about it.

01:04:07.577 --> 01:04:09.445
And this is still
how I live my life

01:04:09.445 --> 01:04:12.915
where I'm like the next year,
these are the goals, this is,

01:04:12.915 --> 01:04:14.684
like, I have big dreams,

01:04:14.684 --> 01:04:17.086
but they're more
how I wanna feel

01:04:17.086 --> 01:04:20.489
not like what I'm
gonna accomplish.

01:04:20.489 --> 01:04:22.825 align:start position:0%
And I think that's an important
thing to share with people

01:04:22.825 --> 01:04:27.597
because probably most of us
might sell ourselves short

01:04:27.597 --> 01:04:29.932
if we were to ask ourselves,

01:04:29.932 --> 01:04:32.435
where will I be in
five or 10 years?

01:04:32.435 --> 01:04:35.404
I don't know, maybe that's
not true, but for me it was.

01:04:35.404 --> 01:04:39.976
- Well, I think that we
all wildly overestimate

01:04:39.976 --> 01:04:41.444
what we can do in a year

01:04:41.444 --> 01:04:45.815
and wildly underestimate what
can be done in many years,

01:04:45.815 --> 01:04:47.617
five years, 10 years, right?

01:04:47.617 --> 01:04:50.019
- Have said that before?
- I have said this before.

01:04:50.019 --> 01:04:51.888
- And is that how
you live your life?

01:04:53.456 --> 01:04:56.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I mean, I set goals
for myself, but I
don't dwell on them.

01:04:56.592 --> 01:05:00.663
I'm really focused on what
the next thing to do is,

01:05:00.663 --> 01:05:04.767
I try to stay more present,
which is sort of a variation,

01:05:04.767 --> 01:05:06.769
I think on what you're saying.

01:05:06.769 --> 01:05:09.906
And I've learned to trust my
intuition and my instinct.

01:05:09.906 --> 01:05:12.675
So I hold those goals,
like they're out there,

01:05:12.675 --> 01:05:16.979 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I hold them loosely and
I'm always making adjustments.

01:05:16.979 --> 01:05:18.748
- And how important to you,

01:05:18.748 --> 01:05:21.384
and like, I'm so
genuinely curious,

01:05:21.384 --> 01:05:24.287 align:start size:84% position:16%
is it that other
people believe in you?

01:05:25.721 --> 01:05:28.624 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like did it matter more
when you were certain time

01:05:28.624 --> 01:05:32.194
in your life or did it ever
matter, does it matter now?

01:05:32.194 --> 01:05:36.399
- I mean, you know, I'm an
unapologetic people pleaser

01:05:36.399 --> 01:05:41.404
and premised huge parts of my
life on external validation.

01:05:42.271 --> 01:05:45.241
So I'm very connected to that

01:05:47.443 --> 01:05:50.246 align:start size:84% position:16%
aspect of what
drives achievement.

01:05:51.547 --> 01:05:53.616 align:start position:0%
But some of the course
corrections that I've made later

01:05:53.616 --> 01:05:57.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
in life were really motivated
by trying to figure out

01:05:58.554 --> 01:06:00.623
what drives me as opposed
to trying to do the thing

01:06:00.623 --> 01:06:03.326
that's gonna get
me the accolades

01:06:03.326 --> 01:06:06.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
or the approval of
people that I care about.

01:06:06.829 --> 01:06:10.032
And that's still a struggle
and a growth thing, you know?

01:06:10.032 --> 01:06:14.003
- Yeah, it reminds me of...

01:06:14.003 --> 01:06:17.106 align:start position:0%
I went to this class day speech
the year before I graduated

01:06:17.106 --> 01:06:20.776
at Dartmouth and it was like,
here's five pieces of advice.

01:06:20.776 --> 01:06:23.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
And one of them was like,
your time in the frat basement

01:06:23.312 --> 01:06:25.614
is not a waste of time,
like some of those things

01:06:25.614 --> 01:06:27.817
that are just like
the gray, you know-

01:06:27.817 --> 01:06:29.118
- I spent a little bit of time

01:06:29.118 --> 01:06:30.886 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the frat basements
at Dartmouth.

01:06:32.088 --> 01:06:33.322
- You know, that's
how I met Jeremy.

01:06:33.322 --> 01:06:35.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's how I met
the love of my life.

01:06:35.891 --> 01:06:38.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
- With the journals along
the wall down there?

01:06:38.494 --> 01:06:43.499 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Just the worst, right?
- It's unbelievable.

01:06:44.233 --> 01:06:45.267
- It's where it's an....

01:06:47.169 --> 01:06:51.407 align:start size:84% position:16%
But one of the things
he said was that

01:06:51.407 --> 01:06:54.510 align:start size:97% position:3%
as long as you're not doing
something actively bad

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:59.148
in the world like working for
a company that hurts people,

01:06:59.148 --> 01:07:01.617
you know, as long as
you're not doing bad,

01:07:01.617 --> 01:07:03.352
you're probably doing good.

01:07:03.352 --> 01:07:07.023
And what he was addressing, I
think was that so many of us

01:07:07.023 --> 01:07:10.126 align:start size:97% position:3%
in college were like, we
should work for a nonprofit,

01:07:10.126 --> 01:07:11.360
we should go into
the Peace Corps.

01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:12.862 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like there were certain
pillars that meant

01:07:12.862 --> 01:07:14.430 align:start size:81% position:19%
you were doing
good in the world.

01:07:14.430 --> 01:07:16.465
But what he was trying to say

01:07:16.465 --> 01:07:19.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
was that as long as
you are not doing bad,

01:07:19.769 --> 01:07:22.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
as long as you're manifesting
the greatest version

01:07:22.371 --> 01:07:25.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
of yourself, you're probably
doing good in the world.

01:07:25.775 --> 01:07:27.710 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that was really
helpful to hear

01:07:27.710 --> 01:07:30.579
because I was about to
go chase an Olympic dream

01:07:30.579 --> 01:07:34.984
which felt very
selfish, exactly.

01:07:34.984 --> 01:07:38.821
And even some of this
creative stuff, I mean,

01:07:38.821 --> 01:07:41.323
it's a team effort, but
I'm pursuing the arts.

01:07:41.323 --> 01:07:44.660 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think to hear
that meant a lot to me,

01:07:44.660 --> 01:07:48.831
'cause it meant that simply
following my North stars

01:07:48.831 --> 01:07:53.836 align:start size:97% position:3%
would do good in the world
even if it's not the same idea

01:07:54.870 --> 01:07:56.372
of good-
- Right.

01:07:56.372 --> 01:07:59.341
Like flipping the lens such
that you're not looking at it

01:07:59.341 --> 01:08:02.645 align:start size:97% position:3%
as indulgent but actually
like the world needs everybody

01:08:02.645 --> 01:08:04.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
to be the best version
of who they are.

01:08:04.547 --> 01:08:05.781
Like we need a little bit more

01:08:05.781 --> 01:08:07.817
of that messaging
out there, I think.

01:08:07.817 --> 01:08:11.454
- Yeah, and that was important

01:08:11.454 --> 01:08:13.222
'cause it can feel a little,

01:08:14.557 --> 01:08:18.027 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think some dream
chasers can feel selfish.

01:08:18.027 --> 01:08:21.097
And I think it's only selfish
if it's not your real dream

01:08:21.097 --> 01:08:22.965
or if you're not committed.

01:08:22.965 --> 01:08:26.535
It's only not great if you
don't commit all the way.

01:08:27.970 --> 01:08:32.007
- So you end up committing
all the way kind of, right?

01:08:32.007 --> 01:08:33.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause you're
doing other things,

01:08:33.175 --> 01:08:37.713
but I'm interested in that like,

01:08:37.713 --> 01:08:40.816
was there an inflection point
in your running at Dartmouth

01:08:40.816 --> 01:08:43.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
where it all
started to click in?

01:08:43.352 --> 01:08:45.287
Like you go from
being the slowest,

01:08:45.287 --> 01:08:48.224
I mean, by the time
you graduate, you're
killing it, right?

01:08:48.224 --> 01:08:49.992 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, you're
progressing as you go.

01:08:49.992 --> 01:08:53.395
Was there a moment where
it all connected for you

01:08:53.395 --> 01:08:57.066
or was it just a gradual growth?

01:08:57.066 --> 01:08:58.634
- It was gradual.

01:08:58.634 --> 01:09:00.936
It appears more sudden

01:09:00.936 --> 01:09:05.941 align:start size:97% position:3%
because junior year I
started scoring team points.

01:09:07.376 --> 01:09:09.411
It kind of seems like it was
sudden when I was a senior

01:09:09.411 --> 01:09:12.314 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the truth is that the
reality is that it's gradual.

01:09:12.314 --> 01:09:15.851
And I think that's
how all dreams are.

01:09:15.851 --> 01:09:18.587 align:start size:97% position:3%
They happen very, very
slowly and then all at once,

01:09:18.587 --> 01:09:20.990
and most people only see
the all at once moment.

01:09:22.291 --> 01:09:26.996
It was, you know, I
added practically,

01:09:26.996 --> 01:09:28.364
I mean this is what
actually happened.

01:09:28.364 --> 01:09:31.700
I added 10 miles a week
to my mileage every year

01:09:31.700 --> 01:09:34.470
for four or five years, it
was just 10 miles a week.

01:09:34.470 --> 01:09:37.673
So I was like 30 miles a
week, 40 as a sophomore,

01:09:37.673 --> 01:09:40.943
50 as a junior, 60 as a
senior, 70 miles a week-

01:09:40.943 --> 01:09:42.778
- These are tiny
adjustments really.

01:09:42.778 --> 01:09:45.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Just tiny and I
stayed healthy, right?

01:09:45.915 --> 01:09:50.920
And then shifts that
I always tried to see

01:09:51.787 --> 01:09:54.723
as choices not sacrifices,

01:09:54.723 --> 01:09:57.159 align:start size:84% position:16%
I drank a lot of
beer my freshman year

01:09:57.159 --> 01:10:00.663
and I stopped drinking a lot
of beer my junior year, right?

01:10:00.663 --> 01:10:03.499
So certain choices that I made

01:10:03.499 --> 01:10:06.535
that were helping
my performance,

01:10:06.535 --> 01:10:09.071 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I probably wasn't
mature enough as a freshmen

01:10:09.071 --> 01:10:11.774
not to explore those things.

01:10:11.774 --> 01:10:14.543
Like that was what I
felt I needed to do.

01:10:15.811 --> 01:10:17.880
And it did help.

01:10:17.880 --> 01:10:20.049 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just think
information is helpful.

01:10:20.049 --> 01:10:21.917
Like I remember someone
came and talked to us

01:10:21.917 --> 01:10:24.286 align:start size:88% position:13%
and he was like a heavy
night of drinking,

01:10:24.286 --> 01:10:26.188
he was like a pro athlete,

01:10:26.188 --> 01:10:28.657
sets you back two
weeks in training.

01:10:28.657 --> 01:10:31.093 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that simple equation
made it a lot easier

01:10:31.093 --> 01:10:33.996
for me to make those
decisions to be honest,

01:10:33.996 --> 01:10:36.732 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause it wasn't this
abstract, like drinking's bad.

01:10:36.732 --> 01:10:38.300
I was like, how bad is it?

01:10:38.300 --> 01:10:40.769
And he's like, two weeks
bad. And I'm like, okay.

01:10:40.769 --> 01:10:44.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if I value my time
and I don't wanna go back

01:10:44.039 --> 01:10:47.843
two weeks in training,
I won't drink that much.

01:10:47.843 --> 01:10:49.578 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, it doesn't
mean never drink.

01:10:49.578 --> 01:10:51.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a time and a place
where you wouldn't care

01:10:51.180 --> 01:10:53.249
about the last two
weeks of training

01:10:53.249 --> 01:10:54.717
like at the end of the season.

01:10:56.285 --> 01:10:57.720
Does that make sense?

01:10:57.720 --> 01:11:00.789 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it was like little
decisions that then added up.

01:11:00.789 --> 01:11:04.093 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think having Mark
Coogan become my coach.

01:11:04.093 --> 01:11:07.796 align:start size:94% position:6%
He also gave me permission
to believe in myself

01:11:07.796 --> 01:11:10.366
and it came from
the right place.

01:11:10.366 --> 01:11:13.969
It was different for my dad
to say, "Lex you can it,"

01:11:13.969 --> 01:11:15.738
than to hear it
from an Olympian.

01:11:17.306 --> 01:11:20.009
That's powerful and I think
that's back to the question

01:11:20.009 --> 01:11:22.478
I asked you of how much
does it matter to you

01:11:22.478 --> 01:11:25.314
that people you revere
and admire tell you,

01:11:25.314 --> 01:11:27.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
I give you permission
to chase this-

01:11:27.650 --> 01:11:28.851
- well, it's everything.

01:11:29.985 --> 01:11:33.155 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's everything and
that idea of mentorship

01:11:33.155 --> 01:11:36.292
or having those kinds of
heroes is like a recurring

01:11:36.292 --> 01:11:38.460
huge theme in your book.

01:11:38.460 --> 01:11:41.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, but you seek them
out like a fisherman.

01:11:41.830 --> 01:11:44.733 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like I feel that some
people think these mentors

01:11:44.733 --> 01:11:46.035
are just gonna come to you.

01:11:46.035 --> 01:11:48.304
And sometimes they do
in the form of a coach

01:11:48.304 --> 01:11:51.507
that you happen upon

01:11:51.507 --> 01:11:56.478
but oftentimes we have to grab
the mentor and allow them in.

01:11:57.980 --> 01:12:00.015
- And it is true like
when that one person

01:12:00.015 --> 01:12:01.483
that you have so
much respect for

01:12:01.483 --> 01:12:06.221
just says something that
maybe a week from then

01:12:06.221 --> 01:12:09.725
they forget about but makes all
the difference in your life.

01:12:09.725 --> 01:12:13.395
- Yes, and that also goes
to what I say in the world

01:12:13.395 --> 01:12:16.432 align:start size:84% position:16%
because now people
have their eyes on me.

01:12:16.432 --> 01:12:18.167
So I need to-

01:12:18.167 --> 01:12:19.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
- All those braveries
running around

01:12:19.968 --> 01:12:22.137
with buns on top of their head.

01:12:22.137 --> 01:12:23.339
- I love, aaw.

01:12:24.940 --> 01:12:26.675
Some buns maybe, you know,

01:12:26.675 --> 01:12:29.978 align:start position:0%
some of them are boys and
maybe they don't have a button,

01:12:29.978 --> 01:12:31.246
maybe they do, but yeah.

01:12:31.246 --> 01:12:36.118
- It's the most adorable
tribe of fans out there.

01:12:37.353 --> 01:12:38.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Thank you.
- Yeah, it's really cool.

01:12:38.854 --> 01:12:42.257
And how you communicate with
them with verse and poetry

01:12:42.257 --> 01:12:44.893
and the inspiration that you
share I think is really cool

01:12:44.893 --> 01:12:47.563
and so uniquely you.

01:12:47.563 --> 01:12:48.764
- Thank you.

01:12:48.764 --> 01:12:51.900
I'm trying to give them
something to imitate

01:12:51.900 --> 01:12:56.405
or to latch onto that I feel
comfortable with them imitating

01:12:56.405 --> 01:12:59.608
or would have hoped that
I could have latched onto

01:12:59.608 --> 01:13:01.710
when I was there in their shoes

01:13:03.045 --> 01:13:05.481 align:start size:88% position:13%
because I don't think
it's that useful to say,

01:13:05.481 --> 01:13:08.984 align:start size:97% position:3%
I just ran 120 miles a
week at this minute average

01:13:08.984 --> 01:13:12.488 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause that's not that
useful to a 15-year old girl.

01:13:12.488 --> 01:13:14.723
Like they don't need
to run that many miles.

01:13:15.824 --> 01:13:18.327 align:start size:84% position:16%
But they can put
their hair up like me

01:13:18.327 --> 01:13:21.230
or they can go on a long run,
whatever that means to them.

01:13:22.398 --> 01:13:25.334
- Or they can dare
to have a big dream.

01:13:25.334 --> 01:13:26.902
- They can dare to
have a big dream.

01:13:26.902 --> 01:13:31.607
And I'm someone who will take
your dreams very seriously.

01:13:31.607 --> 01:13:34.309
So like with my friends,

01:13:34.309 --> 01:13:38.046 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't think I'm the person
to call and like cry to

01:13:38.046 --> 01:13:42.351
because usually I just
will try to navigate

01:13:42.351 --> 01:13:45.687 align:start size:97% position:3%
and figure out what the next-
- Solution.

01:13:45.687 --> 01:13:49.858
- Yeah, and yeah.

01:13:52.227 --> 01:13:55.130
- So tell the story of how
the bravey thing started?

01:13:55.130 --> 01:14:00.135 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, so bravey started
when I wrote a poem

01:14:01.303 --> 01:14:04.339
on social media, which
was run like a bravery,

01:14:04.339 --> 01:14:07.576
sleep like a baby,
dream like a crazy,

01:14:07.576 --> 01:14:10.078
replace can't with maybe.

01:14:10.078 --> 01:14:14.149
And bravery was this word,
came from the word brave,

01:14:14.149 --> 01:14:15.551
but not a real word,

01:14:15.551 --> 01:14:20.189 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think it stuck
because so many of the words

01:14:21.256 --> 01:14:23.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I chased
growing up felt very,

01:14:24.393 --> 01:14:26.328
they felt very outward facing.

01:14:26.328 --> 01:14:30.466
So, like strong, pretty, fierce,

01:14:30.466 --> 01:14:34.536
those felt like words that
I presented to the world

01:14:34.536 --> 01:14:37.372
and bravey because
it's not a real word,

01:14:37.372 --> 01:14:40.609
because it comes from the word
brave felt more like a choice

01:14:40.609 --> 01:14:44.079
like an inward facing word
that you assigned to yourself

01:14:44.079 --> 01:14:47.483 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that you decide exactly
what that means to you

01:14:47.483 --> 01:14:48.984
and I liked that.

01:14:48.984 --> 01:14:52.321 align:start size:88% position:13%
I liked giving the
world something,
giving myself something

01:14:52.321 --> 01:14:54.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I could simply
choose to be.

01:14:54.923 --> 01:14:57.893 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And it's also not telling
you that you need to be brave.

01:14:57.893 --> 01:14:59.628
You just need to be bravey.

01:14:59.628 --> 01:15:02.865
Like you kind of just to be
in the orbit of brave, right?

01:15:02.865 --> 01:15:05.701
It's very inviting
and nonthreating.

01:15:05.701 --> 01:15:07.369
- Yes, I think so, too, right.

01:15:07.369 --> 01:15:10.806 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause you're just making
choices to chase your dreams

01:15:10.806 --> 01:15:15.811 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it has a lightness
to it I think.

01:15:17.212 --> 01:15:20.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
And hopefully it's playful
because dream chasing,

01:15:20.883 --> 01:15:23.385
dream chasing is hard
enough that I think

01:15:24.253 --> 01:15:26.355
finding some lightness is good.

01:15:26.355 --> 01:15:27.656
- Right, right.

01:15:29.391 --> 01:15:31.093
And it becomes like a thing.

01:15:31.093 --> 01:15:34.563 align:start position:0%
This brave thing explodes,
becomes the name of your tribe.

01:15:34.563 --> 01:15:36.265 align:start size:81% position:19%
It becomes the
name of your book.

01:15:37.699 --> 01:15:42.404
- Yeah, well, that I give
my editors credit for it

01:15:43.972 --> 01:15:46.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause I was like we can't
call the book "Bravey".

01:15:46.275 --> 01:15:47.776
No one knows what this is

01:15:47.776 --> 01:15:50.746
and they're like, they will.
- That's more reason to do it.

01:15:50.746 --> 01:15:52.915
- They're like, they will.
- Like "Bravey" what is this?

01:15:52.915 --> 01:15:54.783
- What is it?
- Tell me more.

01:15:54.783 --> 01:15:59.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah, I'm grateful to
put something in the world

01:15:59.588 --> 01:16:02.524 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I feel is
adding to the world.

01:16:02.524 --> 01:16:05.494
You know, I think
it's a good tool.

01:16:05.494 --> 01:16:09.565
And I think it's a good
lesson to tell yourself

01:16:09.565 --> 01:16:11.934 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you can just decide
that you're something

01:16:13.468 --> 01:16:15.070
and that it makes
it more inevitable.

01:16:15.070 --> 01:16:18.006
Like when Jeremy and I
have a goal or a dream,

01:16:18.006 --> 01:16:20.876 align:start size:97% position:3%
we usually talk about it as
if it's real before it's real.

01:16:20.876 --> 01:16:23.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
Whether it's, we
named our pug Bernini

01:16:23.378 --> 01:16:25.347 align:start size:88% position:13%
before she even existed,
we were just like,

01:16:25.347 --> 01:16:27.449 align:start size:84% position:16%
when we get Bernini,
Bernini, Bernini,

01:16:27.449 --> 01:16:30.619
and then we get a pug,
but I think it's the same

01:16:30.619 --> 01:16:34.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
with any dream we're chasing,
where like, talk about it

01:16:34.089 --> 01:16:37.092
as if it's real and
put a label on it.

01:16:37.092 --> 01:16:38.961
And then the world and you will-

01:16:40.395 --> 01:16:43.065
- Right, everything tends
to then coalesce around it.

01:16:43.065 --> 01:16:44.533
I think that's really powerful.

01:16:44.533 --> 01:16:46.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, anybody who's made
an independent feature

01:16:46.902 --> 01:16:47.903
will tell you the same.

01:16:47.903 --> 01:16:49.271
Like you just start making it,

01:16:49.271 --> 01:16:51.607 align:start size:88% position:13%
like you just assume
the money's gonna happen

01:16:51.607 --> 01:16:53.909
and all these element,
like all the variables,

01:16:53.909 --> 01:16:57.846
the eight billion variables
will somehow come together.

01:16:57.846 --> 01:16:59.014
But it starts with that belief

01:16:59.014 --> 01:17:00.515 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then the action
on the belief.

01:17:00.515 --> 01:17:03.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like just start moving forward
as if it's all sorted out.

01:17:03.585 --> 01:17:08.590 align:start position:0%
- Yes, and you know, that's
the very opposite mindset of...

01:17:10.659 --> 01:17:13.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was just thinking about
like when I was sick,

01:17:13.328 --> 01:17:15.397
I thought I knew the future

01:17:15.397 --> 01:17:19.534
in a way that was
really unhealthy.

01:17:19.534 --> 01:17:21.770
'Cause I was like the
future, it will be bad.

01:17:21.770 --> 01:17:23.271
This will never get better.

01:17:23.271 --> 01:17:26.141
And I feel like the minute
we think we know exactly

01:17:26.141 --> 01:17:28.910
what's gonna happen, it's
a little bit of a shift

01:17:28.910 --> 01:17:32.447 align:start position:0%
of what you're just saying,
but that's actually a red flag.

01:17:32.447 --> 01:17:34.983 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like when you're like,
this will never get better.

01:17:36.451 --> 01:17:40.789
I think like a healthy mind
is one that's like, you know,

01:17:40.789 --> 01:17:44.026
these are inevitable goals,

01:17:44.026 --> 01:17:46.895
but I don't really know
how it's gonna happen

01:17:46.895 --> 01:17:48.296 align:start size:81% position:19%
or what, you know-
- Right.

01:17:48.296 --> 01:17:51.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
But not letting the
unknowing prevent you from-

01:17:51.299 --> 01:17:54.936 align:start size:97% position:3%
- From chasing, yeah.
- Yeah, from chasing it, yeah.

01:17:54.936 --> 01:17:59.608
So, on the bravery poem thing,

01:17:59.608 --> 01:18:02.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is not like a one-off
like you're a poet,

01:18:02.044 --> 01:18:04.813 align:start size:84% position:16%
like you study
poetry at Dartmouth-

01:18:04.813 --> 01:18:08.350
- I graduated at the top of
my creative writing class

01:18:08.350 --> 01:18:12.354
and that took a lot of writing.

01:18:12.354 --> 01:18:15.457 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I would imagine
it's reflected in your book.

01:18:15.457 --> 01:18:18.060
I mean, one of the things
that I love about the way

01:18:18.060 --> 01:18:20.262
that you wrote this book,

01:18:20.262 --> 01:18:25.267
you know, most athlete
memoirs are terrible.

01:18:26.368 --> 01:18:29.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's generally
because, in my opinion,

01:18:29.137 --> 01:18:32.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they're
written by an athlete

01:18:33.475 --> 01:18:35.277
typically with a ghost writer

01:18:35.277 --> 01:18:37.746
near or at the end
of their career.

01:18:37.746 --> 01:18:39.815
And they're used as
like marketing tools

01:18:39.815 --> 01:18:41.950 align:start size:88% position:13%
to extend the longevity
of that career

01:18:41.950 --> 01:18:44.619 align:start size:88% position:13%
or to try to create
interest in this person

01:18:44.619 --> 01:18:46.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
for sponsorship
reasons or whatnot.

01:18:48.156 --> 01:18:51.126
And they're like overly heroic

01:18:51.126 --> 01:18:53.762 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they just feel
not dishonest,

01:18:53.762 --> 01:18:56.431
but not terribly honest either.

01:18:56.431 --> 01:18:58.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's the rare
person who can execute

01:18:58.633 --> 01:19:03.038
on an athlete memoir with
a level of vulnerability

01:19:03.038 --> 01:19:07.242 align:start size:97% position:3%
and like emotional awareness
that you have done here.

01:19:07.242 --> 01:19:10.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
And with all these writing
chops that you have,

01:19:10.512 --> 01:19:13.081 align:start size:88% position:13%
it just makes for a
very potent combination.

01:19:13.081 --> 01:19:17.185 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I love the way
that it's very lyrical

01:19:17.185 --> 01:19:19.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the way that it's
written in that, you know,

01:19:19.688 --> 01:19:20.889
there's a linear story

01:19:20.889 --> 01:19:22.924 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it's not really
about the linear story.

01:19:22.924 --> 01:19:27.162
Like you've extracted
out the most emotional

01:19:28.597 --> 01:19:32.601 align:start position:0%
important moments and described
those kind of, you know,

01:19:32.601 --> 01:19:35.103 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's more like a string
of pearls than it is like,

01:19:35.103 --> 01:19:37.773 align:start size:97% position:3%
this happened, then this
happened, then this happened,

01:19:37.773 --> 01:19:40.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
and just the honesty
and the humor

01:19:40.842 --> 01:19:45.847
and the level of self-awareness
I think is really beautiful.

01:19:47.015 --> 01:19:48.550
- Thank you. That's
that means a lot.

01:19:48.550 --> 01:19:51.653
And I know you know your stuff.

01:19:51.653 --> 01:19:55.390
- I'm like well, as somebody
who's written a book like this,

01:19:55.390 --> 01:19:57.492
like I was embarrassed for
myself reading this book

01:19:57.492 --> 01:20:00.862
'cause I was like, this book
is so much better than my book.

01:20:00.862 --> 01:20:02.197
I was like, I should
have done it like that.

01:20:02.197 --> 01:20:04.966
Like, why did I do this?

01:20:04.966 --> 01:20:06.968
Anyway, it's quite
an accomplishment.

01:20:06.968 --> 01:20:08.403 align:start size:81% position:19%
- I really, really
appreciate that

01:20:08.403 --> 01:20:11.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it's definitely the
proudest thing I've ever done.

01:20:13.308 --> 01:20:17.412
I feel like, man, it's fun
when you've done something

01:20:17.412 --> 01:20:19.581
and it's about to be
released in the world

01:20:19.581 --> 01:20:22.117
and you know that
you did your best.

01:20:22.117 --> 01:20:25.153 align:start size:97% position:3%
I really liked that feeling.
- It's really exciting

01:20:25.153 --> 01:20:26.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's very exciting
and it's like a real thing

01:20:26.388 --> 01:20:27.823
that exists in the world.

01:20:27.823 --> 01:20:29.291
- Yeah, it's a tangible thing.

01:20:29.291 --> 01:20:32.427 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it's, and we're
in, I'm not...

01:20:32.427 --> 01:20:34.563 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's funny when you're
like, your editors are like,

01:20:34.563 --> 01:20:37.599 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's no more changes and
you're like, okay, I accept it

01:20:37.599 --> 01:20:40.235
that this is it.

01:20:41.403 --> 01:20:44.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
And Tara Schuster
was one of the blurbs

01:20:44.472 --> 01:20:47.709
and she said that it
felt like a kind knife.

01:20:47.709 --> 01:20:51.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I thought that was like
a really big compliment

01:20:51.613 --> 01:20:54.482
because I think what people
are expecting in this book

01:20:54.482 --> 01:20:59.421
is like more poems and
encouraging somethings

01:21:00.455 --> 01:21:01.990
and those are in there,

01:21:01.990 --> 01:21:04.626
but those somethings came
from something really dark

01:21:04.626 --> 01:21:06.394
and really challenging.

01:21:06.394 --> 01:21:10.932
And I think that what she said
and what you said are really,

01:21:12.334 --> 01:21:16.805
really mean a lot to me because
it means that I communicated

01:21:16.805 --> 01:21:19.307
and it's one thing
to have an experience

01:21:19.307 --> 01:21:21.309
because I didn't need to
write this book honestly

01:21:21.309 --> 01:21:23.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
for myself, I have
had these experiences.

01:21:23.645 --> 01:21:25.747
I'm very happy with where I am.

01:21:25.747 --> 01:21:28.917
But I really wanted
to work hard enough

01:21:28.917 --> 01:21:31.553
and find a way to
communicate them.

01:21:31.553 --> 01:21:33.054
And that's a whole other world.

01:21:33.054 --> 01:21:36.391
It's just putting it in
a way where it translates

01:21:36.391 --> 01:21:40.295
and it speaks to people who
don't know you possibly.

01:21:40.295 --> 01:21:42.731
- Right, and you don't...

01:21:42.731 --> 01:21:45.967
It is an athlete memoir but
it's really not about that.

01:21:47.402 --> 01:21:49.537
It's just about life, you know?

01:21:49.537 --> 01:21:51.373 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you don't shy away
from the darkness.

01:21:51.373 --> 01:21:54.276
I mean, there's some really
difficult stuff in there.

01:21:54.276 --> 01:21:56.678
You know, one of your
memories of your mom

01:21:59.014 --> 01:22:00.649
it was really painful to read.

01:22:01.950 --> 01:22:04.586 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It was painful-
- Yeah, I can't imagine.

01:22:04.586 --> 01:22:05.520
I can't imagine.

01:22:07.255 --> 01:22:09.691
- It was so crazy.

01:22:09.691 --> 01:22:13.461
Some of that stuff it's like,

01:22:13.461 --> 01:22:14.763
I don't know if you can relate,

01:22:14.763 --> 01:22:16.464
but when you're
writing a memoir,

01:22:16.464 --> 01:22:19.501
you're like, wow,
is my life worth?

01:22:19.501 --> 01:22:21.903
Is it like, you know,

01:22:21.903 --> 01:22:23.939 align:start size:97% position:3%
it means that you thought-
- We'll talk about indulgence,

01:22:23.939 --> 01:22:25.774
you're like, really?
- Yeah, you're like...

01:22:25.774 --> 01:22:27.275
(both laughing)

01:22:27.275 --> 01:22:31.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like you thought you
were worthy of writing this.

01:22:31.179 --> 01:22:35.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like that takes a
certain gusto I think

01:22:35.083 --> 01:22:37.085 align:start size:84% position:16%
just like chasing a
dream and you're like,

01:22:37.085 --> 01:22:38.653
I think I can do this,

01:22:39.688 --> 01:22:43.892
but yeah, I just...

01:22:45.327 --> 01:22:50.265
I think I've like been to
the peaks of of some worlds

01:22:50.265 --> 01:22:52.667 align:start size:81% position:19%
and to the bottoms
of some worlds

01:22:52.667 --> 01:22:56.237 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that means that hopefully
I can reach the people

01:22:56.237 --> 01:23:00.475
in between and that's
a unique role to be in

01:23:00.475 --> 01:23:05.447
because I know how much it
meant when certain people

01:23:06.314 --> 01:23:07.882
said certain things to me,

01:23:07.882 --> 01:23:11.453
it went 100 times further than
if somebody else had said it.

01:23:11.453 --> 01:23:14.322
And we've said this in
this conversation before

01:23:14.322 --> 01:23:19.327
but I recognize I was in a
position to say something

01:23:20.762 --> 01:23:23.164
and that the more detail I
could go into at this book,

01:23:23.164 --> 01:23:26.434
like the more specific I would
be, the more it would reach.

01:23:26.434 --> 01:23:29.104
Isn't that the rule
of storytelling?

01:23:29.104 --> 01:23:30.538
The more specific we can get

01:23:30.538 --> 01:23:32.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the more personal?
- The most specific

01:23:32.073 --> 01:23:34.676
but also the more honest and
the more vulnerable, right?

01:23:34.676 --> 01:23:38.913
Because that's where
people can really find

01:23:38.913 --> 01:23:40.382
the emotional connection.

01:23:40.382 --> 01:23:43.318
- Yeah, well, you know what's
funny about this last week

01:23:43.318 --> 01:23:45.086
with the New York
Times thing is,

01:23:45.086 --> 01:23:47.489
I feel like I'm at a
turning point right now

01:23:47.489 --> 01:23:52.494
where the truth is that I've
worked really hard to make sure

01:23:53.862 --> 01:23:57.732
that my film, my acting,
stands on its own pillar

01:23:57.732 --> 01:24:00.602
and my running stands
on its own pillar,

01:24:00.602 --> 01:24:05.407 align:start position:0%
and I actually finally feel
that I've let those worlds talk

01:24:05.407 --> 01:24:09.811
to each other in this book
in a way that might allow me,

01:24:09.811 --> 01:24:14.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I feel it, to just
be exactly who I am

01:24:14.682 --> 01:24:17.252
a little bit more comfortably,

01:24:17.252 --> 01:24:19.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think that all
comes from within, right?

01:24:19.954 --> 01:24:22.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like I'm in the Hollywood
world, but I still feel like,

01:24:22.791 --> 01:24:25.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
oh, I didn't come up like
all these actors did.

01:24:26.795 --> 01:24:30.031
I was spending a lot of time
performing on a circular track

01:24:30.031 --> 01:24:31.900
or oval track.

01:24:31.900 --> 01:24:34.836
And I think before I
might've seen myself

01:24:34.836 --> 01:24:39.574 align:start size:97% position:3%
as needing to hide one world
when I was in the other,

01:24:39.574 --> 01:24:44.446
and now I actually feel like
it's okay that I have come,

01:24:44.446 --> 01:24:46.514
that I am exactly who I am.

01:24:46.514 --> 01:24:48.650 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I wish I had
felt that way sooner,

01:24:48.650 --> 01:24:51.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think it's okay
that I feel that way now.

01:24:51.286 --> 01:24:52.654
- It's interesting
that you say that

01:24:52.654 --> 01:24:55.290
because your movies
are so reflective

01:24:56.624 --> 01:24:59.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your emotional
experience as an athlete.

01:25:00.862 --> 01:25:02.263
They do stand on their own,

01:25:02.263 --> 01:25:04.866 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they're also inextricably
linked to each other.

01:25:04.866 --> 01:25:08.703
- But that was a smart
decision as a filmmaker

01:25:08.703 --> 01:25:11.239
to tell a story in
a world that I'm in

01:25:11.239 --> 01:25:13.141
and know and have access to.

01:25:13.141 --> 01:25:17.278 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like that was a
business woman knowing,

01:25:17.278 --> 01:25:19.781
I mean, a creative person
knowing that the best story

01:25:19.781 --> 01:25:22.617
I can tell is my own
or the world I'm in.

01:25:23.785 --> 01:25:25.954 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I think all
filmmakers do that

01:25:25.954 --> 01:25:27.555
even if it's an
emotional reality

01:25:27.555 --> 01:25:30.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
they know not an
actual reality, right?

01:25:30.325 --> 01:25:33.928
So, I think that was still
my filmmaking cap on it.

01:25:33.928 --> 01:25:38.099
It wasn't me saying, I really
wish these worlds were one.

01:25:38.099 --> 01:25:41.803
It was just, what's the
best movie I can make,

01:25:41.803 --> 01:25:44.139
and that was the best
movie I could make.

01:25:44.139 --> 01:25:48.276 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right, because it's inspired
by your own experiences

01:25:48.276 --> 01:25:50.245 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's something that
you know very well.

01:25:50.245 --> 01:25:51.946
And it's interesting
in looking at the book

01:25:51.946 --> 01:25:54.015 align:start size:88% position:13%
like things that
actually happened to you

01:25:54.015 --> 01:25:58.620 align:start size:97% position:3%
are then translated into
some narrative that shows up

01:25:58.620 --> 01:26:01.322 align:start size:97% position:3%
in one of your movies through
one of the characters.

01:26:01.322 --> 01:26:05.627 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, I think a lot of
filmmakers are like that, right?

01:26:05.627 --> 01:26:07.829
A lot of like a
writer directors.

01:26:07.829 --> 01:26:10.031 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm sure. I'm sure.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:26:10.031 --> 01:26:11.633
So I'm just doing what they do.

01:26:11.633 --> 01:26:13.635
I'm doing what they do-
- Looking your book,

01:26:13.635 --> 01:26:14.469 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm like looking at the
people that blurbed your book

01:26:15.937 --> 01:26:20.775
and it's like, it's just this
weird Venn diagram of worlds.

01:26:21.643 --> 01:26:23.144
It's like Shalane Flanagan,

01:26:23.144 --> 01:26:24.412
and then like, wait, Jay
Duplass like Mindy Kaling,

01:26:24.412 --> 01:26:26.514
like what is going on here?

01:26:26.514 --> 01:26:31.152 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's wild that you have
your feet planted so firmly

01:26:31.152 --> 01:26:32.820
in these two different worlds

01:26:32.820 --> 01:26:35.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
and how they intersect
in this book.

01:26:35.690 --> 01:26:39.194
- And those people have been,
I mean, Jay as an example,

01:26:39.194 --> 01:26:41.162
you know that I met
him on a run here.

01:26:41.162 --> 01:26:43.298
Like I met him randomly.

01:26:43.298 --> 01:26:44.766
So he's a runner and-

01:26:44.766 --> 01:26:46.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
- See, I just figured
you guys are in some cool

01:26:46.901 --> 01:26:48.703
Hollywood group where
you all sit around

01:26:48.703 --> 01:26:51.206
and share each other's
screenplays or something.

01:26:51.206 --> 01:26:53.041 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Yeah, no, no.
- I'm sure.

01:26:53.041 --> 01:26:55.276
- But the truth is that I
met him while I was running

01:26:55.276 --> 01:26:58.580 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it was pouring rain
and he was running,

01:26:58.580 --> 01:27:02.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
we were both on long
runs and I recognized him

01:27:02.250 --> 01:27:04.385
and I just listened to his book.

01:27:04.385 --> 01:27:07.355
And I was running with a girl
who had no idea who he was.

01:27:07.355 --> 01:27:08.623
And I was like, we have to stop,

01:27:08.623 --> 01:27:09.824
I'm sorry, like I have to.

01:27:09.824 --> 01:27:11.759 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know those moments
where you're like,

01:27:11.759 --> 01:27:13.595
this will never happen again.

01:27:13.595 --> 01:27:16.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he knew who I was
because he knew Nick Kroll

01:27:16.397 --> 01:27:18.132
and he had seen
"Olympic Dreams",

01:27:19.734 --> 01:27:23.037
and I don't feel like this is
something that I try to make

01:27:23.037 --> 01:27:24.572
the most of opportunities.

01:27:24.572 --> 01:27:27.475
And in that instance, I was
like, can I send you a link

01:27:27.475 --> 01:27:29.444
to our movie or whatever.

01:27:29.444 --> 01:27:33.381 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we eventually got lunch
and he's a mentor now.

01:27:33.381 --> 01:27:38.119 align:start size:81% position:19%
But that was, he's
someone who...

01:27:38.119 --> 01:27:40.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are a few people who
I think like understand

01:27:40.488 --> 01:27:44.492
both worlds and who are
helping me see myself

01:27:44.492 --> 01:27:47.996 align:start size:88% position:13%
in their world, and
that means a lot to me.

01:27:47.996 --> 01:27:51.065
- That's cool. That's very cool.

01:27:51.065 --> 01:27:53.568
- But the point of that I
think is just that like,

01:27:54.602 --> 01:27:57.171
I'm very grateful to that,

01:27:57.171 --> 01:27:59.307
to the people who
do that for me.

01:28:00.708 --> 01:28:03.511
- Well, you're your
awareness of that,

01:28:03.511 --> 01:28:05.346
your consciousness
of that is reflected

01:28:05.346 --> 01:28:08.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
in how you communicate
to your audience.

01:28:08.283 --> 01:28:11.419
Like, I can tell that
you're very mindful

01:28:11.419 --> 01:28:13.921 align:start size:94% position:6%
of making sure that you're
fulfilling that role

01:28:13.921 --> 01:28:16.758
for the people that are
out there looking for you

01:28:16.758 --> 01:28:18.760
to give them a lead.

01:28:18.760 --> 01:28:20.962
- Hopefully I hope it,

01:28:20.962 --> 01:28:23.798 align:start size:94% position:6%
I hope the book also gives
a more realistic view

01:28:23.798 --> 01:28:25.099
of how it happens.

01:28:26.467 --> 01:28:28.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because if you see someone
just where they are

01:28:28.770 --> 01:28:32.473
in any one moment today,
you probably would assume

01:28:32.473 --> 01:28:34.275 align:start size:84% position:16%
any number of paths
got them there

01:28:34.275 --> 01:28:36.844 align:start size:88% position:13%
that aren't the real
way that got them there.

01:28:36.844 --> 01:28:40.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And when you're an
Olympian, that's on steroids.

01:28:40.114 --> 01:28:43.051 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Whatever it is-
- We just think that Olympians

01:28:43.051 --> 01:28:46.554
they just come out of the womb
fully baked as the superhero.

01:28:46.554 --> 01:28:51.559 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, and it's usually more
circuitous or unexpected.

01:28:53.928 --> 01:28:56.364
But what message does
that send to kids?

01:28:56.364 --> 01:28:58.199
And is it like don't
chase the Olympic dream

01:28:58.199 --> 01:29:00.902
or just like chase
the Olympic dream

01:29:00.902 --> 01:29:03.805
but make sure you're also
manifesting your full self.

01:29:03.805 --> 01:29:05.306
What are we telling them?

01:29:05.306 --> 01:29:07.241
- Well, I don't know, you tell
me what you're telling them.

01:29:07.241 --> 01:29:08.643
You're the one who's
doing the telling.

01:29:08.643 --> 01:29:12.213 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, I would say that
if I had to translate

01:29:12.213 --> 01:29:14.015
what you're trying
to put out there,

01:29:15.650 --> 01:29:18.052
it's saying, it's okay
to have a big dream.

01:29:18.052 --> 01:29:20.922 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's okay to feel different,
understand that it's hard

01:29:20.922 --> 01:29:24.659
anything worth doing
or doing really well

01:29:24.659 --> 01:29:29.564
is gonna be difficult, but
embrace those challenges,

01:29:29.564 --> 01:29:32.967
like be brave, like in
everything that you do,

01:29:32.967 --> 01:29:36.070
bravery is the recurring mantra.

01:29:36.070 --> 01:29:37.839
I don't know how many times
it's said in your movies

01:29:37.839 --> 01:29:41.075
and it's the title of your
book, like this is the thing.

01:29:41.075 --> 01:29:46.080
And bravery is to, you
know, exercise courage

01:29:47.949 --> 01:29:48.916
in the face of fear.

01:29:48.916 --> 01:29:50.551
It's not about being afraid,

01:29:50.551 --> 01:29:55.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's about having the
willingness to, you know,

01:29:55.123 --> 01:29:57.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
deal with the fear, right?
- Try your best.

01:29:57.258 --> 01:30:00.762
Yeah, yeah. I think it's
about trying your best.

01:30:00.762 --> 01:30:02.563
So maybe that's also it that

01:30:03.765 --> 01:30:05.032
as long as you're
trying your best,

01:30:05.032 --> 01:30:07.001
it won't go as you
thought it would,

01:30:07.001 --> 01:30:09.404
but you keep trying your best.

01:30:09.404 --> 01:30:11.406
And it is brave to try your best

01:30:11.406 --> 01:30:14.942 align:start size:88% position:13%
because trying your
best means if you fail,

01:30:14.942 --> 01:30:16.844 align:start size:84% position:16%
you'll know that you
did try your best

01:30:16.844 --> 01:30:20.715 align:start size:97% position:3%
whereas sandbagging is
like where you purposefully

01:30:20.715 --> 01:30:22.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
don't try your
best so that later,

01:30:22.283 --> 01:30:24.719 align:start size:84% position:16%
you can tell yourself
that narrative.

01:30:24.719 --> 01:30:28.089
So perhaps the bravest thing
of all is to try your best

01:30:28.089 --> 01:30:30.525 align:start size:84% position:16%
because then whatever
happens to you,

01:30:30.525 --> 01:30:33.261
you know was like
your honest effort.

01:30:33.261 --> 01:30:37.899
- Right, and to try your best
requires you to be vulnerable.

01:30:37.899 --> 01:30:41.369 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like when you're sandbagging,
you're hiding, right?

01:30:41.369 --> 01:30:42.804
You're indulging that fear

01:30:42.804 --> 01:30:45.907
and you're giving
yourself an out

01:30:45.907 --> 01:30:49.210
and it's scary to say, I'm
gonna remove all the excuses

01:30:49.210 --> 01:30:51.946 align:start size:97% position:3%
and put it all out there and
whatever happens happens.

01:30:51.946 --> 01:30:53.247
- Yeah, yeah.

01:30:55.416 --> 01:30:57.718 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, I had a question
that just came up

01:30:57.718 --> 01:31:00.188
that I wanted to ask
you, can I ask you?

01:31:00.188 --> 01:31:02.290
- You can, but this is
a podcast about you.

01:31:02.290 --> 01:31:03.591
- I know but it doesn't matter.

01:31:03.591 --> 01:31:05.092
It's always gonna come back.

01:31:05.092 --> 01:31:07.728
You're very good at making
it all the way to come back.

01:31:07.728 --> 01:31:12.567
Do you like, because I was
thinking about manifesting

01:31:12.567 --> 01:31:14.836
your greatest self and one of
the things is like the people

01:31:14.836 --> 01:31:16.337
you spend time around.

01:31:16.337 --> 01:31:20.541 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause it just occurred
to me that with your life,

01:31:20.541 --> 01:31:23.411 align:start position:0%
you've structured in a way
where you spend time with people

01:31:23.411 --> 01:31:25.780 align:start size:84% position:16%
in this context that
maybe you admire

01:31:25.780 --> 01:31:27.682
or that you'd like
to spend time with.

01:31:29.016 --> 01:31:32.854
Do you feel like you...

01:31:32.854 --> 01:31:37.458
Well, do you purposefully
put yourself in situations

01:31:37.458 --> 01:31:39.794
to be around people like that?

01:31:39.794 --> 01:31:44.065 align:start size:97% position:3%
And do you also take an
effort to not be around people

01:31:44.065 --> 01:31:46.868
who don't do that for you?

01:31:46.868 --> 01:31:48.402
Whatever that is.

01:31:48.402 --> 01:31:51.272
- Well, to the former point
100% or what do you think?

01:31:51.272 --> 01:31:55.243 align:start position:0%
This whole podcast is basically
a grand scheme to be able

01:31:55.243 --> 01:32:00.047 align:start size:97% position:3%
to hoodwink cool people
like yourself to spend time,

01:32:00.047 --> 01:32:01.349
like I hold them hostage here

01:32:01.349 --> 01:32:03.918 align:start size:88% position:13%
and force them to answer
all these questions.

01:32:03.918 --> 01:32:06.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then I incorporate
them into my life.

01:32:06.888 --> 01:32:10.258 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's very indulgent and
self-serving in that regard.

01:32:10.258 --> 01:32:12.460
I seek out people
that inspire me

01:32:12.460 --> 01:32:17.465 align:start position:0%
and I've given them a compelling
reason to come and meet me

01:32:18.866 --> 01:32:21.836 align:start size:94% position:6%
by amplifying and sharing
what they have to say

01:32:21.836 --> 01:32:25.206 align:start size:88% position:13%
and quite often like
these people then become

01:32:25.206 --> 01:32:26.440
people in my life.

01:32:26.440 --> 01:32:28.142
Like in the way
that you met Jay,

01:32:28.142 --> 01:32:30.411 align:start size:97% position:3%
like a lot of guests that
I've had are my friends now.

01:32:30.411 --> 01:32:32.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's the most
incredible gift

01:32:32.547 --> 01:32:35.716
because these are people
I can call on for advice

01:32:35.716 --> 01:32:38.553 align:start size:97% position:3%
or who have made
themselves available to me.

01:32:38.553 --> 01:32:40.354
And I don't take that lightly.

01:32:40.354 --> 01:32:41.689 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't take it
for granted at all.

01:32:41.689 --> 01:32:44.292
Like it's a huge, huge gift.

01:32:44.292 --> 01:32:48.362
And it's just been the
most expansive experience

01:32:48.362 --> 01:32:52.033
that I could've ever
structured for myself.

01:32:52.033 --> 01:32:56.437
To the point about avoiding,
you know, it's the adage is.

01:32:56.437 --> 01:32:59.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're the average of like
the however many people

01:32:59.240 --> 01:33:00.541
you spend the most
time with, right?

01:33:00.541 --> 01:33:03.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
So choose wisely,
like spend your time

01:33:03.044 --> 01:33:06.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
with high vibrating people
that are demonstrating

01:33:06.681 --> 01:33:09.383
characteristics that
you aspire to manifest

01:33:09.383 --> 01:33:10.618
in your own life.

01:33:10.618 --> 01:33:13.087
Of course that means
spending less time

01:33:13.087 --> 01:33:15.122
with people who are
dragging you down.

01:33:15.122 --> 01:33:17.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's a little bit
more difficult for me,

01:33:17.191 --> 01:33:22.196
but I feel like I've done a
pretty good job of over time

01:33:23.731 --> 01:33:26.534
setting healthier boundaries
around that kind of thing.

01:33:26.534 --> 01:33:28.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's cool, yeah.
- What about you?

01:33:29.904 --> 01:33:34.208
- Yes, I think that I'm doing
a better job of that as well

01:33:35.643 --> 01:33:40.114
but also recognizing that
I think separating the work

01:33:41.015 --> 01:33:42.750
from quality time in my life,

01:33:42.750 --> 01:33:46.253 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that includes, when Jeremy
and I, we work together,

01:33:46.253 --> 01:33:49.991 align:start size:97% position:3%
so separating our work time
from our quality time together

01:33:49.991 --> 01:33:53.160 align:start size:84% position:16%
but also understanding
in my own life

01:33:53.160 --> 01:33:55.963 align:start size:97% position:3%
like there are
circumstances where we must,

01:33:55.963 --> 01:33:58.132
we have colleagues,
we have interactions

01:33:58.132 --> 01:34:00.434
and hopefully those
are people we love,

01:34:00.434 --> 01:34:02.036
but there's sometimes
there's work, right?

01:34:02.036 --> 01:34:03.738 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- But, but making sure

01:34:03.738 --> 01:34:08.542 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I preserve the time
that I need to like reinflate

01:34:08.542 --> 01:34:13.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
or fill myself back up because
that will be important.

01:34:15.616 --> 01:34:19.687 align:start size:84% position:16%
- How does the athlete
mindset contribute

01:34:19.687 --> 01:34:22.957
to your creative pursuits?

01:34:22.957 --> 01:34:27.261
Like most people who
are kind of involved

01:34:27.261 --> 01:34:30.598
in that world, don't come
from an athlete background

01:34:30.598 --> 01:34:33.668 align:start size:97% position:3%
and certainly not as robust
an athlete background

01:34:33.668 --> 01:34:34.935
as you have.

01:34:34.935 --> 01:34:38.105
Do you feel like there
are tools and tactics

01:34:38.105 --> 01:34:42.309
that you've learned as a
runner that benefit you

01:34:42.309 --> 01:34:45.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
when it comes to breaking
a story for a script

01:34:45.179 --> 01:34:48.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
or executing on what
needs to be done

01:34:48.516 --> 01:34:50.451
to get this movie
up on its feet?

01:34:50.451 --> 01:34:52.119
- For sure, there's like...

01:34:52.119 --> 01:34:57.124
So let's just take the idea
of like writer's block, okay?

01:34:58.292 --> 01:35:00.795
So like people have
created this idea

01:35:00.795 --> 01:35:02.630
that there is such thing.

01:35:02.630 --> 01:35:06.634 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think that would be
like showing up to a workout,

01:35:06.634 --> 01:35:08.869 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I know you don't always
feel great every day,

01:35:08.869 --> 01:35:12.039
but it would be like being
like, I just can't even try.

01:35:12.039 --> 01:35:14.709
Like I can't even take a
step and it's like, okay,

01:35:14.709 --> 01:35:17.378
it might not go the way
you think it's gonna go

01:35:17.378 --> 01:35:20.614
but you can warm up and
you can do certain things

01:35:20.614 --> 01:35:23.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
that will lead you,
point you towards, sorry,

01:35:24.418 --> 01:35:25.619
you can do certain things

01:35:25.619 --> 01:35:28.189 align:start size:84% position:16%
that will point
you toward success.

01:35:28.189 --> 01:35:32.193
And as athletes we
put on our outfit

01:35:32.193 --> 01:35:33.627
that will set us up for success.

01:35:33.627 --> 01:35:35.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe we lay out our
clothes the night before,

01:35:35.896 --> 01:35:38.466
we eat the food we know is
gonna set us up for success.

01:35:38.466 --> 01:35:41.936
We go to the trail
that we know we love

01:35:41.936 --> 01:35:44.505 align:start size:84% position:16%
or go to the track,
we meet people.

01:35:44.505 --> 01:35:49.443
So there are things that
we put in place to show up

01:35:49.443 --> 01:35:52.813 align:start size:97% position:3%
and give ourselves the best
chance to try our best.

01:35:52.813 --> 01:35:55.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think with the
creative world, it's similar,

01:35:55.950 --> 01:35:58.753 align:start position:0%
like wear the outfit that makes
you feel like you can rise.

01:35:58.753 --> 01:36:01.322 align:start size:97% position:3%
Sit at the desk that you like,
use the pen that you like

01:36:01.322 --> 01:36:04.024
or computer, you know,
get the right lighting,

01:36:04.024 --> 01:36:06.360
like set it up for yourself.

01:36:06.360 --> 01:36:10.564
And I think that has been one
thing that I do a good job of

01:36:10.564 --> 01:36:13.734 align:start size:97% position:3%
is like curating the
environment to try my best.

01:36:13.734 --> 01:36:16.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
- To make it
conducive to success.

01:36:16.771 --> 01:36:19.940
But at the same time, the
big difference in my mind

01:36:19.940 --> 01:36:23.444
is that as an athlete,
it's much more binary,

01:36:23.444 --> 01:36:25.880
especially as an
endurance athlete.

01:36:25.880 --> 01:36:27.815
Okay, if I put in the
a hundred mile weeks

01:36:27.815 --> 01:36:29.283
and I do these things,

01:36:29.283 --> 01:36:33.420 align:start size:97% position:3%
like you're setting yourself
up for the best result.

01:36:33.420 --> 01:36:36.857 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, you know, it's
mathematical in some regards

01:36:36.857 --> 01:36:41.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
but creativity is
elusive and mysterious

01:36:41.629 --> 01:36:44.465 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it doesn't always
come when you summon it.

01:36:45.466 --> 01:36:49.937
- Yes, but I think that then,

01:36:49.937 --> 01:36:54.942
I think we can point
to the rule of thirds,

01:36:55.810 --> 01:36:56.744
which I was told by a coach.

01:36:56.744 --> 01:36:57.745
This is in the book

01:36:57.745 --> 01:36:59.313
but I think it's life-changing-

01:36:59.313 --> 01:37:01.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I love the rule of thirds.
- It's amazing.

01:37:01.282 --> 01:37:03.884
So my Olympic coach told me

01:37:03.884 --> 01:37:06.287
after a particularly
challenging workout

01:37:06.287 --> 01:37:07.721
where I could not hit my splits

01:37:07.721 --> 01:37:10.991 align:start size:97% position:3%
before going to the Rio
Olympics, that that was okay,

01:37:10.991 --> 01:37:12.159
it was the rule of thirds.

01:37:12.159 --> 01:37:13.360
And he was an Olympian,

01:37:13.360 --> 01:37:15.763
so I always soaked
in everything he said

01:37:15.763 --> 01:37:18.465 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I was like, what's
the rule of thirds?

01:37:18.465 --> 01:37:21.302 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he said, "when
you're chasing a dream

01:37:21.302 --> 01:37:22.870
or doing anything hard,

01:37:22.870 --> 01:37:26.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
you're meant to feel
good a third of the time,

01:37:26.240 --> 01:37:30.578
okay a third of the time, and
crappy a third of the time.

01:37:30.578 --> 01:37:33.380 align:start size:84% position:16%
And if the ratio is
roughly in that range,

01:37:33.380 --> 01:37:34.882
then you're doing fine.

01:37:34.882 --> 01:37:38.752
So today was the crappy day
along your dream chasing."

01:37:38.752 --> 01:37:41.488
And if the ratio is off like
you feel too good all the time

01:37:41.488 --> 01:37:45.659
or too bad, then you got to
look at if you're fatiguing

01:37:45.659 --> 01:37:47.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
or not trying hard enough
or pushing yourself.

01:37:47.761 --> 01:37:50.664
So I think with those days
that you're talking about

01:37:50.664 --> 01:37:54.001
where like creativity doesn't
come or it doesn't feel great,

01:37:54.001 --> 01:37:57.705 align:start size:97% position:3%
you still show up because
maybe that's your crappy day.

01:37:57.705 --> 01:37:59.940 align:start size:88% position:13%
But it doesn't mean
that you quit the goal.

01:37:59.940 --> 01:38:01.442
It doesn't mean you freak out.

01:38:01.442 --> 01:38:05.012 align:start size:97% position:3%
It means that you show up
and live through that crappy

01:38:05.012 --> 01:38:07.147 align:start size:84% position:16%
or that dip because
you're chasing a dream

01:38:07.147 --> 01:38:08.682
and you're doing something hard.

01:38:08.682 --> 01:38:13.687 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I think I've been able
to embrace the ups and downs

01:38:15.055 --> 01:38:17.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the creative dream chasing
by way of understanding

01:38:17.658 --> 01:38:19.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
the parallel in
the athletic world.

01:38:19.660 --> 01:38:23.297 align:start size:84% position:16%
And likewise in
running, I rarely win.

01:38:23.297 --> 01:38:26.467
Like I've won races,
but to not win a race

01:38:26.467 --> 01:38:29.003 align:start size:94% position:6%
doesn't mean that I
haven't done really well.

01:38:29.003 --> 01:38:31.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there's a lot of
nos you get in Hollywood

01:38:31.472 --> 01:38:32.773 align:start size:88% position:13%
like when you're
trying to make a movie.

01:38:32.773 --> 01:38:35.576
And I think just being
able to weather the nos

01:38:35.576 --> 01:38:40.381
like weather all of that
and take it not as a loss.

01:38:41.649 --> 01:38:46.186 align:start size:84% position:16%
And to take criticism
like blood work.

01:38:46.186 --> 01:38:47.655
I think that's another thing

01:38:47.655 --> 01:38:49.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
that was an epiphany.
- Oh, tell me about that.

01:38:49.089 --> 01:38:52.760 align:start size:97% position:3%
I like this.
- Okay, so blood work, right?

01:38:52.760 --> 01:38:55.763
If you don't get
your blood tested,

01:38:55.763 --> 01:38:57.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
it doesn't mean
that you don't have

01:38:57.131 --> 01:38:58.832
low iron or high cholesterol,

01:38:58.832 --> 01:39:01.568
it just means you haven't
found out about it, right?

01:39:01.568 --> 01:39:03.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Right.
- It's fact, it's blood work.

01:39:03.537 --> 01:39:07.107 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think similarly with
like creative criticism

01:39:07.107 --> 01:39:10.744
or feedback we might get
on a script or whatever,

01:39:10.744 --> 01:39:12.346
if I don't ask for the feedback,

01:39:12.346 --> 01:39:14.248 align:start size:84% position:16%
it doesn't mean that
I did a good job.

01:39:14.248 --> 01:39:15.849
It just means that
I'm refusing to know

01:39:15.849 --> 01:39:17.685
what I could've done better.

01:39:17.685 --> 01:39:19.954
And looking at it
like blood work

01:39:19.954 --> 01:39:23.791 align:start size:88% position:13%
has felt a lot less
emotional and offensive,

01:39:23.791 --> 01:39:26.660 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's just like, this
is just feedback.

01:39:26.660 --> 01:39:28.195
It's just my blood work.

01:39:28.195 --> 01:39:31.966
And I want to have it
so that I can improve.

01:39:31.966 --> 01:39:35.536 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that helped me a
lot to flip that switch.

01:39:35.536 --> 01:39:38.505 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Right, like resiliency
plus the hustle

01:39:38.505 --> 01:39:41.642 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the dedication of
getting up and showing up

01:39:41.642 --> 01:39:43.477
on days when you
don't feel like it

01:39:43.477 --> 01:39:45.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
are things you
learn as an athlete

01:39:45.479 --> 01:39:47.281
that of course are life lessons.

01:39:49.917 --> 01:39:54.288 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm reminded of an anecdote
that I recently heard.

01:39:55.656 --> 01:39:59.927 align:start size:84% position:16%
Timothy Olyphant tell,
he was on my friend

01:39:59.927 --> 01:40:01.729
John Moffitt's Podcast.

01:40:01.729 --> 01:40:04.164
And a lot of people
don't know this,

01:40:04.164 --> 01:40:06.300
but like Timothy Olyphant
was an amazing swimmer.

01:40:06.300 --> 01:40:09.403
Like at USC, he was very good.

01:40:09.403 --> 01:40:10.637
He didn't make the Olympics

01:40:10.637 --> 01:40:13.774 align:start size:84% position:16%
but he was a very
outstanding athlete.

01:40:13.774 --> 01:40:16.610
And when he decided he
wanted to be an actor

01:40:16.610 --> 01:40:20.547
and came into the awareness
that like it's one in 100,000

01:40:20.547 --> 01:40:22.049
or 10,000 that make it,

01:40:22.049 --> 01:40:24.485
you know the odds are
stacked against you,

01:40:24.485 --> 01:40:26.520
while he was waiting tables,

01:40:26.520 --> 01:40:29.289 align:start position:0%
he would ask these other people
who are trying to be actors

01:40:29.289 --> 01:40:30.758
like, well, what are you doing?

01:40:30.758 --> 01:40:34.595
And they'd be like, well,
I'm going to auditions

01:40:34.595 --> 01:40:37.664
or my uncle knows a guy
who might know a guy

01:40:37.664 --> 01:40:39.400
who's gonna set me up here.

01:40:39.400 --> 01:40:41.969
Meanwhile, Timothy
is like taking class.

01:40:41.969 --> 01:40:44.304
Like he approached
it like an athlete.

01:40:44.304 --> 01:40:46.306 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like what are the steps
that I need to take

01:40:46.306 --> 01:40:49.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
to put myself in the best
position for success?

01:40:49.143 --> 01:40:50.911
And he's like, when he
would hear those stories,

01:40:50.911 --> 01:40:53.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
he's like, well,
that's one less person

01:40:53.013 --> 01:40:55.949
I have to worry about
competing against.

01:40:55.949 --> 01:40:59.153 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it's interesting
how, my point being that

01:41:00.554 --> 01:41:03.657 align:start size:97% position:3%
there is something to be
said for the athlete mindset

01:41:03.657 --> 01:41:06.593
approach to creative pursuits.

01:41:06.593 --> 01:41:07.928
When we look at
creative pursuits

01:41:07.928 --> 01:41:12.900 align:start size:97% position:3%
is like these unpredictable
realms in which

01:41:14.201 --> 01:41:16.303 align:start size:84% position:16%
not always the
best people succeed

01:41:16.303 --> 01:41:18.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
and some people who are
less deserving are rewarded.

01:41:18.906 --> 01:41:20.808
Like it's very much
not a meritocracy

01:41:20.808 --> 01:41:24.344 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there are things that
you could do to put yourself

01:41:24.344 --> 01:41:27.748
in a position to succeed
when that moment arises.

01:41:27.748 --> 01:41:32.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes, and to build a
stronger foundation, right?

01:41:32.386 --> 01:41:35.789
'Cause the people for whom
they have random connections

01:41:35.789 --> 01:41:39.493
or this and that, it's easy
to feel jealous of people

01:41:39.493 --> 01:41:42.930
who you perceive to be
having a shortcut or two,

01:41:42.930 --> 01:41:47.935
but if you can always lean
back on a strong backbone

01:41:49.336 --> 01:41:52.372 align:start size:97% position:3%
of whatever that means,
whether it's his preparations

01:41:52.372 --> 01:41:55.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
of his classes or
whatever that means to you

01:41:55.375 --> 01:41:58.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
like that backbone,
it'll be there.

01:42:00.581 --> 01:42:04.184
But I do think that every
artist has to face a moment

01:42:04.184 --> 01:42:07.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
probably every athlete too,
where you ask yourself,

01:42:07.921 --> 01:42:10.791
are you interested in this
goal or are you committed?

01:42:10.791 --> 01:42:15.796
And that's a big
difference, right?

01:42:16.964 --> 01:42:20.467
Where if you are merely
interested in a goal,

01:42:20.467 --> 01:42:23.403 align:start size:97% position:3%
you're going to find the
reasons why it wasn't possible

01:42:23.403 --> 01:42:26.940
for you or you're gonna
back out when it hurts

01:42:26.940 --> 01:42:29.510
or when you get one no.

01:42:29.510 --> 01:42:32.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
And if you're committed, then
you will keep showing up.

01:42:34.948 --> 01:42:37.618
So that's how I feel about that.

01:42:39.086 --> 01:42:41.421
- How does it work
working with Jeremy?

01:42:42.823 --> 01:42:46.793 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's great, so I think
Jeremy and I, similar to you

01:42:47.895 --> 01:42:51.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
are thriving when we're
doing these things

01:42:51.331 --> 01:42:52.666
we're so passionate about.

01:42:52.666 --> 01:42:55.035
So we're not misaligned in that.

01:42:55.035 --> 01:42:58.138
We're very much aligned
that we enjoy our work

01:42:58.138 --> 01:42:59.940
and we enjoy working together.

01:42:59.940 --> 01:43:01.542
It's a different
way to love someone

01:43:01.542 --> 01:43:03.577
when you have a common goal,

01:43:03.577 --> 01:43:07.080 align:start size:88% position:13%
because it makes us both
family and teammates,

01:43:07.080 --> 01:43:08.549
which are different, right?

01:43:08.549 --> 01:43:13.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like family, you love no
matter what and it's family.

01:43:14.087 --> 01:43:16.089
Friends, I think you choose

01:43:16.089 --> 01:43:17.791
and that's a different
kind of relationship.

01:43:17.791 --> 01:43:19.126
You don't rely on
them for anything,

01:43:19.126 --> 01:43:21.461
and teammates, you have a goal.

01:43:22.896 --> 01:43:26.900
It's very, very fun and it's
a really enriching experience

01:43:26.900 --> 01:43:28.969
to share a goal with someone.

01:43:28.969 --> 01:43:32.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's hard to because you
kind of need them to show up

01:43:32.906 --> 01:43:35.209
and you're leaning on
them as a teammate.

01:43:36.276 --> 01:43:38.111
We have different strengths.

01:43:38.111 --> 01:43:41.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
So we've learned more
about like what each of us

01:43:41.448 --> 01:43:43.217
brings to the table.

01:43:43.217 --> 01:43:46.753
And I would not be where I am

01:43:46.753 --> 01:43:48.422
even as an athlete without him,

01:43:48.422 --> 01:43:52.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause it's so much a blurred
line of getting it done,

01:43:52.259 --> 01:43:55.429
whatever it takes,
whatever it means.

01:43:55.429 --> 01:43:57.764 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But there's no way
in making a movie

01:43:57.764 --> 01:43:59.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you're not
gonna have conflicts

01:43:59.833 --> 01:44:03.370
over what you think is the
right way to do something.

01:44:03.370 --> 01:44:05.272
So how do you navigate that

01:44:05.272 --> 01:44:07.941 align:start size:84% position:16%
and keep your
relationship intact?

01:44:07.941 --> 01:44:10.577
- Yeah, that's a good question.

01:44:10.577 --> 01:44:14.381
I mean, we, you know,

01:44:14.381 --> 01:44:16.516
I think you'd go back
to the rule of thirds.

01:44:16.516 --> 01:44:20.487
Like being in love is
also a dream of sorts.

01:44:20.487 --> 01:44:22.489
And so it doesn't
always feel good.

01:44:22.489 --> 01:44:25.492 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like it does feel crappy
a third of the time,

01:44:25.492 --> 01:44:28.262
but that's because I'm
not married to myself.

01:44:30.764 --> 01:44:32.899
So, it's not always
gonna be, you know,

01:44:32.899 --> 01:44:37.004 align:start size:84% position:16%
and when we are
working on something,

01:44:38.005 --> 01:44:42.376
I think we instinctually know

01:44:42.376 --> 01:44:44.478
when to like give or take,

01:44:44.478 --> 01:44:46.213
like we respect
each other enough

01:44:46.213 --> 01:44:48.215 align:start size:84% position:16%
to respect the
other person's idea

01:44:48.215 --> 01:44:49.916
even if it wasn't our own.

01:44:51.952 --> 01:44:56.957
And we have tried
to put into place

01:44:59.092 --> 01:45:02.796 align:start size:84% position:16%
vocabulary that allows
us to move forward

01:45:02.796 --> 01:45:04.865
during conflict more gracefully.

01:45:04.865 --> 01:45:08.935 align:start size:97% position:3%
So for example, we will
use this word, recalibrate,

01:45:10.370 --> 01:45:13.674
which basically just means
if something needs to shift,

01:45:13.674 --> 01:45:18.679
like I need to try to respond

01:45:19.546 --> 01:45:21.515
to his ideas in this way,

01:45:21.515 --> 01:45:23.050
even though my instinct,

01:45:23.050 --> 01:45:25.352
my whole life has been to
respond in this other way,

01:45:26.720 --> 01:45:28.655 align:start size:94% position:6%
if I can have the maturity
to just recalibrate

01:45:28.655 --> 01:45:30.590
and never go back
to that old way,

01:45:30.590 --> 01:45:35.595
and he can allow me to be a
mature person who can grow,

01:45:36.797 --> 01:45:38.231
that's a kind thing
for him and for me.

01:45:38.231 --> 01:45:41.201 align:start position:0%
So we use this word recalibrate
when we need to like grow

01:45:41.201 --> 01:45:42.736
and grow instantly.

01:45:42.736 --> 01:45:46.073
And I think this hearkens back
to that word bravery of like,

01:45:46.073 --> 01:45:48.008
you can choose to be
a bravery right now

01:45:48.008 --> 01:45:50.110
and then your bravery for
the rest of your life.

01:45:50.110 --> 01:45:51.912
And I think with recalibrating,

01:45:53.313 --> 01:45:55.882 align:start size:94% position:6%
that can be anything from,
I'm no longer a person

01:45:55.882 --> 01:46:00.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
who apologizes every two
seconds, which I used to be.

01:46:00.320 --> 01:46:01.955
I'm just not that anymore.

01:46:01.955 --> 01:46:05.392
So I think that has helped
us to grow when we need to

01:46:05.392 --> 01:46:07.694
to level up and-

01:46:09.096 --> 01:46:11.965
- You have a crazy amount
of emotional maturity.

01:46:11.965 --> 01:46:13.200
How old are you?

01:46:13.200 --> 01:46:14.634
- I'm 30.

01:46:14.634 --> 01:46:16.403
On my 30th.
- I can't...

01:46:16.403 --> 01:46:19.306
I mean, don't even get me
started on what I was doing

01:46:19.306 --> 01:46:22.309 align:start size:88% position:13%
when I was 30, but like
that is impressive.

01:46:22.309 --> 01:46:25.679
- Well, it's not, and
it's not always a...

01:46:26.847 --> 01:46:31.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
That takes a lot
of, we don't always,

01:46:33.019 --> 01:46:35.188 align:start size:84% position:16%
we are normal people
who fight, right?

01:46:36.556 --> 01:46:40.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
But we really love each
other more than any of these.

01:46:42.262 --> 01:46:44.664
Oh, you know what? I think is
also just that this whole film

01:46:44.664 --> 01:46:46.133
world and the running world,

01:46:46.133 --> 01:46:49.102 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're sandboxes and we're
playing in them, right?

01:46:49.102 --> 01:46:52.005
Like I went through this
depression with Jeremy

01:46:52.005 --> 01:46:54.875
and that was the
worst thing, right?

01:46:54.875 --> 01:46:59.546
So I think also we have
the overall stability

01:47:00.747 --> 01:47:03.350
and safety to know
that we're playing.

01:47:03.350 --> 01:47:05.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's always
important too, right?

01:47:05.352 --> 01:47:06.853
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:47:06.853 --> 01:47:09.322
Well you just, I mean, you
haven't lived in Los Angeles

01:47:09.322 --> 01:47:10.524
that long, right?

01:47:10.524 --> 01:47:11.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
So when you were
making these movies,

01:47:11.625 --> 01:47:13.093
you weren't even living here.

01:47:13.093 --> 01:47:17.097 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you were kind of outside
of the sort of downside

01:47:17.097 --> 01:47:22.102
or the toxic aspects of,
quote unquote, Hollywood.

01:47:23.537 --> 01:47:26.239
- Yes, and even now we live in
the Valley just like you do.

01:47:26.239 --> 01:47:28.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
(both laughing) And I
think that's a little bit-

01:47:28.975 --> 01:47:30.811
- But not so out of it
that you're not gonna run

01:47:30.811 --> 01:47:32.746 align:start size:84% position:16%
into Jay Duplass
when you go running.

01:47:34.214 --> 01:47:37.984
But that, the feeling that that
could happen that's amazing.

01:47:37.984 --> 01:47:40.220
- Well, that's a crazy
thing about living in LA

01:47:40.220 --> 01:47:42.489
or in New York too,
but like, you know,

01:47:42.489 --> 01:47:44.257
you could just run into anybody.

01:47:44.257 --> 01:47:45.492
- You can.

01:47:45.492 --> 01:47:46.726
- And these people
that you look up to

01:47:46.726 --> 01:47:48.094
who are doing the thing
that you aspire to do

01:47:48.094 --> 01:47:50.597
are like around at
the grocery store.

01:47:50.597 --> 01:47:52.699
- Yes, and before that,

01:47:52.699 --> 01:47:55.569
I felt like I needed to
create these interactions

01:47:55.569 --> 01:47:57.871 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that's why
podcasts are important.

01:47:57.871 --> 01:48:00.240
Because when I lived in
these mountain towns,

01:48:00.240 --> 01:48:05.011
I craved those, like,
those interactions.

01:48:05.011 --> 01:48:07.948
I craved like the spontaneity,

01:48:07.948 --> 01:48:10.050
I craved mentorship basically

01:48:10.050 --> 01:48:12.152
and I was living in a tiny town.

01:48:12.152 --> 01:48:15.388 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so I think I
created those moments

01:48:15.388 --> 01:48:17.524
by listening to podcasts.

01:48:17.524 --> 01:48:20.026 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I still love
podcasts and audio books

01:48:20.026 --> 01:48:22.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it has a different
role in my life

01:48:22.429 --> 01:48:24.297
than it did when I never thought

01:48:24.297 --> 01:48:26.233 align:start size:97% position:3%
there would be a possibility
to run into someone

01:48:26.233 --> 01:48:31.104
which was mostly the case when
I was in Eugene, for example.

01:48:31.104 --> 01:48:33.073
- Right, well, the mentors
are just different there.

01:48:33.073 --> 01:48:37.344
You're gonna run into running
mentors and athletic mentors.

01:48:37.344 --> 01:48:39.446
- That's true. That's very true.

01:48:39.446 --> 01:48:43.817
But, yeah, I don't know.

01:48:43.817 --> 01:48:46.353 align:start size:88% position:13%
- We have to talk
about "Olympic Dreams".

01:48:46.353 --> 01:48:48.722
Can we talk about this?
- we can talk about it.

01:48:48.722 --> 01:48:52.425
- I look at "Tracktown"
and as insane as that was

01:48:52.425 --> 01:48:54.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
to compete in the Olympics
and make a feature film

01:48:54.928 --> 01:48:56.763 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the same year,
it's like all right,

01:48:56.763 --> 01:48:59.032
well, how can we up the
degree of difficulty?

01:48:59.032 --> 01:49:03.136 align:start position:0%
I know we're gonna make a
movie, a narrative feature film

01:49:03.136 --> 01:49:06.840
in the middle of the Olympic
games in the Olympic village.

01:49:06.840 --> 01:49:08.575 align:start size:75% position:25%
- It was so fun.
- So crazy.

01:49:08.575 --> 01:49:10.010
- It was so fun, so-

01:49:10.010 --> 01:49:11.912
- It looked fun. It looked hard.

01:49:11.912 --> 01:49:13.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, it didn't
come off looking hard

01:49:13.647 --> 01:49:16.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
but as I'm watching
it, I'm like,

01:49:16.149 --> 01:49:17.751
how the fuck did they do this?

01:49:17.751 --> 01:49:19.252
This is unbelievable.

01:49:19.252 --> 01:49:23.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, we made friends with
the team USA chiropractor

01:49:23.089 --> 01:49:25.859 align:start size:84% position:16%
for their bobsled
team because Jeremy,

01:49:25.859 --> 01:49:27.794
so we had a one-man band.

01:49:27.794 --> 01:49:29.362 align:start size:81% position:19%
It was Jeremy,
Nick Kroll, and I,

01:49:29.362 --> 01:49:31.765
and Jeremy was the
cinematographer,

01:49:31.765 --> 01:49:33.233
the director, and the sound guy,

01:49:33.233 --> 01:49:35.735
because these permissions
to go into the village

01:49:35.735 --> 01:49:38.772
are so difficult and rare.

01:49:38.772 --> 01:49:40.874
So Jeremy was in the
most pain, I would say,

01:49:40.874 --> 01:49:42.375
by carrying all the equipment.

01:49:43.643 --> 01:49:47.380
But it was really felt

01:49:47.380 --> 01:49:50.584
like the feeling you'd
wanna have on any set,

01:49:50.584 --> 01:49:52.152
which is intimate,

01:49:52.152 --> 01:49:56.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
like friends trying
to pull something off,

01:49:56.222 --> 01:49:58.058
like it was a pure adventure.

01:49:59.225 --> 01:50:03.096
And I felt so happy there.

01:50:03.096 --> 01:50:04.731
I felt so happy.

01:50:04.731 --> 01:50:08.301 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I was healing
actually a broken SI

01:50:08.301 --> 01:50:13.073
and I was in pain, but I was
more happy than I was in pain.

01:50:13.073 --> 01:50:14.541
I remember that.

01:50:14.541 --> 01:50:18.912
I remember that it was a little
painful, but I was so joyful

01:50:19.779 --> 01:50:23.249
that it like overtook that.

01:50:23.249 --> 01:50:25.919
- It's like this perfect
melding of the two worlds

01:50:25.919 --> 01:50:29.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you care most about,
like coming together

01:50:29.089 --> 01:50:30.857
for this special experience.

01:50:30.857 --> 01:50:35.762
I mean, we should say, the
movie is about an Olympian

01:50:35.762 --> 01:50:39.933
that you play and her experience

01:50:39.933 --> 01:50:41.968
of competing at the Olympics

01:50:41.968 --> 01:50:46.773 align:start size:94% position:6%
and dealing with the
emotions of the aftermath

01:50:46.773 --> 01:50:48.675
and this encounter that she has

01:50:48.675 --> 01:50:51.411 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a dentist
played by Nick Kroll,

01:50:51.411 --> 01:50:53.313
inspired by this real dentist

01:50:53.313 --> 01:50:57.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you had become friendly
with when you were in Rio

01:50:58.718 --> 01:51:00.754
and what ensues with
their relationship

01:51:00.754 --> 01:51:02.789
and how their pain meets

01:51:02.789 --> 01:51:05.959 align:start size:97% position:3%
and how they both kind of
come away from that experience

01:51:05.959 --> 01:51:07.394
a little bit more complete.

01:51:07.394 --> 01:51:12.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
And to me it felt there's
definitely overtones

01:51:16.970 --> 01:51:20.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
of lost in translation
in the movie.

01:51:20.073 --> 01:51:22.442 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like there's a similar
sensibility to it,

01:51:22.442 --> 01:51:24.844
but there's also a
little light dusting

01:51:24.844 --> 01:51:27.714
of curb your enthusiasm
at moments with Nick,

01:51:27.714 --> 01:51:29.883 align:start position:0%
you know like when he's
interacting with these athletes,

01:51:29.883 --> 01:51:31.785
like that is hilarious.

01:51:31.785 --> 01:51:34.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
- And it was cool
because these athletes,

01:51:34.120 --> 01:51:37.824 align:start position:0%
so these were all real athletes
competing in that Olympics.

01:51:37.824 --> 01:51:41.428
And I would find them
in the dining hall

01:51:41.428 --> 01:51:42.662
or in the game room.

01:51:42.662 --> 01:51:44.197
And if they didn't
look super stressed

01:51:44.197 --> 01:51:46.633 align:start position:0%
like they weren't about to
compete, if they looked relaxed,

01:51:46.633 --> 01:51:48.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would ask if they
wanted to come to a scene

01:51:48.768 --> 01:51:51.237
for this movie, we were
making with Nick Kroll,

01:51:51.237 --> 01:51:53.807
and what was cool is that
athletes were very used

01:51:53.807 --> 01:51:56.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
to being on camera
and being interviewed,

01:51:56.643 --> 01:52:00.947
but that's a certain
posture that we have

01:52:00.947 --> 01:52:03.116
and there's certain
ways that we talk.

01:52:03.116 --> 01:52:06.352 align:start size:84% position:16%
And because of the
small crew that we had

01:52:06.352 --> 01:52:09.456
and because Nick is
so good with people,

01:52:10.824 --> 01:52:14.194 align:start position:0%
he was able to draw out, I
think a real human side of them

01:52:14.194 --> 01:52:18.431
and a different side than the
typical coverage might offer.

01:52:19.332 --> 01:52:21.768
They were just being themselves.

01:52:21.768 --> 01:52:23.903
- And it's all pretty
much improv, right?

01:52:23.903 --> 01:52:26.539 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like when you see these
athletes in the dentist chair

01:52:26.539 --> 01:52:31.344
and Nick Kroll, he starts
talking about his ex fiance

01:52:31.344 --> 01:52:32.979
and how he's having a hard time.

01:52:32.979 --> 01:52:34.481 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the look on the
faces of the athletes.

01:52:34.481 --> 01:52:37.183
They're like, what am I
supposed to do with this?

01:52:37.183 --> 01:52:38.752
- It was so great.

01:52:38.752 --> 01:52:42.355 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that was partly because
Nick knew his character

01:52:42.355 --> 01:52:43.890
really, really well.

01:52:43.890 --> 01:52:47.026
And that's like, when
we do improv in movies,

01:52:47.026 --> 01:52:50.163
the most important thing
is that we know who we are,

01:52:50.163 --> 01:52:52.465
so that if we're
in any scenario,

01:52:52.465 --> 01:52:54.968 align:start size:84% position:16%
we know what our
characters would do.

01:52:56.402 --> 01:53:01.307 align:start position:0%
So that was, you know, speaking
back to how do you compare

01:53:02.175 --> 01:53:03.243
the athletics to the arts?

01:53:04.144 --> 01:53:05.545
Just preparation, right?

01:53:05.545 --> 01:53:10.250 align:start size:88% position:13%
We were prepared so that
whatever that circus

01:53:10.250 --> 01:53:13.920 align:start size:88% position:13%
of an environment
brought, we could do it.

01:53:13.920 --> 01:53:15.889 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And you have a
ticking clock, right?

01:53:15.889 --> 01:53:19.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like this has to be completed
by the time the games

01:53:19.292 --> 01:53:21.227 align:start size:88% position:13%
are done because you're
not gonna be able

01:53:21.227 --> 01:53:23.863
to recreate that environment.

01:53:23.863 --> 01:53:25.999
- For sure, and you don't
wanna get in anyone's way.

01:53:25.999 --> 01:53:30.103
Like the last thing you wanna
do is like alter someone's

01:53:30.103 --> 01:53:32.839
Olympic experience, the
real Olympic experience.

01:53:32.839 --> 01:53:35.108
So we were really
careful about that.

01:53:35.108 --> 01:53:38.077
- But you're in, I mean,
just so people understand,

01:53:38.077 --> 01:53:40.413
like you're in the
Olympic dining hall

01:53:40.413 --> 01:53:42.348
and it's filled with
athletes who are eating

01:53:42.348 --> 01:53:43.817 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you're just
navigating around them.

01:53:43.817 --> 01:53:47.554 align:start position:0%
So there's this documentary
kind of vibe to the whole thing.

01:53:47.554 --> 01:53:50.557
- Yeah, yeah, and it
was such a privilege

01:53:50.557 --> 01:53:52.992
because the best part
about the Olympics

01:53:52.992 --> 01:53:56.462
to me as an athlete was
the village experience.

01:53:56.462 --> 01:53:58.965
Like the village is pure magic.

01:54:00.333 --> 01:54:05.038 align:start position:0%
It is everybody with their
guard down, 24-hour dining hall,

01:54:06.406 --> 01:54:08.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
everyone's in these costumes,
I mean, their uniforms.

01:54:08.474 --> 01:54:10.577
You have to wear your
country's uniform.

01:54:10.577 --> 01:54:14.514
It's like a Wes Anderson
movie, but real, real life.

01:54:16.883 --> 01:54:20.153
And to be able to like give
people a peek into that world

01:54:20.153 --> 01:54:24.390 align:start size:88% position:13%
and do it in a way that
wasn't documentary,

01:54:24.390 --> 01:54:26.960 align:start size:84% position:16%
that like had a
narrative through line

01:54:26.960 --> 01:54:30.763
that could have happened
was such a like thrill

01:54:30.763 --> 01:54:34.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
and such a joy and the
thing I was most nervous for

01:54:34.934 --> 01:54:36.436
same with "Tracktown",

01:54:36.436 --> 01:54:40.106
was when my Olympic
peers saw the movie.

01:54:40.106 --> 01:54:44.477
So Gus Kenworthy was
at the premier and-

01:54:44.477 --> 01:54:46.746
- He was in the movie.
- And was in the movie,

01:54:46.746 --> 01:54:48.815
but I was really nervous
for what he would think,

01:54:48.815 --> 01:54:50.717
just like I was nervous
for what my running peers

01:54:50.717 --> 01:54:54.120
would think of "Tracktown"
because even though I want

01:54:54.120 --> 01:54:58.524
this movie to speak to a
broader cinematic audience,

01:54:58.524 --> 01:55:01.060 align:start position:0%
I want it to speak to the world
that I'm representing first

01:55:01.060 --> 01:55:03.997
to make sure, you know, I
want it to be authentic.

01:55:03.997 --> 01:55:05.999
And it was such a...

01:55:05.999 --> 01:55:10.570
I felt really grateful that
people who are Olympians

01:55:10.570 --> 01:55:14.173
were like, yeah, I would
show my kids that one day

01:55:14.173 --> 01:55:16.676
just to show them what it was
like to be at the Olympics.

01:55:16.676 --> 01:55:19.279
You know, it felt it
was what it was like.

01:55:19.279 --> 01:55:20.980 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, there's an
emotional honesty to it.

01:55:20.980 --> 01:55:24.050
I mean, you see, you get
glimpses of the high highs

01:55:24.050 --> 01:55:27.487
and what we, as average people
would project that experience

01:55:27.487 --> 01:55:30.990
might be about, but
you shine a spot.

01:55:30.990 --> 01:55:33.726 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's really about those
moments in between.

01:55:33.726 --> 01:55:35.995
Like, what does it feel
like after you're done

01:55:35.995 --> 01:55:37.797 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're just wandering
around the village

01:55:37.797 --> 01:55:39.699
trying to figure out
what's gonna come next?

01:55:39.699 --> 01:55:41.701
Or sitting alone
in the cafeteria

01:55:41.701 --> 01:55:43.069
or going back to your dorm room,

01:55:43.069 --> 01:55:45.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
and not really jelling
with your roommate

01:55:45.438 --> 01:55:48.341
and just feeling lonely
and then feeling guilty

01:55:48.341 --> 01:55:51.611 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you feel lonely because
you're basically living

01:55:51.611 --> 01:55:53.379
your dream at the same time.

01:55:53.379 --> 01:55:55.815 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, and that
Olympians just don't,

01:55:55.815 --> 01:55:59.018
we don't prepare for
the moment afterwards,

01:55:59.018 --> 01:56:01.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
because if we did, we
probably wouldn't get there

01:56:01.187 --> 01:56:04.958 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the first place and we
probably wouldn't do very well

01:56:04.958 --> 01:56:07.360 align:start size:88% position:13%
if we were thinking
about anything but that.

01:56:08.294 --> 01:56:10.530
- You can't plan beyond August.

01:56:10.530 --> 01:56:12.699
- Yeah, you know that it's funny

01:56:12.699 --> 01:56:17.704
because I've noticed that that
is true of other worlds too.

01:56:19.238 --> 01:56:24.243
And I mentioned this,
but I met Jimmy Kimmel

01:56:25.411 --> 01:56:26.946 align:start size:84% position:16%
on our press tour
for "Olympic Dreams"

01:56:26.946 --> 01:56:28.948
when Nick was on his show.

01:56:28.948 --> 01:56:33.386 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we talked about this,
like post Olympic depression,

01:56:33.386 --> 01:56:34.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
like just the
subject of the movie.

01:56:34.821 --> 01:56:36.556 align:start size:88% position:13%
And he was like, that's
exactly how I felt

01:56:36.556 --> 01:56:38.758
after I hosted the "Oscars."

01:56:38.758 --> 01:56:41.694 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I was like, oh, this
resonates with people

01:56:41.694 --> 01:56:44.630
who have had singular goals.

01:56:44.630 --> 01:56:48.134
And there's this dip
after where it's normal

01:56:48.134 --> 01:56:52.038
but if you're not prepared for
it, which most of us aren't,

01:56:52.038 --> 01:56:55.508
it's such a bizarre feeling.

01:56:55.508 --> 01:56:57.143
- You see it with music,

01:56:57.143 --> 01:56:58.745 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, you hear about
it with musicians,

01:56:58.745 --> 01:57:01.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
they play a big show,
but then, they're on tour

01:57:01.147 --> 01:57:02.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
and it's a different
city every night

01:57:02.749 --> 01:57:05.752 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're just alone in
their hotel room afterwards.

01:57:05.752 --> 01:57:10.156
So the weird juxtaposition of
like this extreme experience

01:57:10.156 --> 01:57:15.128 align:start size:88% position:13%
followed by this kind
of a weird liminal space

01:57:15.128 --> 01:57:16.562
where you're just by yourself.

01:57:16.562 --> 01:57:18.064
- Right, right.

01:57:19.932 --> 01:57:23.436 align:start size:84% position:16%
I am sure there's
that in every world.

01:57:23.436 --> 01:57:26.472
- Well, the only thing
though in the movie was,

01:57:26.472 --> 01:57:28.274
I was like, where
are her teammates?

01:57:28.274 --> 01:57:30.910
Like, she would be,
like, realistically,

01:57:30.910 --> 01:57:33.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
she would have a team and
there would be a coach there

01:57:33.279 --> 01:57:34.881 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there would be
people around for her.

01:57:34.881 --> 01:57:39.185
So you created a more extreme
version of that experience.

01:57:39.185 --> 01:57:41.154 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You know not
everybody has a coach.

01:57:41.154 --> 01:57:43.489
Like there are country heads,

01:57:43.489 --> 01:57:45.958 align:start size:84% position:16%
but most people can't
have their coach

01:57:45.958 --> 01:57:47.193
in the village with them.

01:57:47.193 --> 01:57:48.461 align:start size:88% position:13%
There are so few people-
- But there's a team,

01:57:48.461 --> 01:57:50.229
like a team Olympic coach.

01:57:50.229 --> 01:57:51.731
- There's a team Olympic coach.

01:57:51.731 --> 01:57:55.234
So Penelope was supposed to
be from, like any country

01:58:01.207 --> 01:58:03.276
that was sort of the IOCs hope,

01:58:03.276 --> 01:58:06.512
and so she was not really
from a designated place.

01:58:06.512 --> 01:58:11.350
And that was just
a universal athlete

01:58:11.350 --> 01:58:14.821
is what they called her,
which I was down with.

01:58:14.821 --> 01:58:17.290
But yeah, certainly, I mean,

01:58:17.290 --> 01:58:19.992
but at the same time when
I think about my experience

01:58:19.992 --> 01:58:22.895
in Rio, I was alone
most of the time.

01:58:22.895 --> 01:58:25.731 align:start size:97% position:3%
Part of that was by choice.
- That makes me so sad.

01:58:25.731 --> 01:58:27.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Why though?
Because I had so...

01:58:30.837 --> 01:58:33.539
I was all eyeballs. I was
like taking it all in.

01:58:33.539 --> 01:58:34.907 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I was sitting with
a different person

01:58:34.907 --> 01:58:36.442
at the dining hall every night.

01:58:36.442 --> 01:58:39.879
Like I remember one day I
sat and ate with this girl

01:58:39.879 --> 01:58:41.681
and I did not know she was

01:58:41.681 --> 01:58:44.283 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause this was
before she competed,

01:58:44.283 --> 01:58:47.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
but it was Katie
Ledecky, the swimmer,

01:58:47.587 --> 01:58:50.590
and we had just like a
normal lunch together.

01:58:50.590 --> 01:58:53.626
And it was just very
early in the games

01:58:53.626 --> 01:58:55.461 align:start size:88% position:13%
and those opportunities
came about

01:58:55.461 --> 01:58:58.364
because I chose to go to
the dining hall alone.

01:58:58.364 --> 01:59:00.233
So I did that by choice

01:59:00.233 --> 01:59:02.702
so that I could have a
different experience.

01:59:02.702 --> 01:59:05.138 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Except when the dentist
asks you to hang out,

01:59:05.138 --> 01:59:07.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
you said, no or
you didn't show up.

01:59:08.441 --> 01:59:11.644
- I knew what happened at
the village pool, okay?

01:59:11.644 --> 01:59:15.181 align:start size:88% position:13%
No savory things.
- All right, I hear you.

01:59:16.582 --> 01:59:21.320 align:start size:97% position:3%
By the way, like give Gus
Kenworthy a movie immediately

01:59:21.320 --> 01:59:23.623 align:start size:97% position:3%
like that guy's a movie star.
- Oh, he's amazing.

01:59:23.623 --> 01:59:26.392 align:start size:88% position:13%
- He was so naturalistic
in his acting.

01:59:26.392 --> 01:59:28.060 align:start size:72% position:28%
He crushed it.
- He acts.

01:59:28.060 --> 01:59:31.297
He like me has these two,

01:59:31.297 --> 01:59:34.300
he is multihyphenate
and multi-interested.

01:59:34.300 --> 01:59:36.769
And that was cool
for me actually

01:59:36.769 --> 01:59:39.839
because I hadn't met so many
people like me and I told him,

01:59:39.839 --> 01:59:43.776
I was like, this is cool
to know that we both...

01:59:43.776 --> 01:59:46.445 align:start size:84% position:16%
It helps me to
know that you exist

01:59:46.445 --> 01:59:51.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause it felt like I was
meeting somebody like-minded.

01:59:52.618 --> 01:59:54.587
- Like, yeah, has he
done other movies?

01:59:54.587 --> 01:59:58.291 align:start size:84% position:16%
- He was in "American
Horror Stories".

01:59:58.291 --> 01:59:59.825
- [Host] Oh, he was?
Oh, I didn't know that.

01:59:59.825 --> 02:00:01.961 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So I think he will
do more of that.

02:00:01.961 --> 02:00:04.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That's cool. That's cool.
- Yeah, really cool.

02:00:04.297 --> 02:00:06.432
- You talked about
this with Dave Chang

02:00:06.432 --> 02:00:10.203
but I thought it was worth
spending a few moments on.

02:00:10.203 --> 02:00:14.073
The moment where
after you've raced

02:00:15.241 --> 02:00:18.744
and we don't really
know how you did,

02:00:18.744 --> 02:00:21.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
we know that you're
not thrilled,

02:00:21.013 --> 02:00:24.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
but there's a
creative choice to not

02:00:24.717 --> 02:00:26.485
basically show the scoreboard

02:00:26.485 --> 02:00:28.688
and what place you came
in or anything like that.

02:00:28.688 --> 02:00:31.357 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's just a quiet
moment of reflection

02:00:31.357 --> 02:00:33.793 align:start size:88% position:13%
where you're processing
what's going on.

02:00:33.793 --> 02:00:37.597 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then you witness
a medal ceremony

02:00:37.597 --> 02:00:39.966
and you see these women
getting their medals

02:00:39.966 --> 02:00:42.902 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're having an
emotional moment with that

02:00:42.902 --> 02:00:44.170
as you're watching.

02:00:44.170 --> 02:00:46.939
And then the aftermath
of that where the grips,

02:00:46.939 --> 02:00:49.175
you know, the gaffers
or whoever come out

02:00:49.175 --> 02:00:52.478
and remove the podium,
and you're like, oh yeah,

02:00:52.478 --> 02:00:54.380
this is just a big show.

02:00:54.380 --> 02:00:55.915
And it's like onto the next.

02:00:55.915 --> 02:00:57.750
It's like the circus is in town

02:00:57.750 --> 02:01:00.019
for a very brief moment of time,

02:01:00.019 --> 02:01:02.088 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we're just
putting on a big show.

02:01:02.088 --> 02:01:05.057 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I love that you
noticed that because,

02:01:05.057 --> 02:01:06.659
and this is where I
think you and Jeremy

02:01:06.659 --> 02:01:10.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
will get along very well,
because that was his,

02:01:13.132 --> 02:01:18.137 align:start size:88% position:13%
that was his like
wholly Jeremy's vision.

02:01:19.005 --> 02:01:21.674
And I think he likes seeing

02:01:21.674 --> 02:01:24.443
like the mechanics of things
like that that we don't see,

02:01:24.443 --> 02:01:26.712
like on NBC, you see
the medal ceremony,

02:01:26.712 --> 02:01:28.981
you do not see it
being cleaned up.

02:01:28.981 --> 02:01:31.884
And that was part of the
fun of "Olympic Dreams,"

02:01:31.884 --> 02:01:34.553
was the showing that
the texture underneath

02:01:34.553 --> 02:01:37.223 align:start size:84% position:16%
and like what happens
the moment after.

02:01:38.457 --> 02:01:41.160
And I really love that
you pointed that out

02:01:41.160 --> 02:01:46.065
because it's when you're
at these events in person,

02:01:46.065 --> 02:01:51.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
as I'm sure anyone who is
involved in other like events,

02:01:53.072 --> 02:01:57.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's so much more that goes
into it than just the event

02:01:57.243 --> 02:01:58.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the medal ceremony.
- Right, it's like when you're

02:01:58.678 --> 02:02:01.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
at Disneyland and
there's a whole like city

02:02:01.280 --> 02:02:03.449
underneath your feet of people

02:02:03.449 --> 02:02:05.418 align:start size:84% position:16%
that make that whole
place function.

02:02:05.418 --> 02:02:08.487 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, it's so strange,
and it's not sad,

02:02:11.924 --> 02:02:16.128
I think it's just
interesting, right?

02:02:16.128 --> 02:02:20.132 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I think it
gives you perspective,

02:02:20.132 --> 02:02:23.035 align:start size:88% position:13%
like oh, I thought this
was like one thing,

02:02:23.035 --> 02:02:25.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I realized like maybe
this isn't everything

02:02:25.905 --> 02:02:28.474 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought it was, you
could put a sad spin on it,

02:02:28.474 --> 02:02:31.210
I don't think it's
necessarily sad,

02:02:31.210 --> 02:02:35.247
but maybe I see it as
more transactional.

02:02:35.247 --> 02:02:37.416 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like this is a
commercial enterprise.

02:02:39.118 --> 02:02:42.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Interesting, yeah.
- Which maybe is cynical.

02:02:43.456 --> 02:02:44.657
- Yeah, or maybe-

02:02:44.657 --> 02:02:47.226 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Because I love
the "Olympic Dream."

02:02:47.226 --> 02:02:49.362 align:start size:81% position:19%
I'm not casting
aspersions on that

02:02:49.362 --> 02:02:53.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the production of
the Olympics is different

02:02:53.833 --> 02:02:57.570
from the aspiration of
the "Olympic Dream."

02:02:57.570 --> 02:03:00.005
- Yeah, but at the same
time, like when you think

02:03:00.005 --> 02:03:01.006 align:start size:88% position:13%
about all the volunteers
and all the people

02:03:01.006 --> 02:03:03.509
that make those moments happen,

02:03:03.509 --> 02:03:07.079 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's also cool to think
about that it is so much more

02:03:07.079 --> 02:03:09.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
than just the athletes
and the spectators.

02:03:09.115 --> 02:03:13.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like there is this
whole, like, you know,

02:03:15.254 --> 02:03:18.090
there are those people that
make it actually happen.

02:03:19.492 --> 02:03:21.627
And we actually got to
interact with quite a few

02:03:21.627 --> 02:03:23.629 align:start size:84% position:16%
of those people
because when we were,

02:03:24.964 --> 02:03:28.601 align:start size:97% position:3%
when we shot in, for
example, the Olympic stadium

02:03:28.601 --> 02:03:31.337 align:start size:84% position:16%
with the flame, with
nobody in there,

02:03:31.337 --> 02:03:36.208
we had people helping us
like open those gates,

02:03:36.208 --> 02:03:38.377 align:start size:84% position:16%
who knew we were
there to do the movie.

02:03:38.377 --> 02:03:41.514
And just to know there
are all these people

02:03:41.514 --> 02:03:44.817 align:start size:97% position:3%
who have been at so many
games, but on the other side,

02:03:44.817 --> 02:03:48.587
they're the ones making it
happen, it's kind of cool.

02:03:48.587 --> 02:03:50.623
Like, it's kind of
like when you think

02:03:50.623 --> 02:03:53.192 align:start size:84% position:16%
about the chaperones
at the dance,

02:03:53.192 --> 02:03:54.660 align:start size:81% position:19%
you're like, yeah,
we need those.

02:03:54.660 --> 02:03:59.098
We need the people to help
make prom prom and it's cool.

02:03:59.098 --> 02:04:01.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Will give some
attention to those people

02:04:01.133 --> 02:04:04.570 align:start size:81% position:19%
in recounting your
own experience

02:04:04.570 --> 02:04:06.572 align:start size:84% position:16%
just before
competing in the book

02:04:06.572 --> 02:04:09.275
where you talk about like
the timing chip person

02:04:09.275 --> 02:04:11.610
and they're the people that
are telling you go here,

02:04:11.610 --> 02:04:13.979
and some people are very
detached from that role

02:04:13.979 --> 02:04:16.682
and some people are
like very nurturing.

02:04:16.682 --> 02:04:19.452 align:start position:0%
But just recognizing that there
are all these human beings

02:04:19.452 --> 02:04:23.456 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are like the
grease to the engine.

02:04:23.456 --> 02:04:24.757
- And they are not your parents

02:04:24.757 --> 02:04:27.460 align:start size:81% position:19%
and they're not
your coach, right?

02:04:27.460 --> 02:04:29.328
And that's where you're like,
why aren't the coaches there?

02:04:29.328 --> 02:04:32.498 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like, actually quite a
bit of the Olympic experiences

02:04:32.498 --> 02:04:34.700 align:start size:84% position:16%
is you taking it in-
- Away from that.

02:04:34.700 --> 02:04:36.001
- Yeah, you're away from that.

02:04:36.001 --> 02:04:38.137
And that can really scare people

02:04:38.137 --> 02:04:42.241 align:start size:97% position:3%
because so I think that
where people go wrong with...

02:04:42.241 --> 02:04:44.844 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think the right
approach to the Olympics

02:04:44.844 --> 02:04:47.680 align:start size:84% position:16%
is to not see it
like any other event,

02:04:47.680 --> 02:04:50.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
to see it as something
exceptional and different.

02:04:50.416 --> 02:04:53.385
When we try to sterilize
it and create it to be

02:04:53.385 --> 02:04:57.623
just like every other race
or every other experience,

02:04:57.623 --> 02:05:00.593
it will never be that
and it is so different.

02:05:00.593 --> 02:05:04.063 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the people that I
knew who tried to recreate

02:05:04.063 --> 02:05:06.799
their comfort zone
didn't do as well

02:05:06.799 --> 02:05:09.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
because it's not your
comfort zone, it's different.

02:05:10.236 --> 02:05:11.504
And maybe that's like a lesson

02:05:11.504 --> 02:05:14.139
for anything we've
never done before.

02:05:14.139 --> 02:05:19.078
Like it will be different and
control what you can, control,

02:05:19.078 --> 02:05:21.747
but also embrace that
some of these things

02:05:21.747 --> 02:05:24.683
are just gonna be different.

02:05:24.683 --> 02:05:26.619 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I mean, I
understand the impulse

02:05:26.619 --> 02:05:29.088
to try to reduce it down to size

02:05:29.088 --> 02:05:30.589
so that it becomes manageable

02:05:30.589 --> 02:05:33.392
because when your whole life
is oriented around that moment,

02:05:33.392 --> 02:05:34.860
it's very overwhelming, right?

02:05:34.860 --> 02:05:36.629
So to say like I'm
just gonna make this

02:05:36.629 --> 02:05:38.063
like every other race

02:05:38.063 --> 02:05:40.633 align:start size:94% position:6%
but then you're less
resilient when you arrive.

02:05:40.633 --> 02:05:42.635
And you're told to
do all these things

02:05:42.635 --> 02:05:44.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you're kind of instructed
to go here and there,

02:05:44.837 --> 02:05:46.372
and it's all very different,

02:05:46.372 --> 02:05:49.008
that's gonna throw you
off and destabilize you

02:05:49.008 --> 02:05:51.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
and perhaps undermine
the performance

02:05:51.010 --> 02:05:54.013
that you're trying to
create for yourself.

02:05:54.013 --> 02:05:56.282
- Yeah. Yeah.

02:05:56.282 --> 02:05:59.518
If you could be in the
village, what was your,

02:05:59.518 --> 02:06:02.054
where would you like to be
or what was like the most-

02:06:02.054 --> 02:06:04.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I mean, I'm
obsessed with the Olympics

02:06:04.290 --> 02:06:06.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I've never
attended an Olympics.

02:06:06.191 --> 02:06:08.961
So I don't know what that
experience would be like.

02:06:08.961 --> 02:06:10.462
I have lots of friends
who've been Olympians

02:06:10.462 --> 02:06:12.698
but it was cool in the movie,

02:06:12.698 --> 02:06:15.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was like, oh, that's
what it looks like inside.

02:06:15.134 --> 02:06:17.903 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, it just
looked like really cold

02:06:17.903 --> 02:06:21.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
and like a bunch of
tents, (laughs), you know,

02:06:22.207 --> 02:06:23.609
it pulled the veneer off

02:06:23.609 --> 02:06:27.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
like the glamour projection
that I had, I think.

02:06:27.780 --> 02:06:29.481
You know, like there was
a bunch of young people-

02:06:29.481 --> 02:06:30.916
- Yeah, bunch of kids

02:06:30.916 --> 02:06:32.418
walking around.
- Hanging around.

02:06:32.418 --> 02:06:35.487
And in the book, you describe
it as summer camp basically.

02:06:35.487 --> 02:06:36.555
- Yeah, that's how it felt.

02:06:36.555 --> 02:06:38.591
I mean, Rio was very romantic.

02:06:38.591 --> 02:06:43.429 align:start size:88% position:13%
Rio was not cold. Rio
was very, very romantic.

02:06:43.429 --> 02:06:45.130
I guess the winter
Olympics are romantic

02:06:45.130 --> 02:06:46.632
in their own way though.

02:06:46.632 --> 02:06:50.836
Everyone's huddled and just
need to keep each other warm.

02:06:52.237 --> 02:06:55.441 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I know, I think I'd rather
be at the summer games.

02:06:55.441 --> 02:06:59.178
But it did look like being
in the cafeteria was cool

02:06:59.178 --> 02:07:01.213
'cause that's where you're
gonna have those collisions

02:07:01.213 --> 02:07:03.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
with all those interesting
people and the people watching

02:07:03.949 --> 02:07:07.853 align:start position:0%
and you recreate with Gus
something that you talked about

02:07:07.853 --> 02:07:10.723
in the book, this idea
of like trying to imagine

02:07:10.723 --> 02:07:13.659
what sport all of
these athletes play

02:07:13.659 --> 02:07:15.227
and like what role they have,

02:07:15.227 --> 02:07:17.262
and it's pretty cool.

02:07:17.262 --> 02:07:18.497
- It was so fun.

02:07:18.497 --> 02:07:20.699
The dining hall has the
best people watching.

02:07:20.699 --> 02:07:22.001
It's just simply the best.

02:07:23.869 --> 02:07:26.405
- It should be mentioned
that you can't just go

02:07:26.405 --> 02:07:28.841 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the Olympics and
make a movie like you,

02:07:29.908 --> 02:07:31.143
correct me if I'm wrong,

02:07:31.143 --> 02:07:33.979 align:start size:81% position:19%
but you got the
support of the IOC

02:07:33.979 --> 02:07:35.714
because they had
seen "Tracktown"

02:07:35.714 --> 02:07:38.517 align:start size:84% position:16%
or whoever the
person in charge had

02:07:38.517 --> 02:07:41.520
and granted you is
unprecedented access.

02:07:41.520 --> 02:07:45.324
Like you basically could
go wherever you wanted.

02:07:45.324 --> 02:07:47.493
- Yes, this was very rare.

02:07:48.861 --> 02:07:51.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
The president of the
Olympics was really taken

02:07:51.664 --> 02:07:56.635
by "Tracktown" which he
saw randomly on a flight,

02:07:56.635 --> 02:07:58.137
which is the same,

